WEBVTT

00:00.100 --> 00:03.390
Welcome to our special council meeting on June 16th,

00:04.939 --> 00:08.240
2026. I'm going to ask everybody in the

00:08.740 --> 00:11.719
chamber to please take their conversations outside. The meeting is starting.

00:15.660 --> 00:19.560
The purpose of this meeting is to discuss 100 Victoria Street encampment,

00:20.060 --> 00:23.320
and I'll now call on Tim Brubaker, regional clerk, to conduct the roll call.

00:23.560 --> 00:27.100
Thank you, Chair Redmond. Present. Uh, we have regrets from Councilor Craig.

00:27.600 --> 00:31.100
Councilor Erb. Councillor Foxton. Present.

00:31.600 --> 00:34.700
Councillor Gowing. Present. Councillor Harris.

00:35.200 --> 00:38.900
Present. Councillor Huinink. Present. Councillor James. Present.

00:39.400 --> 00:42.960
Councillor Leggett. Present. Councillor McCabe. Present. Councillor Nowak.

00:43.460 --> 00:47.440
Present. Councillor Rodriguez. Present. Councillor Salonen. Present. Councillor Shantz

00:47.940 --> 00:51.910
is behind the wall, but I can hear her. Councillor Verbanovic is running late.

00:52.200 --> 00:54.500
Uh, and Councillor Wolff. Thank you, Chair.

00:57.170 --> 01:00.440
I'd like to acknowledge that the land on which we gather today is the traditional

01:00.940 --> 01:04.280
territory of the Haudenosaunee, the Anishnawbe, and the Chonnonton or

01:04.780 --> 01:08.500
Neutral peoples. In doing this, I want to reflect on what it means to be

01:09.000 --> 01:12.940
doing important community work while we are engaging— which we are engaging in collectively

01:13.440 --> 01:17.020
today, and also in my own individual relationship to this land.

01:17.520 --> 01:21.340
As a settler, one of the most important aspects of my individual journey towards

01:21.840 --> 01:25.690
truth and reconciliation has been to listen. I am continually grateful

01:26.190 --> 01:29.719
to a community who shares their stories and their concerns with me.

01:30.290 --> 01:33.810
As well as Chair of the Region, I know I carry a responsibility to ensure

01:34.310 --> 01:38.109
that it is not just myself on an individual level listening and acting,

01:38.609 --> 01:43.090
but that as a government we are listening and creating action with community.

01:43.110 --> 01:46.410
I'm offering this acknowledgement as one of the many actions I personally,

01:46.630 --> 01:50.320
and the Region largely, is taking on our journey towards reconciliation.

01:51.334 --> 01:55.350
We acknowledge the enduring presence of the Indigenous peoples with whom we share this land

01:55.850 --> 01:58.853
today, their achievements and their contributions to our community.

02:00.010 --> 02:02.950
Colleagues, are there any declarations of conflict of interest?

02:04.990 --> 02:07.449
Seeing none, we will now proceed to delegations.

02:08.911 --> 02:12.831
I would like to remind all delegates of some of the guidelines for participation.

02:14.052 --> 02:17.057
If you are attending in person, please proceed to the podium.

02:17.350 --> 02:20.930
When you are called upon to speak. I will note we now

02:21.430 --> 02:24.670
have a new accessible podium that has the ability to be lowered and raised,

02:25.170 --> 02:28.510
and there's a button to the right of the podium as you face the chair.

02:29.469 --> 02:33.190
If you're attending virtually, please turn on your microphone and camera when

02:33.690 --> 02:37.430
called upon. You will have 5 minutes to speak if you registered before

02:37.930 --> 02:40.740
the deadline and 3 minutes if you registered afterwards.

02:41.510 --> 02:45.050
When the light on the podium turns yellow, that means you have 1 minute

02:45.550 --> 02:49.102
remaining. When the light turns red, that means your time is up

02:49.183 --> 02:52.714
and you should sum up. I may give you a reminder,

02:52.859 --> 02:55.106
uh, of your remaining, remaining time.

02:55.989 --> 02:59.521
Additionally, some of the general reminders: if you conduct

03:00.021 --> 03:03.678
yourself in any of the following manners, it will result in immediate removal

03:04.112 --> 03:08.044
from the meeting: interrupting council members, staff, or other

03:08.544 --> 03:11.560
delegations; derogatory statements about councillor,

03:11.672 --> 03:15.690
staff, or others; turning on your microphone or camera

03:16.190 --> 03:20.169
at inappropriate times, and hate speech or defamatory comments.

03:20.530 --> 03:24.010
Please also note that election campaigning or endorsement of

03:24.510 --> 03:27.570
any elected candidates during your delegation presentation is

03:28.070 --> 03:31.610
not permitted. One last reminder for delegations:

03:32.110 --> 03:36.130
please do not take offense if following your presentation there are no questions

03:36.630 --> 03:40.150
from Council. This is Council's time to really listen and reflect upon the

03:40.650 --> 03:43.310
opinions and the ideas shared with us from our community members.

03:43.660 --> 03:46.820
And a quick reminder to members of the audience,

03:47.320 --> 03:50.839
you are asked to maintain order. This means you

03:51.339 --> 03:54.769
may not address council without the permission of the chair,

03:55.160 --> 03:59.279
interrupt anyone, disrupt the meeting with unreasonable noise or vocal

03:59.779 --> 04:03.220
expressions such as applause, booing, or cheering, or displaying posters

04:03.720 --> 04:07.120
or signs. Our first delegation today is

04:07.620 --> 04:11.010
Eleanor Maxwell from Kitchener. Eleanor, I believe you're with us in person.

04:11.440 --> 04:15.110
Uh, please come to the podium. You have 5 minutes and the floor

04:15.610 --> 04:15.940
is yours.

04:22.410 --> 04:26.310
Uh, am I on mic? Yes, I am, and it's the right height.

04:26.810 --> 04:30.250
Uh, good morning everyone. Um, thank you for

04:30.750 --> 04:34.390
giving me this opportunity to speak. Um, my name is Eleanor

04:34.890 --> 04:38.570
Maxwell, a retired teacher, has lived in Kitchener for over 40 years,

04:39.070 --> 04:41.510
and And it's been a great community to raise a family.

04:44.320 --> 04:47.830
During my research for this presentation, I learned a lot.

04:48.520 --> 04:52.520
I was planning to provide a lot of information about, or concentrate

04:53.020 --> 04:56.640
on solutions, but modular housing with wraparound

04:57.140 --> 05:01.220
services, including mental health, drug addiction treatment, employment skills

05:01.720 --> 05:05.650
training, job search, et cetera. Is a

05:06.150 --> 05:09.330
known model, and I'm sure there are others.

05:09.830 --> 05:13.569
The key being that it's a transition housing

05:14.069 --> 05:17.710
to permanent housing. When CBC did a

05:18.210 --> 05:20.830
series on this topic a few years ago,

05:21.310 --> 05:24.000
a business in Seattle was featured.

05:25.050 --> 05:29.630
Modular homes arrived

05:30.130 --> 05:33.370
in a flat pack, sort of like IKEA, I think, and were

05:33.870 --> 05:38.500
erected in 30 minutes. And I did see the video. It is real.

05:38.600 --> 05:42.160
Support services were required for each project, meant to

05:42.660 --> 05:46.199
be a transition to permanent housing. I'm sure a Canadian

05:46.699 --> 05:50.000
company, and it maybe exists right now, using Canadian lumber,

05:50.500 --> 05:53.740
could match any such undertaking.

05:54.540 --> 05:58.020
An article on CBC News a few days ago covered the great

05:58.520 --> 06:02.920
work that Supportive Housing of Waterloo, under Director Brian

06:03.420 --> 06:08.311
Paul, is doing. 10 homes for, for older adults

06:10.077 --> 06:14.363
is a terrific start. Therefore, awareness of solutions

06:14.411 --> 06:18.135
is not really the problem. I guess my focus is implementing

06:18.247 --> 06:22.228
those solutions is the hard part, especially with such a deeply

06:22.646 --> 06:26.434
complicated societal issue. Which brings me

06:26.934 --> 06:30.977
to the current situation of the encampment at 100 Victoria Street in

06:31.073 --> 06:35.307
Kitchener. In existence since late 2021.

06:36.366 --> 06:40.792
I agree with a judge who ruled recently that occupants of the encampment

06:41.292 --> 06:44.640
cannot be evicted unless there is somewhere for them to go.

06:45.907 --> 06:51.151
They are the most vulnerable people in

06:51.199 --> 06:54.872
our society. Not helping them reflects very

06:54.984 --> 07:00.440
negatively— excuse me— on

07:00.940 --> 07:04.549
our society and at an enormous economic cost.

07:04.979 --> 07:09.160
The conflict interest with the vital transportation

07:09.660 --> 07:13.119
hub to be built on that site is an example of that

07:13.619 --> 07:17.720
cost. How did we get to this impasse? And I've

07:18.220 --> 07:22.950
written it in bold: lack of action, which has exacerbated the problem.

07:23.580 --> 07:26.940
The statistics tell the story. 200 people were on

07:27.440 --> 07:28.400
the waitlist for supportive housing in 2018.

07:30.569 --> 07:34.420
Now, 650 people are on the waitlist

07:34.920 --> 07:38.509
today. The time for talk is past.

07:38.579 --> 07:42.100
We must act with speed and determination.

07:43.079 --> 07:46.260
I know it's a complicated issue finding land costs,

07:46.760 --> 07:50.070
etc. The City of Kitchener received a check, if I may be so bold,

07:50.860 --> 07:54.420
for over $8 million last week for exceeding housing

07:54.920 --> 07:57.777
construction targets. That is fantastic.

07:58.659 --> 08:01.998
Perhaps some of that money could be used along

08:02.030 --> 08:05.289
with contributions from other municipalities in

08:05.321 --> 08:08.515
the region. Fundraising donations are another

08:08.595 --> 08:13.025
option. The point is action is needed now for both humanitarian

08:13.525 --> 08:19.687
and economic reasons. Let me briefly address

08:20.187 --> 08:24.968
those who might say that the unhoused are responsible for their direct—

08:25.064 --> 08:28.964
sorry, their dire situation. Not everyone

08:29.061 --> 08:32.303
has the same opportunity in life. Think about those

08:32.367 --> 08:36.829
who have mental health issues or were prescribed OxyContin

08:37.567 --> 08:40.070
by a physician and, uh,

08:41.675 --> 08:45.431
for severe pain and developed an addiction or grew

08:45.931 --> 08:50.133
up in a home where alcohol was easily available or

08:50.647 --> 08:54.579
lost their employment or homes for any number of reasons. If a person drops

08:55.079 --> 08:57.910
in the street with a heart attack, We dial 911,

08:58.410 --> 09:02.270
administer CPR. We don't ask whether they ate a lot of

09:02.770 --> 09:06.250
junk food or have had an unhealthy lifestyle. We render

09:06.750 --> 09:10.390
assistance without thinking. We act. To further explore

09:10.890 --> 09:14.210
that metaphor, we must meet this urgent moment and call it

09:14.710 --> 09:17.930
an emergency. We must dial 911 and bring

09:18.430 --> 09:22.050
all our resources to bear on ending homelessness in our community.

09:22.700 --> 09:26.780
Thank you. Thank you very much. And we do have a question,

09:27.280 --> 09:32.420
Eleanor. Um, Councilor Wolff,

09:32.920 --> 09:35.620
um, through you, Madam Chair, um,

09:36.120 --> 09:40.240
thank you so much for your compassionate, um, delegation and

09:40.740 --> 09:44.040
trying to come up with solutions. And right now I'm going to take the opportunity,

09:44.320 --> 09:47.760
um, to thank the many delegates who

09:48.260 --> 09:52.063
have come out today to speak because your voices matter.

09:53.667 --> 09:57.275
You are here to defend the Charter of Rights of some of the most vulnerable

09:58.077 --> 10:01.188
members of our community, and that takes courage,

10:01.862 --> 10:05.598
compassion, and conviction. I want

10:06.098 --> 10:09.816
to recognize that and thank you for that. We are here to talk

10:10.316 --> 10:13.954
about homelessness, but more importantly, we are here to talk about

10:14.210 --> 10:17.980
people. People who do not have the safety

10:18.480 --> 10:22.120
of stability or dignities of permanent homes,

10:22.620 --> 10:26.480
people whose lives are already shaped by hardship and systemic

10:26.980 --> 10:31.220
barriers. And I'm saying this now so I don't have to say it

10:31.720 --> 10:34.960
after each delegation, because I know some of council has,

10:35.460 --> 10:39.220
has time things. But I want to say how much I appreciate, and I

10:39.720 --> 10:43.520
think council does, that it takes courage to come here as a delegate.

10:44.020 --> 10:46.980
It takes a time commitment. It takes compassion. As I said,

10:47.670 --> 10:51.609
and, uh, I'd like to thank you for doing that. Thank you

10:52.109 --> 10:56.050
very much. So, Eleanor, I would just like to, um, echo Councillor Wolff's,

10:56.550 --> 11:00.429
um, comments, um, that you have come with heart and compassion as well as

11:00.929 --> 11:03.470
solutions. So thank you very much. Those are the only questions I have, so you

11:03.970 --> 11:07.330
can resume your seat. Thank you. Uh, the next,

11:07.830 --> 11:11.290
uh, delegation is Acer Bonaparte. Uh, Acer is from Kitchener.

11:11.790 --> 11:15.577
I believe Acer is in person, and they

11:16.077 --> 11:19.328
can make their way to the podium. You have the floor and

11:19.828 --> 11:20.274
you have 5 minutes.

11:25.900 --> 11:28.385
Hello, Council.

11:29.940 --> 11:33.210
I come to you on this Tuesday morning last minute, having rescheduled

11:33.710 --> 11:36.881
my day around this. I understand the optics of 'we are listening to

11:37.381 --> 11:39.606
you and giving you your own meeting' sound good, but you also could have made

11:40.106 --> 11:42.956
it so the people who are being paid to be here, staff and Council,

11:43.534 --> 11:46.620
could have the early morning and switched the agendas so the community wouldn't have

11:47.120 --> 11:51.400
to scramble, but regardless, I'm here.

11:51.900 --> 11:55.120
We didn't have to get to this point. We really didn't. Homelessness is a symptom

11:55.620 --> 11:59.199
of society's decay and inadequacies. It is caused by failings at every level of government

11:59.699 --> 12:03.060
and our current social order. It disproportionately affects those who are marginalized and living

12:03.560 --> 12:07.120
with intersectional identities, with racialized folks, Indigenous folks, disabled folks,

12:07.620 --> 12:11.320
and queer folks overrepresented. While the blame isn't all on your

12:11.820 --> 12:15.510
hand, you've had every opportunity to act, and instead you've

12:16.010 --> 12:19.350
dragged your heels. And boy, have you consulted. Countless experts have

12:19.850 --> 12:23.140
given you their input on how to alleviate the problems we face relating homelessness,

12:23.250 --> 12:26.350
and a lot of them will tell you that we need to build community with

12:26.850 --> 12:30.610
unhoused folks. Studies have shown the importance of long-term stable community in improving

12:31.110 --> 12:34.510
outcomes from homelessness. 100 Vic has been

12:35.010 --> 12:38.330
this long-term stable community, and yet you're still intent on tearing down 100 Vic,

12:38.830 --> 12:42.620
evicting everyone who lives there. And I hope no unhoused people have

12:43.120 --> 12:45.460
any trauma over being evicted.

12:47.890 --> 12:50.480
Hmm. But you're intent on this path, and I know I can't sway you at

12:50.980 --> 12:54.759
this point. So let's talk about it. You've asked two judges now. In both cases,

12:55.259 --> 12:57.820
your ask was simple: tell us what to do. You seem to be asking that

12:58.320 --> 13:01.440
a lot and never actually following through, but again, whatever.

13:01.940 --> 13:04.840
Now two judges have told you exactly what to do, and you're not doing it.

13:04.900 --> 13:08.330
You seem to be waiting for the province to themselves themselves to step in.

13:08.830 --> 13:12.380
That's what they're threatening to do. And you know what?

13:12.810 --> 13:14.950
I don't feel like you've been very honest with me, but I'm going to be

13:15.450 --> 13:19.010
very honest with you. You can't wash your hands of this. Today you will hear

13:19.510 --> 13:22.600
from community members who you have failed. I hope you listen well.

13:23.270 --> 13:25.770
I hope you consider what horrors bringing the hand of the province into this would

13:26.270 --> 13:30.150
cause. I hope you consider the implications of appealing a ruling like

13:30.650 --> 13:33.980
the decision Gibson passed down. But my faith in you all is pretty low,

13:34.480 --> 13:37.660
and coming from me, that's saying something. So do what you can while you can.

13:40.059 --> 13:43.460
Thank you, Acer. I see no questions, but thank you very much

13:43.960 --> 13:48.020
for delegating. Next delegation is Theo Zhu,

13:48.520 --> 13:52.380
um, from Waterloo. And Theo, you are here in person, so as you make your

13:52.880 --> 13:56.000
way to the podium, the floor is yours, and you have 5 minutes for your

13:56.500 --> 13:56.550
delegation.

14:02.780 --> 14:05.070
Good morning, everyone.

14:05.570 --> 14:09.010
I'm still disappointed to be here. I'm still

14:09.510 --> 14:12.810
frustrated to be here. When I began paying attention to

14:13.310 --> 14:16.900
the problem of council trying to evict 100 Vic

14:17.270 --> 14:20.750
I didn't grasp the depth of

14:21.250 --> 14:24.390
the apathy of the people

14:24.890 --> 14:29.589
who had the power to actually affect permanent

14:30.089 --> 14:34.150
change. I don't have

14:34.650 --> 14:38.990
more statistics for you today. I don't have

14:39.490 --> 14:43.070
more appeals to your

14:43.570 --> 14:47.490
sense of goodness, your sense of

14:47.990 --> 14:50.510
duty to your community members.

14:51.279 --> 14:54.540
All I really have to say is,

14:55.069 --> 14:59.170
if you don't instate safe

14:59.670 --> 15:03.350
tenting, if you choose to try to appeal

15:03.850 --> 15:07.870
this decision, if you choose to let Premier

15:08.370 --> 15:12.230
Ford walk into this community and decide

15:12.730 --> 15:15.750
what is best for us when he doesn't know anything,

15:17.440 --> 15:20.639
I hope every time that

15:21.139 --> 15:23.469
you wake up in the middle of the night and can't go back to sleep,

15:24.059 --> 15:27.400
you think of the people on the street who don't sleep

15:27.900 --> 15:31.340
for days on end because they don't feel

15:31.840 --> 15:35.230
safe or there are rats scurrying around in their tents.

15:35.640 --> 15:39.040
I hope every time you walk

15:39.540 --> 15:43.340
outside and you think, oh, it's a little rainy, it's a little cold,

15:43.490 --> 15:47.430
It's a little warm. You think of the people who are exposed to

15:47.930 --> 15:51.609
those conditions 24/7 and have nowhere else

15:52.109 --> 15:55.730
to go, or in your words,

15:56.230 --> 16:00.350
there are cooling centers, there are warming centers available, just not

16:00.850 --> 16:04.490
at all times of the day, and especially not at night when you would think

16:04.990 --> 16:08.560
that that would be most crucial. I hope that

16:09.440 --> 16:13.760
When you look at your children and your grandchildren, you know

16:14.260 --> 16:18.380
that you have sentenced them to live in a worse world

16:18.880 --> 16:21.559
and that you had a direct hand in this.

16:23.540 --> 16:26.860
That's all.

16:27.360 --> 16:31.020
Thank you, Theo. I have no questions. The next delegation is Joanna Mullen,

16:31.080 --> 16:34.760
Waterloo Regional Legal Services. And Joanna, you're here in person. Uh,

16:35.260 --> 16:37.140
you have the Florin, you have 5 minutes.

16:42.359 --> 16:45.560
Thank you for the opportunity to speak

16:46.060 --> 16:49.220
to you today about the need for a safe tenting protocol in the

16:49.720 --> 16:53.120
Region of Waterloo. I am a lawyer with

16:53.620 --> 16:57.300
Waterloo Region Community Legal Services, and I acted as co-counsel

16:57.800 --> 17:01.260
in the 2025-2026 litigation

17:01.760 --> 17:05.170
between the region and the residents 100 Vic.

17:05.670 --> 17:09.429
My comments today are made without prejudice in terms of

17:09.929 --> 17:13.750
any future litigation. As you know,

17:14.250 --> 17:17.890
Justice Michael Gibson determined that the Region's request to close the encampment

17:18.390 --> 17:22.270
at 100 Vic violated the Charter rights of the encampment residents.

17:23.110 --> 17:26.730
However, the court provided an option by which the

17:27.230 --> 17:30.450
Region may proceed with using that site as a laydown

17:30.950 --> 17:34.980
site for the Kitchener Central Transit Hub. The court's remedy for

17:35.480 --> 17:39.859
the region to comply with the Charter is to create a safe tenting protocol or

17:40.359 --> 17:44.520
provide another piece of land similarly situated to 100 Vic where

17:45.020 --> 17:48.919
folks can tent. Before discussing safe

17:49.419 --> 17:52.610
tenting protocols further, I would like to go back to the numbers.

17:53.760 --> 17:57.020
As of the 2024 point-in-time

17:57.520 --> 18:00.840
count, which was the last comprehensive count performed here,

18:01.280 --> 18:05.060
There were 2,371

18:05.560 --> 18:09.399
people experiencing homelessness in Waterloo Region.

18:09.899 --> 18:13.660
This number is the most accurate picture we have of those currently

18:14.160 --> 18:17.300
unhoused in the region, although it is still likely an

18:17.800 --> 18:21.900
undercount. The HIFAS numbers only capture

18:22.400 --> 18:25.520
those who have interacted with the housing stability system within

18:26.020 --> 18:29.653
the past 90 days. And as of the time of the court case,

18:29.942 --> 18:33.041
there were only 356 shelter beds.

18:33.956 --> 18:37.327
This means that the current shelter system can only accommodate

18:37.827 --> 18:40.667
15% of those unhoused on any given night.

18:42.465 --> 18:46.286
Waterloo Region Community Legal Services wants everyone

18:46.880 --> 18:50.348
currently unhoused in the region to have a roof over

18:50.848 --> 18:54.590
their head and a room of their own. But that simply isn't

18:55.090 --> 18:58.549
possible right now. This is particularly in light of

18:59.049 --> 19:02.430
the region's decision in April 2026 to pause

19:02.930 --> 19:06.460
affordable housing construction projects such as the one on Moorgate Crescent.

19:07.090 --> 19:11.450
While there are currently 11,421 applications

19:11.950 --> 19:16.270
for affordable housing, the wait time for subsidized housing in the region

19:16.770 --> 19:20.620
is 8 to 10 years. None of this changes

19:21.120 --> 19:24.640
without greater investment at all government levels into creating

19:25.140 --> 19:27.660
affordable housing for the people of Waterloo Region.

19:28.340 --> 19:32.120
Moreover, none of this changes by ignoring

19:32.620 --> 19:36.240
or overpowering the rights of encampment residents.

19:36.820 --> 19:41.200
This is not an either-or issue for the Region.

19:41.700 --> 19:45.180
There have been negative comments made in the media which make it

19:45.680 --> 19:48.720
seem like the Region is prevented full stop from proceeding with the KCTH.

19:50.240 --> 19:54.570
Because of the court's ruling. That is simply untrue.

19:56.100 --> 20:00.140
The Region may proceed so long as there is somewhere else

20:00.640 --> 20:04.439
for people to go. Encampment residents should not,

20:04.939 --> 20:08.700
cannot be expected to float in the air, as one

20:09.200 --> 20:12.700
encampment resident told me. Until the number of beds

20:13.200 --> 20:16.729
meet the needs of the Region's unhoused community members, there must

20:16.793 --> 20:20.840
be an alternative place of last resort for people to stay

20:21.032 --> 20:23.377
without being ticketed, fined,

20:23.698 --> 20:27.584
arrested, or displaced when there is nothing else

20:27.873 --> 20:28.387
available.

20:30.924 --> 20:34.441
Waterloo Region Community Legal Services is asking the Region to

20:34.941 --> 20:38.472
follow the guidance in the Plan to End Chronic Homelessness and commit to

20:38.972 --> 20:42.872
maintaining supported spaces while working to achieve functional zero

20:43.032 --> 20:46.540
homelessness. The federal housing advocate has

20:47.040 --> 20:51.179
provided guidance on zones for encampments which provide security

20:51.679 --> 20:54.949
of tenure, habitability, protection from the elements,

20:55.440 --> 20:59.109
privacy, available services, and cultural adequacy.

20:59.779 --> 21:03.200
The region should develop the safe tenting protocol in collaboration

21:03.700 --> 21:07.720
with the great minds at the Co-Creators Table and the lived

21:08.220 --> 21:10.380
expertise prototyping cohort.

21:11.490 --> 21:14.829
The City of London's protocol is a good starting

21:15.329 --> 21:19.710
basis as it provides for tenting in groups of 6, service provisions,

21:20.210 --> 21:23.750
and wraparound supports. It has also clearly indicated no-go

21:24.250 --> 21:27.910
zones. It provides access to safe and clean drinking water,

21:28.410 --> 21:32.290
hygiene and sanitation facilities, waste management systems,

21:32.790 --> 21:36.950
social supports and services, including harm reduction services,

21:37.450 --> 21:39.550
food safety, rodent and pest prevention.

21:40.140 --> 21:43.360
At main service depots, residents can access

21:43.860 --> 21:47.579
food, water, washrooms, and garbages.

21:48.079 --> 21:51.160
I'm almost done. Can I just finish? Okay, if you can sum up, thank you.

21:51.660 --> 21:55.000
Yeah, I'm very close. It remains to be said that the

21:55.500 --> 21:58.840
region commenced the litigation last spring to seek guidance from the court as

21:59.340 --> 22:03.080
to whether the bylaw complied with the Charter. After a year of expensive

22:03.580 --> 22:07.870
and time-intensive litigation, you now have your answer. The bylaw

22:08.370 --> 22:11.570
is not Charter compliant. The court has told the

22:12.070 --> 22:15.650
Region what is necessary to comply with the Charter and use the site

22:16.150 --> 22:19.710
for the much-needed KCTH. We urge you to follow

22:20.210 --> 22:23.730
the guidance from the court and work with the Petch collaborators to

22:24.230 --> 22:28.070
create the safe hunting protocol this community desperately needs.

22:28.570 --> 22:32.710
Thank you. So Joanna, from your comments, I'm taking you are representing the

22:33.210 --> 22:36.970
Waterloo Regional Community Legal Services in these comments. Yes, I'm here

22:37.470 --> 22:41.149
on behalf of our organization. Thank you. We do have a question. Councillor James.

22:41.649 --> 22:44.930
Thank you. Through you, Chair, Joanna, thank you for coming in today. Um,

22:45.430 --> 22:48.910
I'll have formal comments later, Chair, but my question for you,

22:49.410 --> 22:53.570
Joanna, is around the ideal place of last

22:54.070 --> 22:57.290
resort. Could you, um, what does that look like

22:57.790 --> 23:01.630
in your words? What would an ideal place of last resort look like?

23:01.830 --> 23:05.530
And yeah, so, um, some of that was in the comments that

23:06.030 --> 23:09.629
I made, um, that the City of London created a

23:10.129 --> 23:14.030
very comprehensive safe tenting protocol for their community,

23:14.069 --> 23:18.350
and it sets out some of the criteria that a

23:18.850 --> 23:22.810
place that is safe for folks to attend and what services

23:23.310 --> 23:26.890
are available. Um, and so I, I listed some of

23:27.390 --> 23:30.510
those in my comments, but there's an idea of

23:31.020 --> 23:35.180
marrying this site with also having wraparound services

23:35.680 --> 23:39.900
so folks can receive the support that they need to transition

23:40.400 --> 23:44.599
out of homelessness into better options.

23:44.679 --> 23:48.800
Okay, thank you. And Chair, just one follow-up. In terms of the wraparound

23:49.300 --> 23:54.040
services, um, in terms of

23:54.540 --> 23:58.320
resources for that, um, in the work that you do, where is the

23:58.820 --> 24:03.110
biggest gap in resources when it comes to some of those wraparound services

24:03.610 --> 24:07.309
that are required? I'm not

24:07.809 --> 24:11.270
sure that I'm in the best position to answer that question. I think

24:11.770 --> 24:15.630
really the region would be better positioned, but I would

24:16.130 --> 24:19.590
also say that the co-creators table with Patch

24:20.090 --> 24:23.850
as well as the lived expertise prototyping cohort would have

24:24.350 --> 24:26.350
answers to that better than I would.

24:26.890 --> 24:30.570
Thank you, Councilor Shantz.

24:30.869 --> 24:34.670
Thank you, uh, thank you for coming in today. I'm a little interested

24:35.170 --> 24:38.710
in, um, in numbers. So you, you gave us,

24:39.210 --> 24:42.790
um, an overview of, of the total number of homelessness that

24:43.290 --> 24:47.110
are, are, uh, on the books. If you'd like— that sounds really

24:47.610 --> 24:50.510
cold when you're talking about people. I'm sorry. Um,

24:52.100 --> 24:55.880
Apologies for that. What I'm

24:56.380 --> 24:59.859
interested in is we're talking today mostly about

25:00.359 --> 25:04.000
place of last resort and how we can create a place like

25:04.500 --> 25:08.560
that. Do you have numbers of people that are in that

25:09.060 --> 25:13.180
particular situation right now?

25:13.680 --> 25:17.200
So if my memory is correct, from the

25:17.700 --> 25:22.630
Point-in-Time Count of the 2,370 71.

25:23.130 --> 25:26.590
I believe it was 1009 who

25:27.090 --> 25:30.430
I also identified as living rough. Does that answer

25:30.930 --> 25:34.150
your question? Yes, thank you. Thanks,

25:34.650 --> 25:37.170
Joanna. That's all the questions we have. Thank you for coming in. The next delegation

25:37.670 --> 25:40.700
is Tanner Bergsma from Waterloo. Tanner, I know you're here in person.

25:41.510 --> 25:45.470
Uh, as you approach the podium, you have the floor in

25:45.970 --> 25:49.410
5 minutes, and you probably need to raise the podium. Being the

25:49.910 --> 25:50.980
tall person that you are.

25:55.170 --> 25:56.040
This is going to take forever.

25:59.850 --> 26:02.690
Sadly, we have no accompanying music, but it's pretty cool.

26:04.630 --> 26:08.710
Thank you, Chair. Chair, members of

26:09.210 --> 26:12.430
Regional Council, distinguished guests, and fellow residents. My name

26:12.930 --> 26:16.520
is Ambassador Tanner Evan Bergsma, and I am a youth delegate to the

26:17.020 --> 26:20.460
United Nations and co-founder of the Global Youth Sustainability Council. Today I

26:20.960 --> 26:24.700
stand before you not only as an advocate, but as someone who deeply believes

26:25.200 --> 26:28.740
in democratic governance, accountability, and the proper relationship

26:29.240 --> 26:32.230
between municipalities, provinces, and the federal government.

26:32.860 --> 26:36.660
I rise today out of grave concern regarding the situation at 100 Victoria Street

26:37.160 --> 26:40.680
North and the growing discussion surrounding potential provincial interventions through

26:41.180 --> 26:44.439
the use of the extraordinary legislation legislative powers of

26:44.939 --> 26:48.158
the notwithstanding clause. Let me be clear, this debate

26:48.366 --> 26:51.492
is about far more than one encampment. It is about whether

26:51.829 --> 26:55.964
municipalities in Ontario will retain the authority to govern matters

26:56.077 --> 27:00.084
within their own jurisdiction. It is about whether local governments elected

27:00.584 --> 27:04.140
by their local residents will continue to have a meaningful voice in decisions that directly

27:04.156 --> 27:07.794
affect their communities. And it is about whether governments will address

27:07.859 --> 27:10.714
homelessness through solutions or through symbolism.

27:11.389 --> 27:15.307
The Region of Waterloo faces a housing and homelessness crisis that did not just

27:15.807 --> 27:19.290
emerge overnight. The crisis is a result of decades of underinvestment

27:19.338 --> 27:23.225
in affordable housing and supportive housing and vast

27:23.725 --> 27:27.963
variety of mental health services and addictions treatments and homelessness inter— prevention.

27:29.376 --> 27:33.134
Municipalities are expected to respond to these challenges every day,

27:33.471 --> 27:37.165
yet they do not possess the constitutional powers or financial

27:37.665 --> 27:41.564
resources necessary to solve them alone. And that responsibility rests

27:41.580 --> 27:45.562
with all orders of government. The Province of Ontario

27:45.578 --> 27:49.833
has jurisdiction over healthcare, mental health, addictions treatment, and municipal

27:50.333 --> 27:54.281
governments. And the Government of Canada possesses the fiscal capacity necessary to

27:54.781 --> 27:58.584
make transformational investments in housing and infrastructure. And municipalities are

27:59.084 --> 28:02.421
responsible for delivering frontline services and responsible— and responding

28:02.533 --> 28:05.978
to the realities they see on the ground. Every level of government

28:06.478 --> 28:10.032
has a role to play. What concerns me today is the possibility

28:10.145 --> 28:14.007
that prov— that provincial intervention may occur without a comprehensive

28:14.055 --> 28:16.394
plan to address where individuals will go,

28:16.859 --> 28:20.609
what supports they will receive, and how municipalities will retain— maintain

28:20.641 --> 28:24.423
control over decisions within their own communities. Because removing

28:24.487 --> 28:28.367
people from an encampment is not the same as ending homelessness. And displacement

28:28.737 --> 28:32.029
is not just a housing strategy. And political

28:32.529 --> 28:36.526
announcements are not suitable for permanent solutions. The Ontario

28:37.026 --> 28:41.183
Superior Court of Justice has already confirmed that municipalities must consider Charter

28:41.683 --> 28:45.407
rights when considering to give accessible shelter alternatives

28:46.322 --> 28:50.161
when they are not available. And that ruling should not— should

28:50.661 --> 28:54.047
actually serve as a reminder that homelessness is not simply an administrative issue.

28:54.260 --> 28:57.860
It is a matter of human dignity and public policy and constitutional

28:58.360 --> 29:02.520
responsibility. Today I'm asking Regional Council to take a

29:03.020 --> 29:06.760
principled stand. First, I ask the council to formally request

29:07.260 --> 29:11.200
increased provincial funding and resources to support transitional housing,

29:11.220 --> 29:15.060
homelessness response efforts, and the community services for individuals

29:15.560 --> 29:19.200
affected by the situation at 100 Victoria Street North through

29:19.700 --> 29:23.321
an open letter to the provincial government. And second, I ask that the council

29:23.594 --> 29:27.784
to affirm that decisions regarding regional matters should remain under regional

29:28.284 --> 29:31.702
authority and that meaningful consultation

29:32.202 --> 29:35.716
must occur before any provincial intervention gets made.

29:36.679 --> 29:40.709
And third, I ask that the council to call upon the Government of Canada

29:41.209 --> 29:43.728
to significantly increase investments in affordable housing.

29:44.884 --> 29:48.540
This is not just some partisan issue. And homelessness does not

29:49.040 --> 29:52.310
ask who someone voted for.

29:53.499 --> 29:56.819
The reality at the end of the day is that we face

29:57.319 --> 30:00.800
a risk of housing instability and the region will

30:01.300 --> 30:04.780
lose its sovereignty and decision-making. It will, whether we like it or not,

30:05.280 --> 30:08.610
unless we decide to take extensive action today.

30:09.420 --> 30:12.480
And to Premier Ford, I offer this respectful message.

30:12.830 --> 30:16.580
Municipalities are not obstacles to overcome. They are partners

30:17.080 --> 30:20.859
in government. Support municipalities, fund municipalities, work within

30:21.359 --> 30:25.560
municipalities, but do not undermine local decision-making without meaningful

30:26.060 --> 30:29.240
consultation and a comprehensive plan.

30:29.740 --> 30:34.120
And to the Government of Canada, municipalities cannot continue carrying

30:34.620 --> 30:38.200
a national housing crisis on property tax revenues alone.

30:38.280 --> 30:42.060
Municipalities are struggling, and I'm sick and tired of seeing them not have

30:42.560 --> 30:45.840
the resources that they need to be able to tackle issues like this, so we

30:46.340 --> 30:50.659
must invest further into resources to help them.

30:51.159 --> 30:55.100
The question before us today is not whether people— on whether we can move

30:55.600 --> 31:00.100
the people, because that is not what is at play. We have to decide whether

31:00.600 --> 31:03.880
we can stand together as one to be able to do

31:04.380 --> 31:07.760
what's best for the people, and whether we will stand to protect our sovereignty

31:08.260 --> 31:12.130
and our people and our nations. Thank you. Thank you.

31:12.630 --> 31:16.080
Uh, pretty much right on the money for timing, Tanner. Um, we do have questions.

31:16.450 --> 31:20.170
Councillor, uh, James. Thank you. Through you, Chair, thank you for coming in today,

31:20.670 --> 31:23.990
uh, Tanner, and, um, recognizing the

31:24.490 --> 31:28.170
shortfall with resources and, and, you know, the way municipalities

31:28.670 --> 31:32.750
are disadvantaged. Um, I know you know a lot about governance and,

31:32.950 --> 31:37.150
and legislation. I just want to— do you know that the Constitution doesn't even

31:37.650 --> 31:41.020
mention municipalities in it. It really puts everything in the hands of the

31:41.520 --> 31:44.839
province. Absolutely. And the one thing that I keep seeing—

31:45.339 --> 31:48.520
I have done delegations across several different municipalities in Ontario,

31:49.020 --> 31:52.239
and it's the same thing. Provin— municipalities don't have the

31:52.739 --> 31:55.900
financial resources. The region doesn't have the financial resources to

31:56.400 --> 31:59.700
be able to deal with something like this. Yet what are we doing? Do and

32:00.200 --> 32:03.580
will we push back? Will we not allow— will we

32:04.080 --> 32:07.380
allow municipalities to be stepped over from the province? Because you may think, for example,

32:07.970 --> 32:11.760
that not taking a stand, a notwithstanding clause might be a benefit to

32:12.260 --> 32:15.540
you, but you lose the ability to govern in the future. You lose that

32:16.040 --> 32:18.860
ability. Once it's taken from you, it's not going to be coming back. The region

32:19.360 --> 32:23.640
needs to do an open letter to the government signifying that they will,

32:24.140 --> 32:28.080
in fact, protect its sovereignty and to ask for more funding.

32:28.580 --> 32:32.060
Don't be afraid to ask for more funding. Lead by example and lead for the

32:32.560 --> 32:36.370
municipalities of the of Ontario. That's what this region needs to

32:36.870 --> 32:42.170
do. Thank you. Councillor Wolff,

32:42.670 --> 32:46.549
uh, through you, Madam Chair, uh, thank you for an excellent presentation, and you

32:47.049 --> 32:51.190
seem to have hit all the right buttons in terms of where the responsibility truly

32:51.690 --> 32:54.610
lies. We have advocated to the provincial government,

32:55.110 --> 32:57.830
but, uh, for instance, on Odawa, um,

32:58.830 --> 33:01.620
on many, many things about funding.

33:02.050 --> 33:05.830
My question to you is, will you advocate to the

33:06.330 --> 33:10.390
province and to the federal government? I think that we

33:10.890 --> 33:15.190
hear from the people, all local government does hear from the

33:15.690 --> 33:19.450
people on the street, people around us, but I'm not sure that

33:19.950 --> 33:23.190
the province and the federal government do. The province and the federal

33:23.690 --> 33:26.480
government, quite frankly, don't give a crap about our people.

33:27.008 --> 33:30.774
But one of the things that I will continue to do as the

33:31.274 --> 33:34.555
roles that I am under is to continue to advocate. I am set to be

33:35.055 --> 33:38.015
addressing legislature in September, so it is something that we can discuss.

33:39.601 --> 33:42.854
But my continued advocacy has been raised on all levels,

33:42.886 --> 33:46.379
including the federal level as well, for housing concerns.

33:46.827 --> 33:50.144
Okay, thank you for doing that. Thank you, Tanner.

33:50.192 --> 33:53.348
Those are all the questions today, and again, thank you for coming in to delegate.

33:53.540 --> 33:57.490
Shannon Witten, And I believe this is a pre-recorded

33:57.990 --> 34:02.129
video which we will now play, so watch

34:02.629 --> 34:06.260
your monitors.

34:08.670 --> 34:12.170
Hello, thank you for allowing me to

34:12.670 --> 34:15.830
submit this recorded video as part

34:16.330 --> 34:18.240
of my ability to speak for this meeting.

34:19.260 --> 34:22.370
It's 9 AM on a Tuesday or sometime around then. I'm at work.

34:22.560 --> 34:25.740
So anyway, a little about me, the demographic that

34:26.240 --> 34:29.720
I'm coming from. My name is Shannon Whitten and I am a

34:30.220 --> 34:33.830
homeowner within the Kitchener Centre area. I'm very happy to be within that demographic.

34:34.140 --> 34:38.750
I bought with my partner Evan almost 2 years ago now.

34:38.860 --> 34:42.660
We are both working class, blue collar actually. I'm a Red Seal carpenter

34:43.160 --> 34:46.030
and he is on his way to becoming a Red Seal sheet metal worker.

34:46.700 --> 34:50.200
And I would love to say that we pulled ourselves up by our

34:50.700 --> 34:53.519
bootstraps and saved a bunch of money and just worked really hard and that's why

34:54.019 --> 34:56.649
we were able to afford in Kitchener and anybody should be able to do it,

34:57.940 --> 35:01.160
uh, when in reality I was raised by a single mother and my partner just

35:01.660 --> 35:04.839
doesn't have parents. He had an inheritance though and

35:05.339 --> 35:08.900
that's what we were able to put down and now my brother and my sister-in-law

35:09.400 --> 35:13.100
live with us to help make it affordable. So we are very aware

35:13.600 --> 35:19.020
that while we are extremely fortunate in

35:19.520 --> 35:22.279
the grand scheme of the world today, especially in Ontario.

35:23.640 --> 35:27.240
We are aware that we are much closer to being unhoused

35:27.740 --> 35:31.970
than being considered financially stable by,

35:32.620 --> 35:35.880
you know, the standard 20 years ago. So keeping that in

35:36.380 --> 35:39.720
mind, um, with the residents at

35:40.220 --> 35:44.340
100 Vic, we're about a 5-minute drive from them, and I've been following along

35:44.840 --> 35:48.380
with the work that's been put in and the

35:48.880 --> 35:53.440
dispute that's been going on. Nobody likes to see a

35:53.940 --> 35:57.119
housing encampment, but from where I'm coming from, and I feel confident speaking

35:57.619 --> 36:00.640
for the 4 adults in my household with this one, it's because we would like

36:01.140 --> 36:04.880
them to have housing. As far as my understanding is,

36:05.380 --> 36:08.990
this has already been put forward to the Ontario Superior Court court twice and

36:09.490 --> 36:13.110
clearing it's been considered illegal. I am in agreement with that. Judge Gibson

36:13.610 --> 36:17.950
has, um, said that we need to put in a safe tenting bylaw

36:18.450 --> 36:21.989
to move them. I am in agreement with that. I believe my household is as

36:22.489 --> 36:25.770
well. We want to see these people with safe options. They are

36:26.270 --> 36:28.890
our neighbors. They are just down the road from us.

36:30.090 --> 36:33.910
Moving them will only hide the issue that is ever growing

36:34.170 --> 36:37.470
not just in Kitchener but across Ontario.

36:38.030 --> 36:41.710
I am deeply against the use of

36:42.210 --> 36:46.090
the notwithstanding clause. The interference of Doug Ford to try and vacate

36:46.590 --> 36:50.190
an area just for transit production, I mean,

36:50.720 --> 36:54.080
that's a whole other conversation if we want to talk a little bit about irony.

36:54.290 --> 36:58.090
But in general, I don't believe in notwithstanding

36:58.590 --> 37:02.210
clauses, especially in this case though to interfere with something

37:02.710 --> 37:06.590
that's already been handled by the, uh, by the court systems.

37:06.600 --> 37:10.310
And I personally would like to see more resources put in place,

37:10.810 --> 37:14.749
especially, you know, it's never too soon to start. The winter is coming and,

37:15.249 --> 37:19.170
uh, I believe Kitchener really dropped the ball

37:19.670 --> 37:22.990
this past winter when it came to providing warm alternatives for my

37:23.490 --> 37:26.670
neighbors. Um, and it's not gonna be getting

37:27.170 --> 37:29.430
any better. I'm a believer in a housing first solution.

37:30.550 --> 37:33.920
I think that we're a little bit a ways off from that,

37:34.420 --> 37:38.799
as unfortunate as it is, and that's again where having safe tenting bylaws could

37:39.299 --> 37:41.310
really benefit us in the meantime.

37:42.900 --> 37:46.460
But the first thing that we should be prioritizing

37:46.960 --> 37:50.520
is respecting the residents that are at the 100 Victoria

37:51.020 --> 37:54.780
encampment, reminding ourselves that these are very

37:55.280 --> 37:58.950
much like humans that we are all so

37:59.450 --> 38:03.170
much closer to, for any reason, being in a situation

38:03.670 --> 38:08.150
where we've suddenly lost shelter, and they deserve empathy and

38:08.650 --> 38:12.550
respect and to be treated with dignity. I come

38:13.050 --> 38:16.710
from Hamilton, my partner is born

38:17.210 --> 38:20.890
and raised Kitchener-Waterloo, and I've seen how devastating

38:21.390 --> 38:25.190
clearing encampments can be. So I don't think moving them without

38:25.690 --> 38:29.570
a substantial solution is the way for us to move forward,

38:30.070 --> 38:33.740
and I hope that that can be taken into consideration with today's meeting.

38:33.750 --> 38:37.120
So 5 minutes isn't a lot of time, but that's okay because I'll ramble.

38:37.470 --> 38:41.730
So again, thank you for giving me the opportunity to speak, and I

38:42.230 --> 38:45.010
hope everything goes smoothly. I'll be following along as much as I can from work

38:48.670 --> 38:51.860
So colleagues, that was Shannon Whitten. Uh,

38:52.360 --> 38:55.520
the next delegation is Kevin White from Kitchener. Kevin, I believe

38:56.020 --> 39:00.500
you're in the audience. Uh, you have the podium in 5 minutes.

39:05.610 --> 39:09.640
Hello, Regional Council.

39:10.140 --> 39:13.480
Um, I'm here to speak in favor of the region developing an emergency

39:13.980 --> 39:17.938
safe tenting bylaw before the June 22nd appeal deadline. Following the April 23rd,

39:17.954 --> 39:21.719
2025 decision to impose yet another bylaw that violated the Charter rights of

39:22.219 --> 39:25.628
the homeless and proposed punishment for people providing aid on site at 100 Victoria,

39:25.708 --> 39:29.057
a meeting at which multiple delegates, including the lawyers who would go on

39:29.105 --> 39:32.437
to defeat your attempts to violate the constitutional rights of your constituents multiple

39:32.453 --> 39:36.283
times in court, warned you against implementing, I sent you a letter complaining of

39:36.783 --> 39:40.432
your lack of imagination and unity on council. I noted that for all that councillors

39:40.448 --> 39:44.390
have complained about an unresponsive, uncooperative, negligent, and hostile provincial

39:44.890 --> 39:48.040
government, each of you seem quite comfortable replicating similar behavior in your

39:48.540 --> 39:52.039
regional government roles. And this appears to be continuing while the region tries to pretend

39:52.539 --> 39:56.220
that its fewer than 500 shelter beds are providing adequate support for more than 2,300

39:56.720 --> 40:00.380
homeless residents, a number which has continued to grow from 333 counted in

40:00.880 --> 40:04.650
2018. And I note the significance of the change in provincial leadership in that year.

40:05.040 --> 40:08.700
However, the failure to address your own agreements, such as adopting the protocols

40:09.200 --> 40:12.600
outlined in the Plan to End Chronic Homelessness, which were developed between regional staff,

40:13.100 --> 40:15.880
people with lived expertise, advocates, service providers, and others, has been an ongoing point of

40:16.380 --> 40:20.000
contention in community, and Regional Council time and time

40:20.500 --> 40:23.819
again has not engaged in earnest dialogue as regional staff did during the development of

40:24.319 --> 40:27.540
the plan. I wish again to draw your attention to the region's stated mission,

40:28.040 --> 40:32.060
vision, and values. Our vision, what we aspire to: a compassionate community

40:32.560 --> 40:36.160
that cares for all people, stewards the land for future generations,

40:36.660 --> 40:40.480
and where everyone has the opportunity to live a good life. Our mission,

40:40.980 --> 40:44.740
what we do: providing essential services that support quality of

40:45.240 --> 40:47.780
life for each and every person in Waterloo Region. Our core value, how we work:

40:49.792 --> 40:53.173
Care for people and the land we share. To be clear,

40:53.237 --> 40:56.826
with regard to homelessness in Waterloo Region and at 100 Victoria in particular,

40:56.842 --> 41:00.047
to say that you have utterly failed in your mission, vision, and values would be

41:00.547 --> 41:03.861
putting it very, very politely. Because beyond your attempts to repeatedly

41:03.877 --> 41:06.986
violate the Charter rights of residents in the region, these actions blatantly

41:07.082 --> 41:10.223
oppose the stated mission, vision, and values of the region. It is

41:10.723 --> 41:14.485
an embarrassingly disrespectful misuse of taxpayer funds to wage an assault on the rights

41:14.985 --> 41:17.960
of those with the least in our community. It has been a refusal to consider

41:18.460 --> 41:21.880
options that would align with the mission, vision, and values, and a passive acceptance

41:22.380 --> 41:25.660
and reification of the province's hostility to the poor and homeless and those living with

41:26.160 --> 41:30.070
addictions. As Justice Gibson writes, the homeless are not other, they are us.

41:30.300 --> 41:33.820
You use taxpayer dollars, which we pay toward what we imagine to

41:34.320 --> 41:37.940
be the public good, to attempt to violate our Charter rights. None of

41:38.440 --> 41:42.060
us are praising encampments as humanitarian or as adequate support for the social determinants

41:42.560 --> 41:45.860
of health or housing. It has been a frustrating process to have to argue for

41:46.360 --> 41:49.780
the support we want for homeless community members, that is, is housing.

41:50.280 --> 41:53.520
Safe, stable, and supportive housing. But instead, we are reduced to begging for

41:54.020 --> 41:57.920
mitigation and management, which the region twists into containment strategies further and further

41:58.420 --> 42:01.540
away from that goal, one which seems even further out of reach under the current

42:02.040 --> 42:05.560
moratorium. We have asked for progressive and inclusive zoning, funding for support

42:06.060 --> 42:10.020
workers, any defense at all for the valuable work provided at the now-defunct safe consumption

42:10.520 --> 42:14.020
site, transitional housing, emergency shelters, warming and cooling centers,

42:14.520 --> 42:17.777
encampments, and now, as Justice Gibson points out in his ruling, a place where people

42:18.277 --> 42:20.309
who are homeless are allowed to legally exist at all.

42:22.344 --> 42:26.494
At previous council meetings, delegates have accused council of delaying meaningful responses

42:26.526 --> 42:30.035
with the intent of having the province use the notwithstanding clause to evict residents

42:30.535 --> 42:32.807
at 100 Victoria who will otherwise have nowhere else to go.

42:33.817 --> 42:37.133
Council adamantly denied this. Without action taken now,

42:37.342 --> 42:40.578
despite many warnings and opportunities for council to course correct well

42:40.626 --> 42:44.039
in advance, we are now at imminent risk of the delegates being proven correct.

42:45.035 --> 42:48.440
The Region has been relying extensively on the volunteer work of individual

42:48.472 --> 42:52.182
community members above and beyond those working in roles for service providers for

42:52.682 --> 42:56.278
years to even have the inadequate support in place that we have. The least that

42:56.778 --> 43:00.245
could be provided is a location whereby consistent mutual aid from those volunteers

43:00.277 --> 43:03.601
and other service providers may be delivered and relationships may be fostered.

43:04.228 --> 43:07.681
One hopes that our taxes may instead support that rather than attacks on our Charter

43:08.181 --> 43:10.299
rights and further criminalization of poverty and homelessness.

43:11.630 --> 43:15.319
Regional councillors have objected to delegates saying that they lack empathy.

43:15.939 --> 43:19.259
I would argue further that this regional council, as it relates to homelessness and the

43:19.759 --> 43:23.429
residents of 100 Victoria, struggles beyond this in that you have not been remotely trustworthy,

43:24.000 --> 43:27.600
which is something the federal housing advocate warned you against.

43:27.860 --> 43:31.460
As for integrity, you have none. Your words

43:31.960 --> 43:35.740
and actions are opposed. You're crossing a line here, so watch

43:36.240 --> 43:41.089
your words carefully, please. Your words and actions are opposed.

43:42.020 --> 43:46.199
Implement a safe tenting bylaw. Oppose the province's hostility toward democracy

43:46.699 --> 43:48.470
and the public good. And stand up for your community.

43:50.340 --> 43:53.040
There are no questions. Thank you.

43:53.540 --> 43:56.920
Next delegation. And I'm going to

43:57.420 --> 44:01.500
remind the gallery again that if you want to show support,

44:02.000 --> 44:04.280
you can use jazz hands. We've done that in the past. There will be no

44:04.780 --> 44:06.150
clapping. Cheering or booing.

44:09.829 --> 44:13.930
Farah Jurdi, um, who I believe is virtual and from Waterloo.

44:14.430 --> 44:18.379
So Farah, welcome. You have the podium and you have, uh, 5 minutes.

44:19.850 --> 44:23.170
Thank you. Hello, Council. Um, I'm here to talk to

44:23.670 --> 44:27.420
you today as a concerned resident of the Waterloo Region about the 100 Vic encampment.

44:27.430 --> 44:30.830
I have the privilege of being able to delegate at, um, the Council

44:31.330 --> 44:34.650
from the comfort of my home. Where I'm allowed to be at the table discussing

44:35.150 --> 44:38.929
actual real-life consequences to our community members.

44:39.429 --> 44:43.150
Clearing out the 100 Victoria Street encampment in the name of regional development and building

44:43.650 --> 44:46.890
the transit hub without organizing adequate housing alternatives or

44:47.390 --> 44:50.980
a safe tenting bylaw is a violation of the Charter of Rights and Freedoms.

44:51.070 --> 44:54.990
What Doug Ford is threatening with the notwithstanding clause is a blatant disregard for

44:55.490 --> 44:59.010
human rights, and that matters whether it's 1,000 people,

44:59.510 --> 45:03.070
100 people, or 30 people. Because when we lose our ability to

45:03.570 --> 45:07.010
see one another as ourselves, we lose our direction, we lose

45:07.510 --> 45:11.290
our heart, and we lose what makes us conscious, empathetic people.

45:11.790 --> 45:15.830
So then systems get built that not only prioritize but protect profit

45:16.330 --> 45:19.830
over people. And decisions such as Judge Gibson's that insist upon

45:20.330 --> 45:23.610
upholding human rights get scrutinized, they get challenged, and they're

45:24.110 --> 45:27.380
called ridiculous by fascists like Doug Ford. The people of

45:27.880 --> 45:31.620
this region have had enough. We refuse to bend to the demands of agencies like

45:32.120 --> 45:35.660
Metrolinx who have absolutely no interest in the people of our communities.

45:35.900 --> 45:39.260
And we're forced to be here and essentially beg for you to see it our

45:39.760 --> 45:43.360
way, to plead that we can take a stand as a region that protects something

45:43.860 --> 45:46.880
as fundamental as Section 15 and 7 of the Charter.

45:47.180 --> 45:50.480
How humiliating. In your

45:50.980 --> 45:54.180
position, I wonder how comfortable you may be with prolonging this already years-long

45:54.680 --> 45:56.320
deliberation of whether or not unhoused people deserve to be protected by the Charter.

45:58.240 --> 46:02.010
Cities and communities, or if they deserve to have access to social resources,

46:02.259 --> 46:05.820
affordable housing, access to a bathroom or proper meal.

46:06.140 --> 46:10.040
Who are we to decide who is deserving of basic humanity and how

46:10.540 --> 46:14.360
we get to protect that? What precedent does that set? Using the

46:14.860 --> 46:18.360
effort, the resources, as well as the time that it's already taken to deliberate

46:18.860 --> 46:22.340
whether these people deserve dignity and protection from further imposed precarious

46:22.840 --> 46:26.100
situations, maybe we would have had a stronger region for us all by now.

46:26.360 --> 46:29.440
When will the region stop talking about what to do about

46:29.940 --> 46:33.169
the people at the encampment in abstract and actually start taking action?

46:33.539 --> 46:36.979
Maybe the empty beds across the street would actually serve community members who

46:37.479 --> 46:41.069
need it. Maybe then we would begin to move toward a stable future,

46:41.460 --> 46:45.260
a future that does not rely on the dehumanization and the subjugation of

46:45.760 --> 46:49.250
real people, nor exacerbate harm to the residents of the encampment.

46:49.420 --> 46:52.620
Maybe we'll see a today that a regional council decision

46:53.120 --> 46:56.790
does not lose— uh, continue to lose public trust and that the

46:57.290 --> 47:01.210
people sitting on the council actually care about something as fundamental as human rights.

47:01.390 --> 47:05.169
So your decision at the end of these delegations will be remembered by every resident

47:05.669 --> 47:08.870
at 100 Vic, every person who's one emergency away,

47:09.370 --> 47:12.850
one paycheck away from losing their housing, and everyone else in the region, and under

47:13.350 --> 47:16.830
the scrutiny of the entire province and country. So that's why I'm delegating for

47:17.330 --> 47:20.590
the very first time this morning, because it's always been the people that have each

47:21.090 --> 47:24.500
other's backs. So I'm asking, will you have the back of the encampment residents,

47:24.770 --> 47:29.209
and will you have ours in case we need them? Thank you.

47:29.709 --> 47:33.850
Thank you very much, uh, Farah. I have no questions. The next delegation is Laura

47:34.350 --> 47:38.570
Mosher. Laura, I believe you're in person from Kitchener.

47:39.850 --> 47:42.930
You have 5 minutes, and the podium is yours, and you may want to lower

47:43.430 --> 47:43.560
the podium.

47:46.720 --> 47:46.954
Okay,

47:56.534 --> 48:06.967
hello council,

48:07.144 --> 48:11.183
and thank you for the opportunity to speak today. My daughter has to be

48:11.683 --> 48:14.810
here with me because this council settled on a short notice change of plans,

48:15.310 --> 48:17.510
so I'll leave the expletives out today.

48:18.579 --> 48:22.290
But it's now clear to me that this council is not actually listening

48:22.790 --> 48:26.570
to delegates when we say anything you don't want to hear. So let

48:27.070 --> 48:30.630
me address this delegation to you, kiddo. This is

48:31.130 --> 48:34.510
how our community is run. We are told to take time out of our busy

48:35.010 --> 48:38.810
schedules if we want to be heard, only for people like us to

48:39.310 --> 48:42.889
get ignored. Crowds gather to beg

48:43.389 --> 48:46.729
this council to do the right thing, but their opinions are already

48:47.229 --> 48:49.520
bought and owed to more powerful people.

48:50.270 --> 48:53.850
Your grandma and uncle have been forced out of their apartment

48:54.350 --> 48:58.350
because of this council's slow motion towards doing anything about rent evictions.

48:59.390 --> 49:02.930
Your schizoaffective uncle is forced to sleep in a bed set up in a small

49:03.430 --> 49:06.680
living room. Because this council is so bad at their jobs that there is nowhere

49:07.180 --> 49:10.799
else for your uncle to spend the night. But if you can believe it,

49:10.940 --> 49:14.099
he's in a lucky situation. There are people

49:14.599 --> 49:18.480
stuck living outside. They don't get a bed. If they

49:18.980 --> 49:22.180
try to sleep, they will be forced awake and forced

49:22.680 --> 49:25.310
to move. This council thinks that's acceptable.

49:26.320 --> 49:29.420
In fact, this council would prefer them to not even have the power to set

49:29.920 --> 49:32.700
up their own tent. They won't even look into safe tenting.

49:34.699 --> 49:37.960
So a group of people without shelter got together to protect each other from

49:38.460 --> 49:41.679
this council's drive to ruin their chances of sleeping in the same place every night.

49:43.080 --> 49:45.900
People just like them live— or people like them,

49:46.400 --> 49:49.750
yeah, just like them, live by the train station. You know them.

49:50.500 --> 49:52.750
You helped put cement on the walls of one of their shelters.

49:54.600 --> 49:58.500
This council wants to tear it all down. They are so determined,

49:59.000 --> 50:03.279
they took these poor people to court, the highest court in Ontario.

50:03.680 --> 50:07.500
And the judge told them, 

50:08.000 --> 50:11.190
Get them beds first.

50:11.480 --> 50:14.420
They found a loophole. They said,

50:14.920 --> 50:18.800
What if we just say its just for this piece of land?

50:19.300 --> 50:22.760
they took these poor people back to court. The second judge told

50:23.260 --> 50:26.480
them, No, that violates human rights.

50:26.980 --> 50:28.750
Get them shelter or let them make their own.

50:30.599 --> 50:34.439
So now here we are. Why are we here?

50:34.900 --> 50:38.449
To again ask council to let these poor people make their own shelter.

50:39.400 --> 50:42.960
Do you think they will? Or do you

50:43.460 --> 50:44.130
think they'll try another trick?

50:46.300 --> 50:50.240
Two judges and countless delegates have told this council how

50:50.740 --> 50:54.660
to stop violating humans and their rights. I'm only

50:55.160 --> 50:59.079
asking them to stop being servants of powerful fascists and let the most vulnerable

50:59.579 --> 51:03.239
in this region build the shelters this council is too incompetent to provide.

51:03.739 --> 51:06.940
Today you'll hear what they decide.

51:09.010 --> 51:14.560
Thank you, Laura. There are no questions. The next delegation is

51:15.060 --> 51:17.440
Kathy Eckert. Kathy, I believe you're here in person.

51:20.080 --> 51:22.779
The podium is yours and you have 5 minutes.

51:27.200 --> 51:31.500
Stand,

51:32.000 --> 51:35.360
please.

51:35.860 --> 51:38.190
Stand. Oh, my phone's acting up.

51:39.700 --> 51:41.790
Okay, we ready?

51:43.660 --> 51:47.200
Thank you again to the Region Council for allowing me to speak again.

51:48.089 --> 51:52.030
Just a few reality checks. I also ride the public transit

51:52.530 --> 51:56.130
in this community. My life has not been affected by not having a

51:56.630 --> 51:59.950
transit hub. There are 4 basic needs

52:00.450 --> 52:03.030
for a person to survive. That's shelter,

52:03.530 --> 52:06.730
clothes, food, and medicine. The Region has

52:07.230 --> 52:09.950
not provided any of these things to the homeless.

52:10.300 --> 52:13.890
I.e., tents are donated to the homeless on the

52:14.390 --> 52:18.069
streets. The soup kitchen in St. Mary's Church and communities

52:18.569 --> 52:22.260
donate food and clothes to feed the homeless.

52:23.290 --> 52:28.590
And OHIP and Sangwan provide medicine.

52:28.610 --> 52:29.880
Get back here.

52:32.020 --> 52:36.190
Ah. The region want the responsibility to

52:36.690 --> 52:40.300
look after the homeless people. And so far, it is the community looking

52:40.800 --> 52:44.660
after them, not the Region, nor has the Region provided any

52:45.160 --> 52:48.860
shelter for them and have failed in honouring your contractual

52:49.360 --> 52:53.100
obligations. Maybe one day the community

52:53.600 --> 52:58.080
will vote in elected officials who don't cry and use

52:58.580 --> 53:02.640
the notwithstanding clause because they can't always have what they want.

53:02.740 --> 53:05.060
But you do have the four basic needs.

53:06.285 --> 53:09.381
To survive, you have clothes, food,

53:09.718 --> 53:14.050
shelter, and medicine. Buddhist monks are also homeless

53:14.290 --> 53:18.365
voluntarily. Our homeless are not homeless voluntarily.

53:19.216 --> 53:22.970
So think mindfully about your decision for the homeless and

53:23.470 --> 53:26.499
what it means to the Buddhist community of Waterloo Region,

53:27.221 --> 53:30.574
Ontario, and Canada, since Buddhist monks are also

53:31.074 --> 53:34.440
homeless. Respect is given to all

53:34.940 --> 53:38.657
people, as explained in Section 15 of the Canadian Charter of

53:38.689 --> 53:41.817
Rights and Freedoms, to promote diversity

53:42.218 --> 53:45.008
and living in peace and harmony in the community.

53:45.585 --> 53:49.851
Respect is not given on the basis of material wealth or

53:49.915 --> 53:53.203
gain. That is not Charter grounds for

53:53.703 --> 53:57.399
a basis of human rights abuse. And trust must

53:57.899 --> 54:01.179
be earned. If you keep disobeying a court order,

54:01.679 --> 54:05.340
it is called contempt of court. That means His Majesty the

54:05.840 --> 54:09.120
King's court detains you at their pleasure until

54:09.620 --> 54:13.760
you are called to apologize to His Majesty's

54:14.260 --> 54:17.440
court. In your rush to disrespect the

54:17.940 --> 54:21.840
homeless, you disrespect our King by using the notwithstanding

54:22.340 --> 54:26.060
clause, ignoring the Truth and Reconciliation and every

54:26.560 --> 54:30.120
child matters. To quote Venerable Bhikkhu

54:30.620 --> 54:34.619
Pannakara, try this when making your decisions:

54:35.119 --> 54:39.500
in the first second, choose loving kindness. It doesn't require power,

54:40.000 --> 54:43.480
money, or a title. It's simply a choice to stop hurting

54:43.980 --> 54:47.140
a person. In the second second,

54:47.640 --> 54:50.960
choose compassion. We don't know another's pain.

54:51.070 --> 54:55.089
They carry, what loss they have faced,

54:55.589 --> 54:57.230
or how hard they are trying to survive.

54:58.930 --> 55:02.090
Compassion does not mean agreement. It means not

55:02.590 --> 55:06.750
adding more pain. A compassionate world doesn't abandon the

55:07.250 --> 55:11.490
weak and create more suffering. And the third second,

55:11.990 --> 55:15.330
to love. Children grow up safe, others live

55:15.830 --> 55:18.980
with dignity, and we don't turn away those in pain and

55:19.480 --> 55:23.020
suffering. The fourth second, choose harmony,

55:23.520 --> 55:27.379
to live in peace. In the fifth second, choose hope.

55:27.600 --> 55:30.610
Peace does not come from violence or hatred.

55:30.740 --> 55:34.740
It comes from living and caring for each other. And hope

55:35.240 --> 55:39.200
that progress is not built on human suffering. I am

55:39.700 --> 55:43.345
Canadian and a practitioner of Theravada Buddhism. I have not

55:43.845 --> 55:46.923
forgotten the unwholesome remarks this region of Waterloo members,

55:47.099 --> 55:50.484
shelter staff, and members of the community also said about

55:50.984 --> 55:53.420
me when I was homeless and formerly homeless.

55:55.297 --> 55:58.635
And the court apologized to me on

55:59.453 --> 56:02.806
your behalf when

56:02.838 --> 56:06.015
you showed me no compassion over the death of my late husband.

56:08.775 --> 56:12.291
Long live His Majesty King Charles III.

56:13.576 --> 56:16.626
God bless our King. May you all be happy,

56:16.883 --> 56:20.801
peaceful, and well. Kathy, thank you for

56:21.301 --> 56:24.430
delegating. I see no questions. The next

56:24.930 --> 56:28.894
delegate is virtual, Lily Fozard.

56:29.151 --> 56:32.619
Lily is from Waterloo. Lily, you have 5 minutes.

56:35.040 --> 56:38.350
Hello, my name is Lili Fawzard. I am many things, but relevant to

56:38.850 --> 56:41.440
this speech are that I live in Waterloo Region and that I am transfeminine.

56:42.150 --> 56:46.150
I join you here from Toronto outside of Women's College Hospital due to the last-minute

56:46.650 --> 56:50.010
change in time and date. My thanks to the council for showing such respect and

56:50.510 --> 56:53.610
care for our time. I address you during Pride Month about

56:54.110 --> 56:57.910
the notwithstanding clause. For those of you joining from Cambridge, Pride Month is a

56:58.410 --> 57:02.170
celebration of queers, a political protest against the cis-heteronormative regime and

57:02.670 --> 57:05.899
the mechanisms of state that are used to this day to oppress us. To murder

57:06.399 --> 57:10.280
us. One such mechanism is the notwithstanding clause, an ill-thought-out compromise

57:10.780 --> 57:15.199
designed to give provinces the ability to overrule the courts on matters of

57:15.699 --> 57:19.369
technical infringement on the Charter, supposedly to benefit the disenfranchised.

57:20.340 --> 57:23.500
What has it actually been used for? Human rights violation after

57:24.000 --> 57:27.900
human rights violation. According to the Canadian Civil Liberties Association, in recent

57:28.400 --> 57:32.629
years the clause has been used to prevent education workers from striking, ban provincial

57:33.129 --> 57:36.689
government employees, daycare workers, and school staff from wearing religious symbols,

57:36.790 --> 57:40.429
ban prayers in public and at school, promote assimilist policies at the

57:40.929 --> 57:44.250
expense of interculturalism, limit third-party political spending ahead of

57:44.750 --> 57:48.490
elections, prohibit gender-affirming care for trans youth, ban transgender

57:48.990 --> 57:53.150
girls from participating in amateur female sports, and prevent trans youth from being—

57:53.650 --> 57:57.090
from using their chosen names and pronouns in schools. I want to be clear here:

57:57.590 --> 58:01.230
the above uses have and will continue to cause death, especially of trans youth,

58:01.730 --> 58:04.203
a population who are disproportionately affected by homelessness.

58:05.166 --> 58:09.017
In short, the perfect mechanism to allow the government to brush

58:09.161 --> 58:13.237
all the undesirables in our society under a rug, to force those denied housing,

58:13.509 --> 58:16.686
denied the right to live, to the margins, out of our cities.

58:17.264 --> 58:20.890
If not here, where? You have been told repeatedly by

58:21.390 --> 58:24.484
this community the options you have before you. You have been told repeatedly by the

58:24.984 --> 58:28.223
courts that your actions violate the Charter. Your options are simple: pass a safe

58:28.271 --> 58:31.817
tenting bylaw or delay long enough that Doug Ford violates human rights for you.

58:32.160 --> 58:36.030
It's interesting to see the rhetoric of drug use invoked over and over again,

58:36.070 --> 58:39.430
almost as a mantra justifying the over-policing and criminalization of

58:39.930 --> 58:43.050
those this government has failed. It reminds me of how we as trans women are

58:43.550 --> 58:47.030
called rapists and pedophiles, how hatred and rhetoric are used to justify

58:47.530 --> 58:51.590
our social and literal murders. Our humanity is stripped from us until we are caricatures,

58:52.090 --> 58:55.110
scarecrows at whose feet the sins of society can

58:55.610 --> 58:59.627
be laid, our lives and suffering sacrifices you are willing to make I struggle

59:00.127 --> 59:03.970
to see humanity in you. I struggle to see anything redeeming in you. And yet,

59:04.194 --> 59:07.319
our social framework, our charter of rights, demands that I do.

59:08.040 --> 59:11.565
That is the promise of such a document, flawed as it is, that even the

59:11.581 --> 59:15.106
worst among us are guaranteed protection. I will withstand you.

59:15.427 --> 59:18.744
I will withstand those like you. I have two asks of this institution.

59:19.096 --> 59:22.958
Firstly, pass a safe tenting bylaw. And second, when you eventually come

59:23.458 --> 59:28.190
for me, have the decency to murder me in cold blood. What a Pride Month.

59:28.690 --> 59:31.910
I intended to leave my delegation there, but as I was approaching Women's College

59:32.410 --> 59:36.230
Hospital, I ran into a crowd of community service workers striking for wages stolen under

59:36.730 --> 59:40.390
Bill 124. They have the bravery and the courage to resist

59:40.890 --> 59:45.710
Doug Ford. Do you?

59:45.820 --> 59:49.550
Thank you, Lily. I have no questions. Thank you for

59:50.050 --> 59:53.770
delegating. And the next delegate, I believe, is in person. It's Megan Snyder.

59:53.780 --> 59:57.500
And Megan is from Kitchener. So Megan, when you get to the podium, you have

59:58.000 --> 01:00:01.520
5 minutes. Thank you. Thank you

01:00:02.020 --> 01:00:06.000
for the opportunity to speak. My name is Megan and I am a homeowner in

01:00:06.500 --> 01:00:10.720
Kitchener. I find it very telling that today you have almost 30

01:00:11.220 --> 01:00:14.780
delegates, a number of written submissions, an email sent

01:00:15.280 --> 01:00:18.940
by many folks to all of you. This many people here in chambers on

01:00:19.440 --> 01:00:23.120
a Tuesday morning at 9 a.m. And this is all without any

01:00:23.620 --> 01:00:28.220
actual evidence yet that you are appealing or otherwise resisting

01:00:28.720 --> 01:00:32.660
Justice Gibson's decision. We don't actually know

01:00:33.160 --> 01:00:36.520
if you've worked something out with your friend from the province or if

01:00:37.020 --> 01:00:40.420
you're going to continue going after 100 Vic. I mean,

01:00:40.920 --> 01:00:44.140
we know because we've all hung around this place enough,

01:00:44.640 --> 01:00:48.380
but we don't actually know. We just know

01:00:48.880 --> 01:00:52.040
that we really sadly can't trust you to

01:00:52.540 --> 01:00:56.519
do the humane thing. We know that two Ontario

01:00:57.019 --> 01:01:00.880
Superior Court justices told you that evicting 100

01:01:01.380 --> 01:01:04.640
Vic would be unconstitutional and a violation

01:01:05.140 --> 01:01:08.180
of the Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms,

01:01:08.680 --> 01:01:12.340
depriving your constituents of their rights to life,

01:01:12.840 --> 01:01:16.950
liberty, and security of the person we know that isn't enough

01:01:17.450 --> 01:01:20.070
for you to stop trying to make it happen.

01:01:20.969 --> 01:01:24.350
I would like to quote Justice Gibson, reading from pages 5 to

01:01:24.850 --> 01:01:28.670
6 of his 88-page decision: Homelessness

01:01:29.170 --> 01:01:32.510
is a public policy issue that engages practical,

01:01:33.010 --> 01:01:36.410
moral, and legal dimensions. The prevalence of

01:01:36.910 --> 01:01:40.110
encampments reflects Canada's challenges in meeting its

01:01:40.610 --> 01:01:43.933
human rights Obligation to ensure that all

01:01:44.141 --> 01:01:46.902
individuals have access to adequate housing,

01:01:47.576 --> 01:01:50.031
housing that is secure, affordable,

01:01:50.385 --> 01:01:54.028
habitable, accessible, culturally appropriate,

01:01:54.573 --> 01:01:57.671
and in a suitable location with access to services,

01:01:58.329 --> 01:02:02.325
while faced with the practical constraints of available resources

01:02:02.502 --> 01:02:05.824
and the challenges in resource allocation, as well

01:02:05.888 --> 01:02:09.066
as planning for projects that aim to advance the public good.

01:02:09.965 --> 01:02:13.561
Assessing the issue of homeless encampments from a Charter perspective

01:02:13.914 --> 01:02:17.430
requires consideration of contending factors that

01:02:17.510 --> 01:02:21.604
often seem to pull in different directions. It is a complex

01:02:21.620 --> 01:02:24.557
and contentious task, often fraught with emotion,

01:02:24.750 --> 01:02:28.811
with no simple solution. But it ultimately

01:02:28.844 --> 01:02:32.761
needs to be informed by one primary thought: the

01:02:32.841 --> 01:02:37.260
homeless are not other. They are us.

01:02:37.410 --> 01:02:40.669
They are rights bearers, no less entitled than

01:02:41.169 --> 01:02:44.310
any other Canadian citizens to the full benefit

01:02:44.810 --> 01:02:48.790
and protection of the Charter. I'll read that last part again.

01:02:48.810 --> 01:02:52.770
They are rights bearers, no less entitled than

01:02:53.270 --> 01:02:56.790
any other Canadian citizens to the full benefit and

01:02:57.290 --> 01:03:01.121
protection of the Charter. Human rights This is me speaking

01:03:01.153 --> 01:03:03.845
now. Human rights are an obligation,

01:03:04.278 --> 01:03:08.652
not an option. You right now behind

01:03:08.684 --> 01:03:12.819
closed doors are presumably trying to find your way out

01:03:12.931 --> 01:03:16.745
of a human rights obligation. You are trying

01:03:16.841 --> 01:03:21.119
to make it okay and justifiable to violate your constituents'

01:03:21.183 --> 01:03:25.189
rights to life. There is no justification when

01:03:25.689 --> 01:03:29.634
it comes to our human rights. The homeless are not

01:03:29.987 --> 01:03:32.669
other, they are us, they are you.

01:03:33.680 --> 01:03:37.518
We are bound to each other in upholding our human

01:03:37.534 --> 01:03:41.387
rights, and if that is something that you are not willing to take

01:03:41.887 --> 01:03:45.402
up as a regional councillor, I would really urge you to

01:03:45.418 --> 01:03:49.175
reconsider whether you belong in this role and whether you should

01:03:49.675 --> 01:03:51.937
remain sitting around this horseshoe. Thank you.

01:03:55.260 --> 01:03:58.890
Okay, again, jazz hands work, clapping doesn't.

01:03:59.390 --> 01:04:02.470
Uh, Megan, there is a question. Councillor Rodriguez.

01:04:02.970 --> 01:04:06.589
Thanks for coming in, Megan. Um, I think you were here in January when council

01:04:07.089 --> 01:04:10.390
tried to take a step towards understanding more about a safe tenting

01:04:10.890 --> 01:04:11.900
protocol, and I think,

01:04:12.010 --> 01:04:15.670
um, there was an understanding

01:04:16.170 --> 01:04:20.860
of where the support was and where there might be impediments to implementing

01:04:21.360 --> 01:04:24.719
a safe protocol in Waterloo Region. And we'll talk about Thunder Bay or London.

01:04:24.780 --> 01:04:28.220
Those are single-tier municipalities that have sort of

01:04:28.720 --> 01:04:31.960
final say in their communities. And so we're in this unique position where

01:04:32.460 --> 01:04:36.380
any, any land that we have as a, as a government is in

01:04:36.880 --> 01:04:40.860
an area municipality or is a provincial land or Conservation Authority or

01:04:41.360 --> 01:04:44.680
someone else's thing. Have you had a chance in that time since

01:04:45.180 --> 01:04:48.080
January to have any conversations with some of those partners, or have you thought about—

01:04:48.580 --> 01:04:51.770
if this is something that we're trying to figure out, how do we— you know,

01:04:51.800 --> 01:04:55.580
council brought forward the managed encampment on Urbis

01:04:56.080 --> 01:04:59.840
Road. And that's as far as that got.

01:05:00.340 --> 01:05:04.920
So just curious if you had any conversations in that regard about—

01:05:05.420 --> 01:05:08.440
in terms of that jurisdictional issue that I think some of us are facing in

01:05:08.940 --> 01:05:12.190
wanting to learn more about safe tenting. Right. Sorry. Just to clarify.

01:05:12.740 --> 01:05:16.770
When you say conversations with partners— sorry, which partners do

01:05:17.270 --> 01:05:19.050
you— are you referring to? Yes, great question. Like cities,

01:05:19.550 --> 01:05:23.089
townships, um, members of provincial parliament. Um, I'm just

01:05:23.589 --> 01:05:28.150
curious if you've had any conversations with any of those folks, because we

01:05:28.650 --> 01:05:32.350
need that, like, that partnership. Like, we need that okay or their land for this

01:05:32.850 --> 01:05:36.450
to work. And I— we've seen examples where municipalities put in

01:05:36.950 --> 01:05:40.210
safe tenting and it basically doesn't work because there's no— like, the policy is

01:05:40.710 --> 01:05:43.650
great, but it's kind of DOA, it just sort of sits there. So I'm just

01:05:44.150 --> 01:05:48.030
curious. Sure, yeah, I would say that I've had conversations on an advocacy

01:05:48.530 --> 01:05:52.850
level with other folks from other municipalities around

01:05:53.350 --> 01:05:56.889
the issue, but this is not my day job, so I have

01:05:57.389 --> 01:06:00.390
not had any sort of policy level discussions.

01:06:01.450 --> 01:06:04.970
But I certainly know from an advocacy perspective— yeah, yeah,

01:06:05.470 --> 01:06:09.570
from an advocacy perspective, yes, I certainly know that there Of course,

01:06:10.070 --> 01:06:14.410
as Joanna Mullins said, the co-creators table, I am sure there are—

01:06:14.910 --> 01:06:17.130
well, I know for a fact there are many people there who would be able

01:06:17.630 --> 01:06:21.770
to speak to their discussions at a policy level.

01:06:22.270 --> 01:06:25.570
Appreciate it. Thank you. Thank you. Thanks, Chair. Thanks, Megan. That was the only

01:06:26.070 --> 01:06:29.430
question. Next is Laura Pinn. Laura is joining us

01:06:29.930 --> 01:06:33.410
virtually, and it's from Hamilton. Laura, you have 5

01:06:33.910 --> 01:06:37.670
minutes and the floor is yours. Good morning,

01:06:38.170 --> 01:06:41.290
members of council. I'm sorry I can't be here in person today. With the changed

01:06:41.790 --> 01:06:45.709
date and time, I wasn't able to be here. In light of the recent Ontario

01:06:46.209 --> 01:06:50.130
Superior Court ruling concerning the encampment at 100 Victoria Street North,

01:06:50.630 --> 01:06:53.990
I'm delegating as the lead researcher on a project realizing the right

01:06:54.490 --> 01:06:57.810
municipal policy responses to encampments. This is a federally funded

01:06:58.310 --> 01:07:01.650
academic research project, and I'm delegating to share some findings

01:07:02.150 --> 01:07:05.770
from our work and urge the region to adopt a Safe Tenting

01:07:06.270 --> 01:07:10.170
Protocol Grounded in Human Rights Principles. This also is a follow-up

01:07:10.670 --> 01:07:14.190
to a letter sent to community council on June 2nd by

01:07:14.690 --> 01:07:18.230
myself, Dr. Erin Day, and Regan Brusay that includes links

01:07:18.730 --> 01:07:22.230
to the full report and executive summary. The project's really focused on

01:07:22.730 --> 01:07:26.510
taking high-level human rights principles and translating them into strategies for

01:07:27.010 --> 01:07:31.390
action to respond to encampments. It includes a policy scan of 38 municipalities

01:07:31.890 --> 01:07:35.040
in Ontario. As well as a survey of 105 people

01:07:35.540 --> 01:07:39.359
who have stayed in an encampment in the past 12 months, including 65

01:07:39.859 --> 01:07:43.610
people who have stayed in an encampment in Waterloo Region for 12 months.

01:07:46.190 --> 01:07:50.700
Municipal responses to encampments identifies 5

01:07:51.200 --> 01:07:54.220
core pillars of the human rights response: decentering policing— Laura,

01:07:54.720 --> 01:07:57.140
you're breaking up. Maybe do you want to turn your camera off and just see

01:07:57.640 --> 01:08:00.361
if it makes the audio less stilted.

01:08:02.526 --> 01:08:05.411
If you will just give me a second, I will do that, and I think

01:08:05.911 --> 01:08:08.457
I might be able to fix my connection. All right.

01:08:15.335 --> 01:08:17.066
Is this better? It is.

01:08:19.038 --> 01:08:22.565
So as I was saying, the report identified 5 core pillars of a human rights

01:08:23.065 --> 01:08:27.100
response based in the literature. Decentering policing and enforcement,

01:08:27.600 --> 01:08:31.239
addressing basic needs and conditions in encampments, ensuring equitable and accessible

01:08:31.739 --> 01:08:35.100
services, supporting meaningful participation of encampment residents

01:08:35.600 --> 01:08:39.259
in decision-making, and recognizing Indigenous rights.

01:08:39.419 --> 01:08:42.820
At the same time in the report, we identify several promising practices

01:08:43.320 --> 01:08:46.780
from municipalities across Ontario. For example, the City of London's

01:08:47.280 --> 01:08:50.400
Service Depot model provides one example of a more rights-aligned

01:08:50.900 --> 01:08:54.979
approach. Through mobile service delivery located directly near

01:08:55.479 --> 01:08:59.379
encampments, London's improved access to water, food, hygiene supplies,

01:08:59.879 --> 01:09:03.240
harm reduction support, washroom outreach services, and fire

01:09:03.740 --> 01:09:08.140
safety resources. This model is effective because it reduces transportation

01:09:08.640 --> 01:09:12.740
barriers and allows people to access supports without risking the

01:09:13.240 --> 01:09:17.540
loss or destruction of belongings. Similarly, municipalities including London,

01:09:18.040 --> 01:09:21.999
Thunder Bay, and Toronto have adopted policy language that explicitly recognizes that

01:09:22.499 --> 01:09:25.619
homelessness is not ilegal. Services must remain voluntary and

01:09:26.119 --> 01:09:29.879
grounded in self-determination. These approaches acknowledge that

01:09:30.379 --> 01:09:34.139
people cannot be forced into services or shelter arrangements that are unsafe,

01:09:34.639 --> 01:09:38.159
inaccessible, or inappropriate for them, aligned with some of

01:09:38.659 --> 01:09:40.989
the findings of Justice Gibson in the court case.

01:09:41.859 --> 01:09:45.139
A second component of this project, which is not yet published,

01:09:45.639 --> 01:09:48.559
is a survey of 105 people, as I mentioned, who have stayed in an encampment

01:09:49.059 --> 01:09:53.090
in the past year. There are many findings, but one that speaks particularly

01:09:53.122 --> 01:09:56.571
to the necessity of safe tenting is how harmful forced removal

01:09:57.071 --> 01:10:00.469
and displacement is. Most of the people we surveyed had experienced

01:10:00.501 --> 01:10:03.886
forced displacement. Some of the harms they experienced as

01:10:04.386 --> 01:10:08.009
a result: 75% experienced the loss or destruction of personal property,

01:10:08.666 --> 01:10:12.580
half lost connection to family or friends, just under

01:10:12.597 --> 01:10:15.949
half lost connection to health services and social services.

01:10:16.623 --> 01:10:20.331
Other people spoke about losing access to health cards, birth certificates,

01:10:20.476 --> 01:10:24.505
valuable items. Here are some specific

01:10:24.569 --> 01:10:28.630
comments that people left. Quote, they destroyed

01:10:28.727 --> 01:10:31.777
everything. Anything left behind was put in dump trucks.

01:10:33.639 --> 01:10:37.138
My belongings were bulldozed. I had no

01:10:37.170 --> 01:10:38.968
other place to go, left high and dry.

01:10:40.782 --> 01:10:43.559
I lost my phone, which meant connection to services and friends.

01:10:43.850 --> 01:10:46.660
My wife couldn't call me, which is very important.

01:10:48.499 --> 01:10:52.099
Outreach workers I had been talking to no longer knew where to find me.

01:10:53.760 --> 01:10:57.730
It was very disorienting and chaotic to handle the situation in the moment.

01:10:58.540 --> 01:11:02.160
I lost sleep, which led me to lose access to daytime social and

01:11:02.660 --> 01:11:06.960
health services. Personal items that were irreplaceable

01:11:07.460 --> 01:11:10.950
were destroyed, like family photos. We weren't allowed to go

01:11:11.450 --> 01:11:12.720
into our tents to recover items.

01:11:15.909 --> 01:11:19.270
It's important to note that when people are being displaced, they are not being displaced

01:11:19.770 --> 01:11:23.469
into housing or even emergency shelter. People are being displaced into further homelessness

01:11:23.969 --> 01:11:28.210
and vulnerability. In light of both the evolving legal landscape

01:11:28.710 --> 01:11:32.890
and the findings of this research, we urge the Region of Waterloo to take advantage

01:11:33.390 --> 01:11:36.870
of the excellent staff support that exists locally within housing services.

01:11:36.890 --> 01:11:40.450
Task that staff with developing and researching a safe tenting

01:11:40.950 --> 01:11:44.870
protocol in conjunction with the Co-Creators Table,

01:11:45.370 --> 01:11:48.530
a course of action that's already been supported by 1,800 community members

01:11:49.030 --> 01:11:51.710
through the petition. Those are all my comments.

01:11:53.610 --> 01:11:57.010
Thank you, Laura. We do have a question. Councilor Hunnicutt. Thank you, Laura, for coming

01:11:57.510 --> 01:12:00.930
today. Um, Laura, I believe that housing is a human right, and— but

01:12:01.430 --> 01:12:05.543
I don't believe that a parking lot should serve as a house. And so

01:12:05.639 --> 01:12:09.552
it surprises me that the court believes that safe

01:12:10.052 --> 01:12:13.128
tenting is good enough. Can you speak to how,

01:12:14.539 --> 01:12:18.051
like, the safety of tenting seems like an irony to

01:12:18.551 --> 01:12:22.237
me? Can you speak to why? Yeah, tenting is safe enough in your

01:12:22.737 --> 01:12:26.535
opinion? Yeah, absolutely. And, and safe tenting

01:12:26.567 --> 01:12:30.531
does not reach— meet the the standard for adequate

01:12:30.563 --> 01:12:34.000
housing, which is how housing as a human right is typically considered.

01:12:34.675 --> 01:12:38.738
The issue is that there is no housing

01:12:38.931 --> 01:12:42.882
for the number of people currently experiencing homelessness, and there are not enough

01:12:42.946 --> 01:12:46.480
emergency shelter spaces. And there are hundreds of people on the

01:12:46.980 --> 01:12:50.479
prioritization list for housing right now. A lot of the people we talk to

01:12:50.979 --> 01:12:53.113
who are staying in encampment, you know, many of them are not on the housing

01:12:53.145 --> 01:12:55.820
list, but a lot of them are. And they said, well, I was prioritized a

01:12:56.320 --> 01:12:59.400
few years ago. I don't know what's going on. And so in the context of

01:12:59.900 --> 01:13:03.780
a lot of bad choices, safe tenting is

01:13:04.280 --> 01:13:07.620
the least harmful for people. Uh, it enables people

01:13:08.120 --> 01:13:11.600
to survive. It enables people to connect with service providers, and it

01:13:12.100 --> 01:13:15.380
can serve as a connection to an exit from homelessness.

01:13:15.480 --> 01:13:19.340
But the fact is that in the immediate or even medium term, we do not

01:13:19.840 --> 01:13:23.715
have emergency shelter and housing options for the people experiencing homelessness in

01:13:23.747 --> 01:13:26.844
the region, and they are going to have to exist in public spaces.

01:13:27.502 --> 01:13:31.001
And when they are experiencing criminalization and forced removal,

01:13:31.515 --> 01:13:34.725
it really leads to a lot of harm for them, as was outlined in the

01:13:35.225 --> 01:13:37.678
court case, and I think is, as is supported by our research.

01:13:39.796 --> 01:13:41.947
Just to follow up, uh, to Redmond, if I may.

01:13:43.327 --> 01:13:46.890
Laura, thank you for your perspective here, and to all of the advocates here,

01:13:47.179 --> 01:13:50.294
um, I just I just want to encourage you that we want to

01:13:50.794 --> 01:13:54.436
have as much supportive housing and shelters as we need. And so if you could

01:13:54.565 --> 01:13:58.338
join the Region of Waterloo in advocating to the province for that, it would

01:13:58.838 --> 01:14:01.790
be greatly appreciated. Thanks,

01:14:02.272 --> 01:14:05.515
Laura. Those are all the questions we have. So thank you for delegating today.

01:14:11.408 --> 01:14:13.367
Laura? We may have lost Laura.

01:14:14.840 --> 01:14:18.160
Oh, Laura, are you still there? You're very welcome. I thought that was

01:14:18.660 --> 01:14:21.640
all the questions, so I was going to turn off my camera. Councillor Wolff,

01:14:22.140 --> 01:14:25.050
I guess, just didn't get in the queue quick enough. So go ahead, Councillor Wolff.

01:14:26.100 --> 01:14:29.439
Um, through you, Madam Chair, uh, thank you for the report, Laura. I wonder

01:14:29.939 --> 01:14:34.260
if you could share it with council, your research. Could you send us

01:14:34.760 --> 01:14:36.990
what you, what you just said?

01:14:38.440 --> 01:14:41.920
Yeah, absolutely. So that there are, uh, it is in

01:14:42.420 --> 01:14:46.000
the submission to community council from June 2nd, but I'd be happy to send directly

01:14:46.500 --> 01:14:50.159
to council. I will do so after this meeting. Thank you,

01:14:50.659 --> 01:14:54.420
Laura. Now I think it's safe to go. Thank you very much for delegating.

01:14:54.599 --> 01:14:58.120
That is all the questions. Uh, the next delegation is

01:14:58.620 --> 01:15:03.260
joining us with a pre-recorded video, and it's Craig Sloss from

01:15:03.760 --> 01:15:06.780
Waterloo. So, uh, please look at the monitor.

01:15:42.270 --> 01:15:45.860
We're experiencing some tech issues, so I think we will come back to

01:15:46.360 --> 01:15:49.770
Craig in the next— uh, oh, never mind, we're there.

01:16:34.989 --> 01:16:38.580
Okay, we're going to go to the next delegation and come back to Craig when

01:16:39.080 --> 01:16:42.700
we've resolved our tech issues. John Bannister, I believe you're here

01:16:43.200 --> 01:16:46.180
in person. Welcome to the podium, and you have 5 minutes.

01:16:46.940 --> 01:16:49.620
Chair Redmond, just a quick point of order. Do we have quorum?

01:16:50.540 --> 01:16:52.520
We do. We have several members online.

01:16:59.740 --> 01:17:03.320
Good morning, Council. I'm John Bannister.

01:17:03.710 --> 01:17:07.450
Other people know me as Chairman John, but I think today I'm the

01:17:07.950 --> 01:17:11.590
Vice President of the Southwest ONDP, for whatever that's

01:17:12.090 --> 01:17:15.470
worth. And I represent about 15,000

01:17:15.970 --> 01:17:19.890
members in all the Southwest. I think it's fair to say that this

01:17:20.390 --> 01:17:23.620
didn't go how you wanted it to to go.

01:17:24.120 --> 01:17:28.020
Let's rewind. From my perspective, of course, I'm a constituent. The region is

01:17:28.520 --> 01:17:31.739
tirelessly at work to find a humane solution to

01:17:32.239 --> 01:17:35.569
homelessness in our region, constantly looking at that compassionate solution.

01:17:36.300 --> 01:17:38.260
But you've sued the encampment.

01:17:41.260 --> 01:17:45.080
Cambridge has actually already cleared encampments. Actually, and just a

01:17:45.580 --> 01:17:48.920
note too, I find calls to join advocacy together

01:17:49.420 --> 01:17:53.290
really silly because you actually sit with mayors of

01:17:53.790 --> 01:17:56.480
the municipality. You actually work in the same space.

01:17:56.880 --> 01:18:00.030
Um, so I just find that a little absurd. Um,

01:18:00.530 --> 01:18:03.010
so I mean, what's the priority? It's not humane.

01:18:03.389 --> 01:18:07.170
Um, otherwise you would have done more. So it seems

01:18:07.670 --> 01:18:11.270
like the priority is a train stop or appeasing

01:18:11.770 --> 01:18:15.090
the province maybe, um, or sitting on the police board at the

01:18:15.590 --> 01:18:19.450
same time as being a councillor. So how can it be humane? People already

01:18:19.950 --> 01:18:23.920
know they're getting screwed, that police are there to protect private property,

01:18:23.930 --> 01:18:27.349
not the public. And actually, every bit of change

01:18:27.849 --> 01:18:30.490
has come from the public fighting you,

01:18:31.370 --> 01:18:35.250
fighting back against your position, which holds up the idea

01:18:35.750 --> 01:18:38.750
that exploitation should be first and profit second.

01:18:40.490 --> 01:18:43.990
We already know our government is helping Israel. We already know our

01:18:44.490 --> 01:18:48.180
government is helping ICE. At the provincial level, and our region actually hosts

01:18:48.680 --> 01:18:52.060
weapons manufacturers. So I

01:18:52.560 --> 01:18:56.120
just— coming to you as your constituent, um, I, I think,

01:18:56.620 --> 01:19:00.339
I think people are a little done. Um, so Ford abandoned

01:19:00.839 --> 01:19:04.060
you, right? You can't use the notwithstanding clause unless there's Parliament in session and

01:19:04.560 --> 01:19:08.060
the Legislative Assembly is raised. Um, so now you're just on your

01:19:08.560 --> 01:19:12.020
own, uh, with the knowledge that everyone knows you'd sell

01:19:12.520 --> 01:19:15.020
all of us out out for money or power.

01:19:16.440 --> 01:19:19.740
And it's— this isn't a democratic process either. If it was democratic,

01:19:20.240 --> 01:19:23.900
then we would all choose. All of us, we'd all get to say, not just

01:19:24.400 --> 01:19:28.159
me talking at you, we would actually be able to participate in change.

01:19:29.840 --> 01:19:33.340
And I mean, like, I get it, it's, it's an easy change.

01:19:33.840 --> 01:19:37.200
You know, you, you sit on the board and you pass the police budget and

01:19:37.700 --> 01:19:40.866
you get an extra $12K. It's easy, I get But

01:19:42.422 --> 01:19:46.384
I grew up here, and I know you have a lot of these people fooled.

01:19:47.570 --> 01:19:49.447
This is not a place of democracy at all.

01:19:51.901 --> 01:19:55.718
This is a place of brutish power and a really

01:19:55.814 --> 01:19:59.551
ugly place at that. So I refuse this electoral process that

01:19:59.599 --> 01:20:02.919
systemically allows you guys to make choices over us.

01:20:04.892 --> 01:20:07.218
I cannot be a part of that in any way, shape, or form. And the

01:20:07.718 --> 01:20:12.020
region is dictated by this council. When everyone knows you're just like us.

01:20:12.810 --> 01:20:16.569
And we already know you don't care. The system actually totally prevents

01:20:17.069 --> 01:20:20.930
you from caring. And you all won popularity contests,

01:20:21.009 --> 01:20:25.080
which gives you the power over life for others, and I'm meant to trust you,

01:20:25.590 --> 01:20:28.750
believe in you, over my neighbor, my school

01:20:29.250 --> 01:20:32.460
teachers? I should trust your police on my streets?

01:20:34.500 --> 01:20:37.900
When we can't afford to live, to eat, to shelter ourselves, I should

01:20:38.400 --> 01:20:41.759
depend on you? The government who polices,

01:20:42.259 --> 01:20:46.699
demonizes, and demeans the homeless, and that's not saying much when

01:20:47.199 --> 01:20:49.220
every ordinary person is already struggling.

01:20:49.609 --> 01:20:53.100
Units sitting empty because, again,

01:20:53.600 --> 01:20:56.110
the order is exploitation and then profit.

01:20:57.200 --> 01:21:00.060
But this is real life for us, you know, like, this is not a spin.

01:21:00.240 --> 01:21:02.640
I wish I was coming to you with some big narrative.

01:21:03.020 --> 01:21:06.339
It's not.

01:21:06.839 --> 01:21:10.420
And we're supposed to humanize you?

01:21:10.840 --> 01:21:14.140
If this was 300 years ago, we'd have pitchforks and torches, and I know you

01:21:14.640 --> 01:21:17.960
know that. What you've done,

01:21:18.460 --> 01:21:23.040
consider your legacy's cooked. You're just— it's done. It's over.

01:21:23.540 --> 01:21:27.040
You could have helped the people at the encampment. You didn't. You could have housed

01:21:27.540 --> 01:21:31.299
them. You didn't. You could have told the police to be more charitable. You didn't.

01:21:31.799 --> 01:21:36.060
You could have told the Barbara Collins security police patrol to not be there 24/7.

01:21:36.560 --> 01:21:40.060
You didn't. And I know I'm paying for that. That's my tax dollars

01:21:40.560 --> 01:21:43.660
at work, really. Because that's what

01:21:44.160 --> 01:21:47.400
I know this system is, that we all need to realize what this system is

01:21:47.900 --> 01:21:50.860
and that coming here to tell these people what we think is not just a

01:21:51.360 --> 01:21:53.860
waste of time, when the province has its thumb on the scale.

01:21:59.159 --> 01:22:02.240
We all need to get a little bit smarter about what we're doing here.

01:22:03.250 --> 01:22:06.210
Thank you. And I refuse your questions.

01:22:07.470 --> 01:22:11.630
Again, I'm gonna caution, no applause.

01:22:12.130 --> 01:22:14.910
And the next— do we have Craig Sloss up?

01:22:15.410 --> 01:22:17.770
So we will go back to the video that was provided.

01:22:19.930 --> 01:22:23.770
On at Central Station. The trip involves

01:22:24.270 --> 01:22:28.409
a lengthy walk from Central Station to the intercity rail station along a sidewalk

01:22:28.909 --> 01:22:31.930
that, at points, is unsafe and inaccessible,

01:22:32.430 --> 01:22:35.010
especially in winter or when carrying luggage for the trip.

01:22:35.910 --> 01:22:39.190
I, and many others, would benefit greatly from having a central transit

01:22:39.690 --> 01:22:43.370
hub that is near the ION station. The Region's transit

01:22:43.380 --> 01:22:46.420
Transit plans have frequently been cited as justification for

01:22:46.920 --> 01:22:50.899
the attempts to forcibly remove the residents at the Victoria Street encampment.

01:22:52.060 --> 01:22:56.450
The Region's current approach has twice been determined by courts to be unconstitutional.

01:22:58.060 --> 01:23:01.700
Establishing a safe tenting protocol is the only practical approach

01:23:02.200 --> 01:23:04.450
for allowing the Transit Hub project to proceed.

01:23:05.300 --> 01:23:08.880
This is not a situation in which we have to make a choice between

01:23:08.910 --> 01:23:12.700
supporting transit and supporting our constitutional rights.

01:23:12.770 --> 01:23:15.969
That is a false choice. On the contrary,

01:23:16.370 --> 01:23:19.750
this is a situation in which two problems have a common solution,

01:23:20.090 --> 01:23:22.840
and that solution is a safe tenting protocol.

01:23:23.410 --> 01:23:27.310
A safe tenting protocol is necessary for the obvious reason

01:23:27.810 --> 01:23:31.250
that people won't be able to leave the encampment unless they have another place to

01:23:31.750 --> 01:23:35.150
go. The ideal solution would be to ensure

01:23:35.650 --> 01:23:38.810
that that there is long-term stable housing for everyone in our community.

01:23:40.110 --> 01:23:43.690
Unfortunately, this doesn't look like it's happening anytime soon.

01:23:44.150 --> 01:23:47.630
It takes time to build new housing. A safe

01:23:48.130 --> 01:23:52.480
tenting protocol provides a short-term solution until housing becomes available.

01:23:53.510 --> 01:23:57.350
Framing the issue as a choice between a safe tenting protocol and permanent

01:23:57.850 --> 01:24:01.690
housing would be another false choice. We need the short-term

01:24:02.190 --> 01:24:05.470
solution to address the immediate problem while we wait for the long-term

01:24:05.970 --> 01:24:09.869
solution to be in place. We need both a safe tenting protocol

01:24:10.369 --> 01:24:14.210
and long-term stable housing. I am

01:24:14.710 --> 01:24:18.070
disappointed that 6 months ago, this council rejected a proposal

01:24:18.570 --> 01:24:22.870
from Councilor Wolff to direct staff to report on a safe tenting protocol.

01:24:23.310 --> 01:24:26.810
This motion failed on an 8-8 tie vote.

01:24:27.720 --> 01:24:30.390
A single vote could have changed the result.

01:24:31.240 --> 01:24:34.559
Every councillor who voted against the motion is responsible for its

01:24:35.059 --> 01:24:38.839
defeat. What bothers me most is that this wasn't

01:24:39.339 --> 01:24:43.100
yet a motion to adopt the bylaw. It was just a motion asking staff

01:24:43.600 --> 01:24:47.100
to look into it. It bothers me that you didn't even want

01:24:47.600 --> 01:24:51.140
to hear what the options were. Other cities like

01:24:51.640 --> 01:24:55.315
Thunder Bay, London, and Guelph have adopted various approaches to safe

01:24:55.348 --> 01:24:58.957
tenting. Why are we not looking at what other

01:24:59.457 --> 01:25:03.016
cities are doing successfully, evaluating their approaches, and adapting

01:25:03.049 --> 01:25:06.819
them to our needs? The Region has twice

01:25:07.319 --> 01:25:10.268
been told by courts that its current approach is unconstitutional.

01:25:11.215 --> 01:25:14.761
Council has twice passed unconstitutional bylaws that have done

01:25:14.841 --> 01:25:18.242
nothing but waste time and money as you attempt and fail

01:25:18.290 --> 01:25:19.847
to defend these approaches in court.

01:25:21.741 --> 01:25:25.335
You now have a direct instruction from a judge to implement a safe

01:25:25.835 --> 01:25:28.962
tenting protocol or provide an alternative encampment site.

01:25:30.487 --> 01:25:33.584
In the 6 months since council rejected the safe tenting motion,

01:25:33.921 --> 01:25:37.468
the situation has worsened to the point that we are now hearing extreme

01:25:37.968 --> 01:25:40.437
proposals like suspending constitutional rights.

01:25:41.945 --> 01:25:46.214
Adopting a safe tenting protocol would show respect for the law preserve

01:25:46.714 --> 01:25:50.432
our fundamental rights, show compassion for unhoused members of our community,

01:25:50.945 --> 01:25:54.939
and allow our transit plans to proceed. I urge council

01:25:55.439 --> 01:25:58.933
to reconsider its earlier decision and to adopt a safe tenting protocol as

01:25:58.965 --> 01:26:02.222
soon as possible. Thank you for taking the time

01:26:02.254 --> 01:26:03.681
to listen to my feedback today.

01:26:08.205 --> 01:26:12.680
So, colleagues, the next delegation is Lorraine— Lorraine Grenier

01:26:13.180 --> 01:26:17.100
from Kitchener. Lorraine, welcome to the podium, and you have 5 minutes.

01:26:25.910 --> 01:26:29.009
Good morning, Chairman Redmond. Good morning,

01:26:29.509 --> 01:26:33.050
Chairman Redmond and Council. I just want to clarify,

01:26:33.250 --> 01:26:37.410
I'm directing my deputation with regard to Victoria Street

01:26:37.910 --> 01:26:42.500
encampment. Radical social justice outbursts

01:26:43.000 --> 01:26:47.060
on social media attempting to discredit, humiliate, and shame

01:26:47.560 --> 01:26:50.990
Premier Ford and Regional Council is nonproductive.

01:26:51.660 --> 01:26:56.190
The negative outcome of such behavior is loss of credibility.

01:26:56.660 --> 01:27:00.490
The Premier and Regional Council do not hate homeless,

01:27:00.980 --> 01:27:05.260
disabled, and people with addiction. Moving forward,

01:27:05.760 --> 01:27:09.930
I'm speaking out loud. What people in this community whisper.

01:27:10.570 --> 01:27:14.090
