01:35:59.110 --> 01:36:02.470
heard $2 million. It's probably more. And what do we have to show for it?

01:36:02.970 --> 01:36:06.509
Did you like—

01:36:07.009 --> 01:36:10.330
yeah, we— you house some people. Okay. Like if I show up with every poached

01:36:10.830 --> 01:36:14.470
egg being hard when they're not like, okay, so you did

01:36:14.970 --> 01:36:18.650
a mediocre job and you got some people housed. Like, that's cool. But like,

01:36:19.460 --> 01:36:22.590
it's still full of people who don't have homes, right? So like,

01:36:23.090 --> 01:36:26.030
I don't know where the success story is, to be honest. And yeah, it sucks.

01:36:26.530 --> 01:36:29.350
You guys have a hard job. But like, you also haven't done a good job

01:36:29.850 --> 01:36:33.190
at it. So it's not an attack. Like, you guys take everything personal

01:36:33.690 --> 01:36:37.350
when it's actually just job performance reviews that you guys can't handle. And considering

01:36:37.850 --> 01:36:40.950
the age group of the council, it's bizarre that you all want participation ribbons.

01:36:41.510 --> 01:36:45.030
So, because I don't do that. So, Ryan, thank you very much. That was the

01:36:45.530 --> 01:36:52.310
only question we had. The next delegation is—

01:36:52.810 --> 01:36:56.340
so again, I'm going to caution that the applause stops or we'll ask you to

01:36:56.840 --> 01:37:01.240
leave the chamber. The next delegation

01:37:01.740 --> 01:37:05.279
is Simon Guthrie from Kitchener. Simon, welcome to the podium, and you have 5 minutes.

01:37:08.540 --> 01:37:11.880
Thank you, Chair and members of council. My name is Simon Guthrie,

01:37:12.380 --> 01:37:16.380
and I'm here today to speak about Justice Gibson's ruling on the 100 Victoria

01:37:16.880 --> 01:37:19.820
encampment. I'm asking Regional Council to make 4 commitments.

01:37:20.190 --> 01:37:23.680
First, do not appeal this ruling.

01:37:23.800 --> 01:37:26.760
Second, do not request, endorse,

01:37:27.260 --> 01:37:30.640
welcome, rely on, or participate in any provincial attempt to

01:37:31.140 --> 01:37:33.230
override the ruling through the notwithstanding clause.

01:37:33.800 --> 01:37:37.400
Third, begin immediate work with all municipalities on a safe,

01:37:37.900 --> 01:37:41.520
lawful tenting protocol. It's a necessary part of our collective response

01:37:42.020 --> 01:37:45.240
to homelessness. And fourth, move forward on the

01:37:45.740 --> 01:37:48.980
Kitchener Central Transit Hub in a way that respects the rights and dignity

01:37:49.480 --> 01:37:52.700
of the people living nearby. Justice Gibson's ruling

01:37:53.200 --> 01:37:57.440
is not the end of the story, because it can't be. Courts cannot solve homelessness,

01:37:57.940 --> 01:38:01.140
but they can remind governments that people have rights, and that's what this ruling

01:38:01.640 --> 01:38:05.340
does. Justice Gibson wrote, 

01:38:05.840 --> 01:38:09.370
us.

01:38:10.060 --> 01:38:13.880
People living without shelter are not a planning inconvenience. They're not a

01:38:14.380 --> 01:38:17.301
public relations problem. They are not obstacles to be moved out of sight when their

01:38:17.801 --> 01:38:21.669
presence becomes uncomfortable. They are people, they are neighbors, they are rights

01:38:21.717 --> 01:38:24.318
bearers. At the same time, I want to be clear,

01:38:25.008 --> 01:38:28.750
I support the Kitchener Central Transit Hub. Good transit matters.

01:38:29.247 --> 01:38:32.828
Climate responsible infrastructure matters. Connected, equitable,

01:38:32.876 --> 01:38:36.473
lower carbon communities matter. We need public infrastructure that

01:38:36.973 --> 01:38:39.989
helps people move through this region with dignity, efficiency, and fairness.

01:38:40.824 --> 01:38:44.356
But public infrastructure cannot be built by treating unhoused people as disposable.

01:38:44.918 --> 01:38:48.273
The Gibson ruling does not stop the hub. It tells Waterloo Region

01:38:48.354 --> 01:38:51.436
how to proceed. It tells us that we cannot use enforcement as

01:38:51.936 --> 01:38:55.386
a substitute for housing policy. It tells us that if government wants to restrict

01:38:55.434 --> 01:38:58.741
where people shelter, government must provide a lawful alternative.

01:38:59.431 --> 01:39:02.995
That means a safe tenting protocol. It means a lawful alternative site.

01:39:03.541 --> 01:39:06.479
It means meaningful implementation of the Plan to End Chronic Homelessness.

01:39:06.660 --> 01:39:10.730
And it means ending the fiction that displacement is the same thing as a solution.

01:39:11.520 --> 01:39:15.359
An appeal would not house anyone. It would not make the transit hub more just.

01:39:15.740 --> 01:39:19.640
It would not make our community safer. It would spend more public money trying to

01:39:20.140 --> 01:39:24.960
litigate our way out of a crisis that requires practical, rights-respecting leadership.

01:39:25.460 --> 01:39:28.900
Council should also be clear about the notwithstanding clause. I know that Regional Council

01:39:29.400 --> 01:39:33.330
cannot invoke Section 33 of the Charter, but the province can. And the Premier's

01:39:33.830 --> 01:39:37.070
first response to this ruling gave many people reason to worry that Queen's Park

01:39:37.570 --> 01:39:40.729
may be prepared to override rights rather than require governments to do the

01:39:41.229 --> 01:39:44.870
difficult work of creating lawful alternatives. Using the

01:39:45.370 --> 01:39:48.850
notwithstanding clause here would not solve homelessness. It would simply suspend the

01:39:49.350 --> 01:39:52.990
rights of people who are already among the least protected in our community.

01:39:53.490 --> 01:39:56.730
It would say that the life, security, and equality rights of unhoused people

01:39:57.230 --> 01:40:00.569
can be set aside when they become inconvenient. Regional Council

01:40:01.069 --> 01:40:04.882
should reject that path clearly and immediately. Council should say that Waterloo Region

01:40:04.979 --> 01:40:08.987
will not ask for such an override, will not endorse it, will not welcome it,

01:40:09.004 --> 01:40:12.467
and will not rely on it. This commitment applies not only to

01:40:12.967 --> 01:40:16.668
the Regional Council, but to the Region as a whole, including every regional agency,

01:40:17.182 --> 01:40:21.543
board, service, and affiliated body, such as the Waterloo Regional Police Service.

01:40:22.714 --> 01:40:26.145
There is also a local governance problem that this ruling has brought into sharper focus.

01:40:26.803 --> 01:40:30.557
The Region is responsible for homelessness services, but the cities and townships

01:40:30.573 --> 01:40:34.071
control much of the land and public space where any real response would

01:40:34.087 --> 01:40:37.472
have to happen. The mayors of those municipalities also sit at this

01:40:37.972 --> 01:40:41.564
table. That means no one can solve us this alone, but it

01:40:41.628 --> 01:40:44.820
also means no one should be allowed to point to another level of local government

01:40:45.173 --> 01:40:48.591
as an excuse for inaction. A safe tenting framework will

01:40:48.687 --> 01:40:52.940
only work if the Region, 3 cities, and 4 townships work together.

01:40:53.440 --> 01:40:57.119
The mayors should bring their councils together around a region-wide protocol rather than

01:40:57.619 --> 01:41:00.660
allowing responsibility to be passed back and forth while the crisis deepens.

01:41:01.439 --> 01:41:05.120
Other municipalities have begun this work. London has taken steps.

01:41:05.179 --> 01:41:08.940
Thunder Bay has taken steps. Chatham-Kent has taken steps.

01:41:09.200 --> 01:41:13.090
These are all single-tier municipalities, and Waterloo Region structure is different.

01:41:13.220 --> 01:41:16.620
But difference is not an excuse for analysis or for paralysis.

01:41:17.430 --> 01:41:21.110
In the worst framing, our two-tier structure has become an obstacle to both

01:41:21.610 --> 01:41:24.930
addressing homelessness and building the transit hub. In a more generous framing,

01:41:25.430 --> 01:41:29.020
it allows good people in different offices to wait for someone else to move first.

01:41:29.510 --> 01:41:32.990
Either way, the outcome is failure. I recently visited

01:41:33.490 --> 01:41:36.810
the Mi'kmaq Museum for Human Rights in Winnipeg. It's a powerful place

01:41:37.310 --> 01:41:40.390
to reflect on the gap between the rights we proclaim and the rights we are

01:41:40.890 --> 01:41:44.170
actually willing to honour. That's the test before Waterloo Region now.

01:41:44.360 --> 01:41:47.510
The question is not whether we uphold rights when it is

01:41:48.010 --> 01:41:52.309
easy. The question is how we uphold rights when doing so is difficult,

01:41:52.809 --> 01:41:56.370
inconvenient, and politically uncomfortable. Council has choices.

01:41:56.710 --> 01:42:00.890
You could appeal, you could delay, you could wait for Queen's Park to override

01:42:01.390 --> 01:42:04.690
rights. You could continue pretending that enforcement is a housing strategy.

01:42:04.950 --> 01:42:08.080
You could allow our municipal structure to be an excuse for inaction.

01:42:08.510 --> 01:42:12.170
Or you can do better. Accept the ruling, reject the notwithstanding clause,

01:42:12.180 --> 01:42:15.980
work together on a safe and lawful tenting protocol, implement the plan

01:42:16.480 --> 01:42:19.640
to end chronic homelessness, build the Kitchener Central Transit Hub.

01:42:20.140 --> 01:42:23.520
Thank you. Thank you, Simon. I see

01:42:24.020 --> 01:42:26.539
no further questions. Just for the audience's aware, I had to step into the chair

01:42:27.039 --> 01:42:30.640
as Chair Redmond had to step away for a moment. Next we

01:42:31.140 --> 01:42:34.320
will have Daisy Skye, Kitchener, you are virtual.

01:42:37.120 --> 01:42:39.940
Oh, it says you're virtual. You're in person? Okay.

01:42:53.159 --> 01:42:56.562
Thank you for coming in, Daisy. You have 5 minutes. Uh,

01:42:57.578 --> 01:43:00.625
thank you for, uh, taking your position in the chair on this, and thank you

01:43:01.125 --> 01:43:03.753
to the councillors for having me here today. Uh, my name is Skye Daisy.

01:43:03.835 --> 01:43:07.256
Uh, I want to start by saying

01:43:07.673 --> 01:43:11.273
I grew up in a community where we had

01:43:12.510 --> 01:43:16.688
30 years of legal challenge to our right to exist within

01:43:16.704 --> 01:43:20.191
our homes due to challenges around the ownership of

01:43:20.207 --> 01:43:23.855
the land. And so I am very familiar with the tensions that

01:43:24.355 --> 01:43:28.386
a community tolerates and the stresses

01:43:28.483 --> 01:43:30.395
that that can create for members.

01:43:31.776 --> 01:43:35.301
Having grown up in that community, I am also

01:43:35.801 --> 01:43:39.339
aware of the intense relationships that can develop

01:43:39.839 --> 01:43:43.409
in those communities and the importance of those for sustaining individuals who

01:43:43.909 --> 01:43:47.303
are struggling, as I was the child of a mother who struggled quite deeply

01:43:47.495 --> 01:43:49.706
and would never have survived without that support.

01:43:51.405 --> 01:43:54.930
I grew up and became a registered midwife, and I served in

01:43:54.946 --> 01:43:58.615
this community for 20 years, delivering many of the babies around

01:43:59.115 --> 01:44:02.660
this community, and even serving as the head midwife at Grand River Hospital,

01:44:03.160 --> 01:44:05.800
which at that time, for 4 years.

01:44:06.020 --> 01:44:09.139
I feel like I have contributed deeply to this community

01:44:09.639 --> 01:44:12.860
and grown to care an enormous amount for it.

01:44:13.360 --> 01:44:17.120
This battle is personal to me. I live in the downtown area.

01:44:17.620 --> 01:44:20.080
I grew up in the downtown of Toronto, but I live in the downtown of

01:44:20.580 --> 01:44:24.120
Kitchener. I'm old, so I've seen a lot of things come and go.

01:44:24.620 --> 01:44:27.810
Some of you might be old enough to have done that too. And as I

01:44:28.310 --> 01:44:32.210
retired from midwifery, I felt compelled enough around social justice

01:44:32.710 --> 01:44:36.380
issues to return to school and to get my Master's of Social Work.

01:44:36.870 --> 01:44:41.930
In that capacity, I became closely linked

01:44:42.430 --> 01:44:46.210
to individuals who were doing work at the encampment and became quite committed to

01:44:46.710 --> 01:44:50.070
both the individuals there and the work that the community was doing

01:44:50.570 --> 01:44:54.430
to support those individuals. I became aware of the role that

01:44:54.930 --> 01:44:57.469
policing, over-policing,

01:44:57.969 --> 01:45:02.369
over-scrutinizing by security,

01:45:02.869 --> 01:45:06.970
these were being used to seemingly

01:45:07.470 --> 01:45:10.730
be punitive in many ways for the residents. I'm well

01:45:11.230 --> 01:45:15.170
aware of the housing that was provided to many residents because

01:45:15.670 --> 01:45:19.150
many of those residents would return. And tell us about the inadequacy of the services

01:45:19.650 --> 01:45:23.670
that they were provided with. That led me to understand the

01:45:24.170 --> 01:45:27.589
degree to which those services were— and I'm going to use the word that's been

01:45:28.089 --> 01:45:32.130
said here a few times— performative. People were being offered housing that

01:45:32.630 --> 01:45:36.230
didn't meet their needs, and then they were returning to the encampment because they continued

01:45:36.730 --> 01:45:40.010
to need the social supports. They continued to need the community,

01:45:40.130 --> 01:45:43.530
and they continued to need a sense of belonging that they

01:45:44.030 --> 01:45:46.070
lost when they were fragmented into the larger community.

01:45:47.580 --> 01:45:51.519
I am aware that we are all sitting in a

01:45:52.019 --> 01:45:56.040
region that is located within a province and that this province is looking at

01:45:56.540 --> 01:45:59.579
more jail beds, putting 3 to a room,

01:46:00.660 --> 01:46:04.800
looking at reducing the options of bail, having more punitive sentences,

01:46:05.300 --> 01:46:08.860
and that we're all participating in this particular time

01:46:09.360 --> 01:46:12.850
at an increased criminalization of of poverty. I think we're aware

01:46:13.350 --> 01:46:16.490
that that targets people who are already vulnerable in ways

01:46:16.990 --> 01:46:20.830
that it may not touch our personal doorsteps, but it will

01:46:21.330 --> 01:46:24.190
touch many of those that we know, many of those that we care about,

01:46:24.210 --> 01:46:27.710
and it definitely touches this community. I am

01:46:28.210 --> 01:46:31.790
very concerned that this is not actively being

01:46:32.290 --> 01:46:35.220
acknowledged as a part of the context for this discussion.

01:46:37.338 --> 01:46:41.047
I live close enough to the downtown core that my routes

01:46:41.191 --> 01:46:43.905
of transit are regularly affected by building.

01:46:45.607 --> 01:46:50.135
I am well aware of the

01:46:50.424 --> 01:46:55.289
implications of shutting down Francis Street for the build at Francis

01:46:55.562 --> 01:46:58.838
and King, and that block has

01:46:58.870 --> 01:47:02.145
been there for months and months, possibly a year.

01:47:02.360 --> 01:47:05.970
You cannot tell me that there are not

01:47:06.470 --> 01:47:10.189
places where you could shut down a block of traffic and

01:47:10.689 --> 01:47:14.429
park a vehicle, materials, or other things of

01:47:14.929 --> 01:47:17.730
concern, because it's already done.

01:47:18.230 --> 01:47:21.440
It's done for corporate interests. It's done for expansion.

01:47:22.270 --> 01:47:25.710
This is a target, and the refusal to answer

01:47:26.210 --> 01:47:30.276
the call from the Ontario Supreme Court The refusal to

01:47:30.324 --> 01:47:34.058
be accountable to your jobs

01:47:34.875 --> 01:47:38.689
and to rise to the occasion and creatively think of solutions that

01:47:38.801 --> 01:47:41.926
will honor the dignity, the humanity, and the

01:47:42.426 --> 01:47:45.868
very most basic human needs of our community is actually

01:47:46.368 --> 01:47:49.906
an embarrassment. All I can say to you folks—

01:47:49.954 --> 01:47:53.399
and I see every single one of you here wanting to be seen for your

01:47:53.479 --> 01:47:56.650
own humanity in these seats. Is that if you

01:47:57.150 --> 01:48:00.249
are not actually feeling that there are

01:48:00.749 --> 01:48:04.340
options, then you aren't actually looking at it closely enough.

01:48:04.470 --> 01:48:08.170
And if it has to be the province, let it be the province.

01:48:08.710 --> 01:48:12.390
But I'm gonna tell you, all of this looks bad on you,

01:48:12.870 --> 01:48:16.910
because it does, right? It's just that simple.

01:48:16.950 --> 01:48:20.570
Pushing people out of their homes and saying you have no solutions, saying you have

01:48:21.070 --> 01:48:24.840
no options, options is not adequate. Thank you, Daisy. We have no questions.

01:48:25.050 --> 01:48:27.970
The next delegation is Angela White, Kitchener,

01:48:28.470 --> 01:48:31.970
and again, I think we have a pre-recorded video, so please watch the

01:48:32.470 --> 01:48:32.919
monitors.

01:48:37.550 --> 01:48:41.410
Hello, my name is Angela White. I would

01:48:41.910 --> 01:48:46.010
like to extend my thanks to council for allowing delegation today in this format.

01:48:46.170 --> 01:48:49.550
I have a number of health-related appointments that I was not able to reschedule

01:48:50.050 --> 01:48:51.901
on short notice. Otherwise, I would be there in person.

01:48:53.842 --> 01:48:57.196
I am a person who has lived in precarious housing for a number of years

01:48:57.229 --> 01:49:00.887
now due to being disabled. I worked and shared community

01:49:01.064 --> 01:49:04.418
in the region of Waterloo starting in 2007,

01:49:04.771 --> 01:49:08.462
including former employment at BlackBerry, and I have now lived here for

01:49:08.962 --> 01:49:12.634
2 years. I am telling you this so that you have an understanding of the

01:49:12.714 --> 01:49:15.202
depth of change I am talking to you about today.

01:49:16.405 --> 01:49:19.888
The fear I have that when I run out of even the precarious

01:49:20.080 --> 01:49:23.970
options I have been able to access I could be arrested,

01:49:24.470 --> 01:49:28.069
fined, or incarcerated has only been slightly allayed because of

01:49:28.569 --> 01:49:32.010
my knowledge of the encampment at 100 Vic.

01:49:32.510 --> 01:49:37.090
Justice Gibson described it as a miserable and desperate place,

01:49:37.590 --> 01:49:41.150
the only remaining safety valve for the region's homeless as a refuge

01:49:41.650 --> 01:49:45.230
of last resort, and knowledge that it exists

01:49:45.730 --> 01:49:48.030
has given me comfort.

01:49:48.430 --> 01:49:53.335
Please really take that in.

01:49:53.659 --> 01:49:57.320
You have been given an opportunity to do better for the people who,

01:49:57.820 --> 01:50:01.140
for whatever reason, are not currently able to live in the

01:50:01.640 --> 01:50:05.560
comfort and safety that you experience, that I used

01:50:06.060 --> 01:50:09.540
to experience before I became disabled in a way that doesn't allow me to live

01:50:10.040 --> 01:50:13.540
a conventional life, when I did not understand that most of

01:50:14.040 --> 01:50:18.148
us are only a handful of bad experiences away from losing

01:50:18.244 --> 01:50:19.912
that comfort and safety.

01:50:21.322 --> 01:50:24.881
Today, I am asking you to do what Justice Gibson is proposing:

01:50:25.538 --> 01:50:29.594
allow safe tenting and decriminalized camping. Please honor

01:50:29.626 --> 01:50:33.072
the plan to end chronic homelessness. This is not

01:50:33.153 --> 01:50:36.775
only about currently unhoused people. This is also about

01:50:37.275 --> 01:50:39.885
the people who will become unhoused in the near future.

01:50:40.050 --> 01:50:43.030
And who carry the same fears I do.

01:50:44.170 --> 01:50:47.470
Please take Justice Gibson's words to heart,

01:50:47.970 --> 01:50:51.730
that the homeless are not other. They are us. They are

01:50:52.230 --> 01:50:56.250
rights bearers of no less entitled than any other Canadian

01:50:56.750 --> 01:50:59.860
citizens to the full benefit and protection of the Charter.

01:51:01.030 --> 01:51:04.700
They are me. They are us.

01:51:05.730 --> 01:51:08.505
They are you.

01:51:09.700 --> 01:51:10.620
Thank you for your time.

01:51:13.379 --> 01:51:16.740
Thank you. We'll move on to the next delegation, is Jason Fitzgerald.

01:51:17.240 --> 01:51:18.660
Jason, I believe you're here in person.

01:51:20.960 --> 01:51:22.750
Jason is from Kitchener.

01:51:25.550 --> 01:51:28.610
We'll move on and maybe Jason will join us later.

01:51:28.880 --> 01:51:35.069
Um, 	 Suhanya Ketheeswaran

01:51:35.569 --> 01:51:39.090
from Cambridge, and I believe, Suhanya, you're joining

01:51:39.590 --> 01:51:42.000
us virtually. You have 5 minutes.

01:51:44.010 --> 01:51:47.490
Uh, Chair Redmond, I was wondering if I could actually ask an administrative question before

01:51:47.990 --> 01:51:48.880
I begin.

01:51:50.090 --> 01:51:53.630
So, thank you. I was working towards

01:51:54.130 --> 01:51:57.470
preparing a slide deck in time for 7 PM today because the meeting

01:51:57.970 --> 01:52:01.480
was originally scheduled for 7 PM tomorrow, and when I received a

01:52:01.980 --> 01:52:05.740
confirmation that I could delegate this morning, I worked through the night to prepare my

01:52:06.240 --> 01:52:11.700
slide deck in time for this meeting. Now, I understand that the clerk is—

01:52:12.200 --> 01:52:14.500
needs to abide by the rules that the slide deck needs to be shared 24

01:52:15.000 --> 01:52:18.480
hours in advance. The only reason you didn't have it 24 hours in advance is

01:52:18.980 --> 01:52:22.180
because the meeting was changed, and given the time and the effort that I made

01:52:22.680 --> 01:52:25.228
to not only be at the meeting today, but also to have a slide deck

01:52:25.728 --> 01:52:29.080
prepared. I would like to ask if I could share my screen, or if you

01:52:29.580 --> 01:52:32.915
can bump me further in the agenda, I am happy to stay on longer,

01:52:32.931 --> 01:52:36.125
but I would like to be able to use the presentation that I worked

01:52:36.625 --> 01:52:39.896
on. So, uh, I was aware of this issue, and I will

01:52:39.977 --> 01:52:43.443
ask for unanimous consent from my colleagues that the slide deck

01:52:43.507 --> 01:52:44.133
be shared.

01:52:47.295 --> 01:52:50.488
Okay, I see no, um, dissenting votes, so you may go

01:52:50.504 --> 01:52:52.655
ahead, and your slide deck will be put up.

01:52:55.520 --> 01:52:58.760
Wonderful. Thank you so much. And so I believe my time will start

01:52:59.260 --> 01:53:02.700
now. Yes.

01:53:03.200 --> 01:53:06.460
Okay. Thank you. So I am speaking to you today from Cambridge,

01:53:06.960 --> 01:53:09.270
Ontario, and want to thank you for the opportunity to delegate.

01:53:09.980 --> 01:53:13.260
I'm here to share some local perspectives on homeless

01:53:13.760 --> 01:53:16.000
encampments from the people that I love in my life.

01:53:16.240 --> 01:53:20.032
And this is ultimately a story about people coming together over shared

01:53:20.065 --> 01:53:22.199
values and longstanding bonds.

01:53:23.996 --> 01:53:27.672
So earlier this year, my family and I opened our home to our friends and

01:53:28.172 --> 01:53:31.555
family every Friday night in both January and February for soup

01:53:32.055 --> 01:53:36.081
and games. All we asked in return is that they bring a donation

01:53:36.274 --> 01:53:39.772
for the people sleeping outside in the bitter cold, under bridges,

01:53:39.885 --> 01:53:43.239
in parks, beside railway tracks, and in encampments.

01:53:44.683 --> 01:53:48.086
At the time, mayors across Ontario were asking the province to

01:53:48.150 --> 01:53:51.720
declare a state of emergency. The homeless population

01:53:52.220 --> 01:53:56.180
had increased 50% in 3 years to 85,000 people,

01:53:56.680 --> 01:54:00.120
of which 20,000 were children and youth.

01:54:00.620 --> 01:54:04.240
The same story was playing out at the local level in every community

01:54:04.740 --> 01:54:08.380
in Ontario. Here in Waterloo Region, the homeless population

01:54:08.880 --> 01:54:12.280
had actually more than doubled from 1,121

01:54:12.780 --> 01:54:17.770
to over 2,300. By 2024.

01:54:18.270 --> 01:54:21.929
To be completely honest, I wasn't sure what to expect when we sent

01:54:22.429 --> 01:54:25.770
our invite. Oh, I'm so sorry,

01:54:25.790 --> 01:54:29.440
I realize that I am supposed to be saying next slide.

01:54:29.510 --> 01:54:30.990
Can you go to the next slide, please?

01:54:33.090 --> 01:54:36.530
So this is a slide that shows the invite that went out to our friends

01:54:37.030 --> 01:54:40.050
about the tradition that we had started, and as I said, we invited them over

01:54:40.550 --> 01:54:43.860
for soup and games and asked asked that in return they bring a donation of

01:54:44.360 --> 01:54:47.220
winter supplies for those who were sleeping in the bitter cold. Next slide,

01:54:47.720 --> 01:54:48.220
please.

01:54:51.240 --> 01:54:54.540
At the time, mayors were asking the province to declare a state of emergency.

01:54:54.819 --> 01:54:58.220
This was based on the fact that homelessness had increased in Ontario by

01:54:58.720 --> 01:55:02.580
more than 50% in 3 years. 85,000 people were homeless

01:55:03.080 --> 01:55:06.300
as of the end of 2024. 20,000 of them were children

01:55:06.800 --> 01:55:10.590
and youth. And here in Waterloo Region, the number had actually more than doubled from

01:55:11.090 --> 01:55:14.649
1,100 in 2021 to 2,300 people or more

01:55:15.149 --> 01:55:16.610
in 2024. Next slide,

01:55:17.110 --> 01:55:21.210
please. To be completely honest, I wasn't sure what to expect

01:55:21.710 --> 01:55:25.010
when we sent our invite. Homelessness, encampments, and the

01:55:25.510 --> 01:55:28.270
idea of public camping had become polarizing topics.

01:55:28.650 --> 01:55:31.720
But our friends met the moment with overwhelming generosity.

01:55:32.340 --> 01:55:35.920
Week after week, they arrived with donations by the

01:55:36.420 --> 01:55:38.649
bagful. Next slide, please.

01:55:39.639 --> 01:55:43.240
In many societies, we reward this kind of generosity

01:55:43.740 --> 01:55:47.600
with praise, recognition, awards, but it's worth

01:55:48.100 --> 01:55:51.940
asking the question: when everyday folks are gathering survival

01:55:52.440 --> 01:55:55.700
supplies so that the people outside don't freeze to death,

01:55:56.200 --> 01:55:59.328
is that an example of Waterloo Region's barn raisers spirit,

01:56:00.211 --> 01:56:03.952
or are we forcing people with a strong moral compass to fill

01:56:04.081 --> 01:56:06.473
the gaps that are being left by our governments?

01:56:07.774 --> 01:56:11.900
Next slide, please. Seen here

01:56:12.414 --> 01:56:16.123
are VPs and retirees, business owners and public

01:56:16.623 --> 01:56:18.981
sector employees, landlords and tenants,

01:56:19.495 --> 01:56:22.273
moms, dads, aunts, uncles, cousins,

01:56:22.931 --> 01:56:25.960
each with their own politics each with their own viewpoints.

01:56:27.640 --> 01:56:30.760
And while our friends who showed up to the soup nights are thoughtful, caring,

01:56:31.260 --> 01:56:35.980
and compassionate, they don't all share the same perspective on encampments.

01:56:36.200 --> 01:56:39.960
And yet they all seem to agree on one thing:

01:56:40.460 --> 01:56:44.300
something needs to be done about the rising homelessness in our communities,

01:56:44.800 --> 01:56:48.140
not just because the reports and the statistics say so, but because we

01:56:48.640 --> 01:56:52.310
could see with our own eyes that the issue is getting worse with each year

01:56:52.810 --> 01:56:55.290
that goes by. Next slide, please.

01:56:56.889 --> 01:56:59.410
I understand that when it comes to the 100 Vic encampment,

01:56:59.770 --> 01:57:03.290
Council has been trying to determine and working on the

01:57:03.790 --> 01:57:07.170
best way forward for more than 3 years now. Today's meeting is

01:57:07.670 --> 01:57:11.090
an important one. You've invited us here to share our thoughts on your next steps,

01:57:11.410 --> 01:57:14.830
so I posed a question to those in our group who are residents of Waterloo

01:57:15.330 --> 01:57:18.650
Region and therefore your constituents. I asked them,

01:57:19.150 --> 01:57:22.250
given that a court told the Region in January 2023 and again in

01:57:22.750 --> 01:57:26.609
May 2026 that homeless residents at the Hundred Vic encampment cannot

01:57:27.109 --> 01:57:30.910
be evicted because doing so in the absence of adequate accessible

01:57:31.410 --> 01:57:35.290
shelter space is a violation of their Charter rights, and given

01:57:35.790 --> 01:57:39.610
that as of June 2026 the number of shelter beds across the Region is

01:57:40.110 --> 01:57:43.350
still less than half of what's needed, I actually think this number is

01:57:43.850 --> 01:57:47.989
an underestimate for the 1,400+ people who are currently homeless, and the

01:57:48.489 --> 01:57:51.640
wait time for subsidized housing is approximately 10 years.

01:57:51.890 --> 01:57:54.650
What do you want to say to that, or what do you want council to

01:57:55.150 --> 01:57:58.590
know, and what do you want to ask them? I promised them that their responses

01:57:59.090 --> 01:58:03.210
would be anonymous, and like I shared before, their politics vary and

01:58:03.710 --> 01:58:06.400
their viewpoints vary, but I'd like to share with you what they said.

01:58:06.570 --> 01:58:09.970
I care about making sure people aren't left living on the streets.

01:58:09.990 --> 01:58:13.630
My question to council would be, what is the plan to

01:58:14.130 --> 01:58:17.610
increase support, shelter, and affordable housing so we

01:58:18.110 --> 01:58:21.670
can create long-term solutions? I didn't

01:58:22.170 --> 01:58:25.439
know that the number of homeless people is so astonishingly high in the region.

01:58:25.570 --> 01:58:28.930
That's really sad and shocking. Why do you

01:58:29.430 --> 01:58:32.750
value human life so low? I would want

01:58:33.250 --> 01:58:36.590
to know why they have waited so long to get more beds and shelters set

01:58:37.090 --> 01:58:40.080
up if they have known for 3 years that they need them in order to

01:58:40.580 --> 01:58:44.200
address the encampment issues. I would also want to know what system do they have

01:58:44.700 --> 01:58:48.360
in place to make sure no one is abusing the subsidized housing and what

01:58:48.860 --> 01:58:52.220
plans they have to get that waitlist down to a reasonable number.

01:58:53.640 --> 01:58:57.580
Based on the data of subsidized housing being almost a 10-year wait and

01:58:58.080 --> 01:59:01.860
only less than half the beds being available, it's important to tackle the root

01:59:02.360 --> 01:59:06.230
problem and look into case studies of other regions in Ontario and what requires

01:59:06.730 --> 01:59:10.209
iteration and what has worked. Souhanya, I gave you

01:59:10.709 --> 01:59:13.370
a little bit extra time because it was a slow start. We didn't start the

01:59:13.870 --> 01:59:16.229
timer until you started talking, so if you can sum up now, that would be

01:59:16.729 --> 01:59:20.030
great. Sure. Maybe I'll just pick one more quote.

01:59:21.150 --> 01:59:24.630
Um, well, the first thing that comes to my mind is, 

01:59:25.130 --> 01:59:28.656
government needs time to make change, but what is being done towards that direction?

01:59:28.753 --> 01:59:31.766
what are the smallest changes government is doing for the larger goal,

01:59:32.279 --> 01:59:35.726
the smallest changes that are impactful and visible? If this

01:59:36.226 --> 01:59:40.295
is the current problem, how does the government prepare for future onset of

01:59:40.795 --> 01:59:44.591
recession in our Canadian economy? So to wrap up, next slide,

01:59:44.639 --> 01:59:46.355
please, or final slide, please.

01:59:48.952 --> 01:59:52.398
This is a story, as I said at the beginning, of people coming together over

01:59:52.898 --> 01:59:56.410
shared values and longstanding bonds, of people being willing to bring

01:59:56.910 --> 02:00:01.449
both compassion and curiosity to an issue that is as devastating as

02:00:01.949 --> 02:00:05.130
it is frustrating. I hope Council has found it helpful to see how

02:00:05.630 --> 02:00:09.189
people in your community are coming together to meet the moment and to

02:00:09.689 --> 02:00:13.210
fill gaps that shouldn't exist in a city, a province, and a

02:00:13.710 --> 02:00:16.910
country as prosperous as ours. We hope that you too will meet the

02:00:17.410 --> 02:00:20.650
moment as you decide on your next steps. Thank you so much for your time.

02:00:20.700 --> 02:00:24.200
For the accommodation around the slide deck and the additional few seconds that

02:00:24.700 --> 02:00:27.589
you gave me at the end, Chair Redmond. Thank you. I have no questions.

02:00:27.640 --> 02:00:31.640
The next delegation is Vanessa Haynes-Denotter from

02:00:32.140 --> 02:00:35.700
Kitchener, and again, it's a pre-recorded delegation, so please

02:00:36.200 --> 02:00:36.950
watch the monitors.

02:00:43.560 --> 02:00:47.360
Hi, it's Vanessa Haynes-Denotter, hoping that

02:00:47.860 --> 02:00:51.510
we can secure a place to stay. Um,

02:00:52.010 --> 02:00:56.459
I believe that anyone and anyone in the community that's homeless deserves a chance.

02:00:56.710 --> 02:01:00.120
So, uh, here's to the best. Uh, fingers crossed.

02:01:00.290 --> 02:01:03.870
Thank you.

02:01:04.380 --> 02:01:06.860
Thank you very much. That was brief.

02:01:07.310 --> 02:01:10.750
Um, the next delegation is Julian Ickham. Julian,

02:01:11.250 --> 02:01:13.760
you're here in person from Kitchener, and you have 5 minutes.

02:01:20.290 --> 02:01:24.160
I'd like to start by talking about the Nuremberg

02:01:24.660 --> 02:01:28.840
Principle. Now, the Nuremberg Principle happened after World War

02:01:29.340 --> 02:01:32.600
II as a result of the genocide of

02:01:33.100 --> 02:01:36.480
communists, Jews, Romanies, and many

02:01:36.980 --> 02:01:40.570
others. The idea behind the Nuremberg Principle is

02:01:41.070 --> 02:01:44.870
that you can't just say, 

02:01:45.370 --> 02:01:49.390
can't just allow genocide to happen

02:01:49.890 --> 02:01:53.230
without doing nothing, that you yourself are

02:01:53.730 --> 02:01:57.490
responsible for what is happening. And I would argue that there

02:01:57.990 --> 02:02:01.450
is a genocide of poor people happening

02:02:01.950 --> 02:02:05.190
in Kitchener-Waterloo. When we're having 10

02:02:05.690 --> 02:02:09.340
to 30 overdoses a day as a result

02:02:09.840 --> 02:02:13.939
of the shutting down of the safe injection site.

02:02:14.439 --> 02:02:18.760
When we're having people being murdered and raped on

02:02:19.260 --> 02:02:23.100
the streets of Kitchener, as happened 2

02:02:23.600 --> 02:02:26.760
weeks ago to the woman many of us know with the dog

02:02:27.260 --> 02:02:31.090
that hangs out outside of Shoppers, and nothing is being done.

02:02:31.300 --> 02:02:35.210
When we're having people freezing or

02:02:35.710 --> 02:02:39.650
dying because of lack of access to medical services.

02:02:40.150 --> 02:02:43.860
I say that there is a class war going on and a genocide going on,

02:02:44.090 --> 02:02:47.809
and this genocide is happening towards poor

02:02:48.309 --> 02:02:52.010
people. It's— I find it funny that

02:02:52.510 --> 02:02:56.490
we're coming to council and begging you or asking

02:02:56.990 --> 02:03:00.642
you or thanking you to enact a court

02:03:01.142 --> 02:03:05.232
ruling by a judge. This is a

02:03:05.732 --> 02:03:11.235
federal decision, and once again, you are trying to

02:03:11.251 --> 02:03:14.990
back out of this by putting the blame on

02:03:15.183 --> 02:03:18.794
Doug Ford, not challenging the notwithstanding

02:03:18.874 --> 02:03:23.095
clause, and allowing human rights to be violated.

02:03:23.512 --> 02:03:28.020
What are human rights? Human rights are something we have because

02:03:28.520 --> 02:03:32.180
we are human. It's not something that we need

02:03:32.680 --> 02:03:36.400
to, like, work to merit. It's not a privilege.

02:03:37.060 --> 02:03:40.880
Simply because we are born as humans, we have basic

02:03:41.380 --> 02:03:45.260
rights. That includes the right to shelter fit

02:03:45.760 --> 02:03:50.160
for human beings. That includes the right not to be criminalized

02:03:50.660 --> 02:03:54.780
when we are denied shelter and are forced to create our

02:03:55.280 --> 02:03:59.099
own encampments. People don't sleep in encampments

02:03:59.599 --> 02:04:03.190
because, you know, they're a great place.

02:04:03.380 --> 02:04:06.660
They sleep at encampments because within the

02:04:07.160 --> 02:04:11.100
shelter system there is violence. Within the shelter

02:04:11.600 --> 02:04:15.300
system there is no sense of community

02:04:15.800 --> 02:04:19.540
and people are being attacked, people are being robbed,

02:04:19.857 --> 02:04:23.389
and people are being beaten in their sleep,

02:04:24.112 --> 02:04:29.362
while we see a constant— while

02:04:29.410 --> 02:04:33.633
we see a constant criminal

02:04:35.367 --> 02:04:38.643
turning a blind eye to these things by shelter

02:04:39.143 --> 02:04:42.786
workers, many of them who abuse

02:04:42.978 --> 02:04:46.230
the rights of the people that they're

02:04:46.730 --> 02:04:49.640
supposed to be served. Now, in terms of,

02:04:49.909 --> 02:04:53.410
you know, how much money we've spent on

02:04:53.910 --> 02:04:56.720
homelessness, I was part of the PIT count.

02:04:57.410 --> 02:05:00.950
And many people from the PIT count that we

02:05:01.450 --> 02:05:05.150
were counting, we weren't allowed to include because the

02:05:05.650 --> 02:05:09.390
question was, on this day, do you have a place to

02:05:09.890 --> 02:05:12.630
stay? If they're staying at a friend's or something,

02:05:13.100 --> 02:05:16.989
then, you know, the thing was ended. But despite this,

02:05:17.489 --> 02:05:20.670
the PIT count doubled, so the amount of homeless people

02:05:21.170 --> 02:05:25.089
doubled. You have a responsibility to all

02:05:25.589 --> 02:05:28.410
people who live in Kitchener-Waterloo,

02:05:28.910 --> 02:05:33.020
not just those with money. By reneging this responsibility,

02:05:33.930 --> 02:05:37.810
you are in violation of the Nuremberg

02:05:38.310 --> 02:05:44.050
Principles, and you are participating in this genocide.

02:05:44.550 --> 02:05:47.889
I'd like to give two examples, Julius Streicher and

02:05:48.389 --> 02:05:51.869
Eichmann. Eichmann didn't kill Jewish people,

02:05:52.010 --> 02:05:55.289
he didn't kill Romas. What he did was he built tracks, he built

02:05:55.789 --> 02:05:59.710
railroad tracks. But because these railroad tracks were part

02:06:00.210 --> 02:06:04.510
of the Holocaust, Eichmann was indicted.

02:06:05.010 --> 02:06:09.274
Julius Streicher was a newspaper writer. He wrote fascist

02:06:09.774 --> 02:06:14.306
stuff, and the way in which he talked about

02:06:14.806 --> 02:06:19.033
Romas, the way in which he talked about communists and Jews created

02:06:19.533 --> 02:06:22.623
the atmosphere that led to the genocide.

02:06:25.042 --> 02:06:29.529
Regional Council is creating an atmosphere that

02:06:30.029 --> 02:06:33.199
is continuing to allow this genocide to happen.

02:06:34.034 --> 02:06:37.534
By actually taking

02:06:38.192 --> 02:06:42.993
like the first court decision to court by saying

02:06:43.652 --> 02:06:47.890
that, you know, we have done enough by allowing

02:06:49.030 --> 02:06:54.409
the Ford government to take

02:06:54.909 --> 02:06:57.846
away our rights by the notwithstanding clause.

02:06:59.070 --> 02:07:02.610
You are allowing this to happen and you have a

02:07:03.110 --> 02:07:06.510
moral responsibility. Julian, you're out of time. If you want to,

02:07:07.010 --> 02:07:10.750
one final statement. Yeah. What will history say

02:07:11.250 --> 02:07:14.910
about this council 10, 20 years from now when

02:07:15.410 --> 02:07:18.810
we have the new Nuremberg trials? Thank you, Julian.

02:07:19.310 --> 02:07:23.290
I have no questions. The next delegation is Kenyava

02:07:23.790 --> 02:07:27.740
Demiri. And you're in person.

02:07:30.739 --> 02:07:33.280
So welcome to the podium, and you have 5 minutes.

02:07:34.260 --> 02:07:38.020
Hello, my name is Tanavya Dimri. I'm a computer science graduate

02:07:38.520 --> 02:07:42.220
from the University of Waterloo. I'm speaking today to request you to pass a

02:07:42.720 --> 02:07:46.560
safe tenting bylaw that would give the over 2,000 homeless people in

02:07:47.060 --> 02:07:49.820
the region a last resort with some dignity and support.

02:07:50.130 --> 02:07:53.970
It is the responsibility of not just the community, who has

02:07:54.470 --> 02:07:58.130
been spending so much of their own time and effort in mutual aid, to provide

02:07:58.630 --> 02:08:03.030
the basic essential needs of vulnerable people. While it's

02:08:03.530 --> 02:08:07.590
not just the responsibility of us, but it is the responsibility of you as well.

02:08:08.090 --> 02:08:11.450
While there is a lot of talk from a legal point of view about protecting

02:08:11.950 --> 02:08:16.050
human rights, we simply cannot expect poverty-stricken

02:08:16.550 --> 02:08:20.122
people who have no support to be able to function properly in society

02:08:20.235 --> 02:08:24.377
without their material needs being met. Every one of us has the privilege

02:08:24.505 --> 02:08:27.251
of support from family and friends. Then,

02:08:29.225 --> 02:08:32.565
not just the privilege of support from family and friends,

02:08:32.741 --> 02:08:37.076
but also food, clothing, and perhaps the most important guarantee

02:08:37.576 --> 02:08:41.315
of all in our current society, a roof above our heads. Before we can

02:08:41.347 --> 02:08:45.070
go out and get educated, find a job, and be successful.

02:08:45.480 --> 02:08:48.860
However, people at the bottom of society do not have that

02:08:49.360 --> 02:08:52.500
privilege. I come from India, a country where I've seen

02:08:53.000 --> 02:08:56.380
firsthand even more rampant poverty and inequality, where people

02:08:56.880 --> 02:09:00.460
slip through the cracks every single day. Do we want the

02:09:00.960 --> 02:09:04.940
same situation here too? To solve homelessness, the solution cannot be

02:09:05.440 --> 02:09:09.721
to get rid of these people. We cannot take away their last resort.

02:09:11.212 --> 02:09:14.275
If we just ignore that these people exist,

02:09:14.355 --> 02:09:16.616
that does not mean they will cease to exist.

02:09:17.883 --> 02:09:21.299
We need to find a way to provide these

02:09:21.331 --> 02:09:25.003
people with a last resort. If we do not do that,

02:09:25.324 --> 02:09:28.836
they will find a way to survive, but it will only make it orders of

02:09:28.852 --> 02:09:32.508
magnitude harder to do so. Possibly some of them will die,

02:09:33.118 --> 02:09:36.800
especially as the next winter comes around. Is that what we want?

02:09:38.030 --> 02:09:41.730
As council members and our representatives,

02:09:42.230 --> 02:09:45.660
I hope that you do not want this. I do not want this.

02:09:46.110 --> 02:09:49.770
I want to fight up— fight back for their dignity and for

02:09:50.270 --> 02:09:54.150
their livelihood. Please take seriously all the research done

02:09:54.650 --> 02:09:58.710
and put into the plan to end chronic homelessness and invest resources

02:09:59.210 --> 02:10:02.560
for a long-term solution that aims to end homelessness rather than

02:10:03.060 --> 02:10:06.319
just treat the symptoms. However, as a short-term solution for harm

02:10:06.819 --> 02:10:10.240
reduction and to save lives, please pass a safe tenting bylaw

02:10:10.740 --> 02:10:14.700
so we can have a decriminalized camping site with support systems in place.

02:10:14.800 --> 02:10:18.140
Lastly, please stand up to Premier Ford and his threats to

02:10:18.640 --> 02:10:22.640
use the notwithstanding clause to forcibly clear out the encampment. That would

02:10:23.140 --> 02:10:26.520
only lead to tragedy— that would not only lead to tragedy but

02:10:27.020 --> 02:10:30.300
set up the wrong precedent for further violations in the future.

02:10:30.540 --> 02:10:34.340
Thank you. Thank you very much. I have no questions,

02:10:34.840 --> 02:10:38.560
so thank you for delegating. The next delegation is Lynn Intini

02:10:39.060 --> 02:10:42.060
from Kitchener. And Lynn, I believe you're here in person.

02:10:42.760 --> 02:10:44.800
You have 5 minutes and the floor is yours.

02:10:48.000 --> 02:10:51.840
Hello. I've previously been told there is no need

02:10:52.340 --> 02:10:56.260
to investigate any aspect of encampment evictions in Cambridge because there's

02:10:56.760 --> 02:11:00.379
shelter spaces. This was during discussions about how

02:11:00.879 --> 02:11:05.180
safe it is to evict people in extreme weather, which we were told doesn't

02:11:05.680 --> 02:11:09.460
happen. This was said during a meeting which delegates could not attend in

02:11:09.960 --> 02:11:14.140
person for the safety of council, a situation which lasted for weeks.

02:11:14.280 --> 02:11:17.190
Many of us disagreed vehemently, but we were told,

02:11:17.420 --> 02:11:21.060
reports say that there's shelter spaces available, there's places to

02:11:21.560 --> 02:11:25.390
go. In reading evidence provided to

02:11:25.890 --> 02:11:29.810
Justin Gibson, I think I found part of the confusion. Shelter occupants—

02:11:30.310 --> 02:11:34.010
and I'm quoting— shelter occupancy numbers are not 100% accurate.

02:11:34.510 --> 02:11:37.750
Shelter beds are counted as full or empty in the morning, and shelters have curfew

02:11:38.250 --> 02:11:41.290
times. If a person accessing shelter does not show up before curfew

02:11:41.790 --> 02:11:44.990
time, that bed would be counted as empty, even though staff

02:11:45.490 --> 02:11:49.560
would not give the bed to anyone who was staying for a few days.

02:11:51.100 --> 02:11:54.460
Is this in addition to allegedly empty beds that were not

02:11:54.960 --> 02:11:58.760
available during shelter renovations? Is this where the allegedly empty beds are

02:11:59.260 --> 02:12:00.650
coming from that people are supposed to go to?

02:12:02.560 --> 02:12:05.760
Of the 24/7 shelters where you don't have to leave during the

02:12:06.260 --> 02:12:09.720
day, 20 out of 200 are for women and gender

02:12:10.220 --> 02:12:14.100
diverse people. They are always full. When there's not shelter, these folks sleep

02:12:14.600 --> 02:12:18.040
rough in remote locations to avoid detection. Some resume unsafe,

02:12:18.540 --> 02:12:22.640
exploitative relationships and exchange sex for shelter, posing particular

02:12:23.140 --> 02:12:26.340
safety concerns for women and gender-diverse folks.

02:12:26.380 --> 02:12:30.260
But this is much like one of the options the Regis generously

02:12:30.760 --> 02:12:34.680
offered. During cross-examination, Commissioner Sweeney admitted

02:12:35.180 --> 02:12:38.820
the region no longer places women or gender-diverse people

02:12:39.320 --> 02:12:43.300
at one of the four motels due to reports of sexual coercion and exploitation

02:12:43.800 --> 02:12:45.780
by motel staff under threat of eviction.

02:12:46.610 --> 02:12:50.310
Thank you for no longer sending women into

02:12:50.810 --> 02:12:54.730
trafficking, but this does not solve the problem. Trafficking like

02:12:55.230 --> 02:12:58.509
this is a criminal act, and yet what criminal charges will this motel

02:12:59.009 --> 02:13:02.430
owner face? It seems this criminal is still being paid

02:13:02.930 --> 02:13:06.590
by the region. The region's told Justice Gibson

02:13:07.090 --> 02:13:10.650
they are not prepared to decriminalize survival camping somewhere else,

02:13:11.150 --> 02:13:14.870
so folks tenting will be more likely to face carceral punishment

02:13:15.370 --> 02:13:16.910
for their survival. Than a human trafficker.

02:13:18.950 --> 02:13:22.150
Bylaw amendments were made to improve compliance with human rights,

02:13:22.650 --> 02:13:25.570
including best efforts to create, uh,

02:13:26.070 --> 02:13:29.970
IHPs, which the region describes as personalized plans designed to match individuals

02:13:30.470 --> 02:13:33.340
with housing options reflecting their specific needs and preferences.

02:13:33.450 --> 02:13:37.070
However, Justice Gibson notes that 3 IHPs on record don't

02:13:37.570 --> 02:13:40.870
support that contention. 2 offer emergency shelter spaces, one of which

02:13:41.370 --> 02:13:44.740
the recipient could not accept because the spaces did not accommodate

02:13:45.240 --> 02:13:47.980
couples. The third offered a motel space.

02:13:49.439 --> 02:13:52.620
All three are undated, consist of only a few lines, and offer no information

02:13:53.120 --> 02:13:56.160
as to what the unnamed recipient's specific needs and preferences are.

02:13:56.660 --> 02:14:00.840
Impressive. But the Region's lawyers claimed it doesn't have to be perfect

02:14:01.340 --> 02:14:03.350
as long as it's Charter compliant.

02:14:03.980 --> 02:14:07.740
Continuing with, if it's found to fall short, the Region seeks a

02:14:08.240 --> 02:14:11.930
realistic roadmap from the court as to any further steps it must take.

02:14:12.811 --> 02:14:16.178
So Justice Gibson has now affirmed for you to follow the

02:14:16.678 --> 02:14:20.763
Pesh recommendation of safe tenting. Why did the lawyers repeatedly

02:14:21.263 --> 02:14:24.771
make this offer? Please surprise me— us, surprise us

02:14:25.271 --> 02:14:30.766
all and implement safe tenting. The premier has continued the

02:14:31.266 --> 02:14:34.598
existing path of not funding community supports, only promises of

02:14:34.630 --> 02:14:38.260
jail cells. Punitive and divisive rhetoric

02:14:38.760 --> 02:14:42.660
of deserving and undeserving. If you

02:14:43.160 --> 02:14:46.699
choose not to enable decriminalized camping protocols, you risk

02:14:47.199 --> 02:14:51.080
a legal precedent of a last resort from

02:14:51.580 --> 02:14:55.280
being a thing. This is about a last resort

02:14:55.780 --> 02:14:59.460
to prevent folks from being criminalized, and you're asking

02:14:59.960 --> 02:15:03.250
us to advocate to the province A man who throws a temper tantrum when a

02:15:03.750 --> 02:15:07.209
Superior Court judge doesn't do as he wants and demands party members

02:15:07.709 --> 02:15:11.370
vote with the party or be dismissed. Why aren't you doing everything

02:15:11.870 --> 02:15:14.690
you can possibly do to thwart a premier who is making every choice to destroy

02:15:15.190 --> 02:15:18.630
our environment, children's schools, the healthcare system, erodes tenant protections

02:15:19.130 --> 02:15:22.890
that keep people housed, and is threatening our democracy while changing laws to prevent

02:15:23.390 --> 02:15:26.670
transparency? When is the last time he's done anything

02:15:27.170 --> 02:15:30.500
good for our communities? And I don't mean promise something good,

02:15:31.000 --> 02:15:34.519
I mean something that actually turned out to make things better for people and has

02:15:35.019 --> 02:15:37.690
followed through on what he alleges he's going to do.

02:15:40.159 --> 02:15:42.010
Thank you, Lynn. I have no questions.

02:15:44.140 --> 02:15:47.860
Uh, the next delegation is Jacara Droog from Kitchener.

02:15:48.180 --> 02:15:50.160
Jacara, I believe you're here in person.

02:15:53.700 --> 02:15:56.760
You have 5 minutes and the podium is yours.

02:15:57.970 --> 02:16:01.930
I'm a little not

02:16:02.430 --> 02:16:05.769
as tall as Tanner, but it's okay.

02:16:06.269 --> 02:16:09.850
Um, just before I begin, um, I'd like to add that only 6

02:16:10.350 --> 02:16:14.410
residents from 100 Vic have been housed, um, in permanent housing,

02:16:14.910 --> 02:16:18.470
and others are in temporary accommodation. Um, and this

02:16:18.970 --> 02:16:21.920
evidence comes from the undertakings through the court process.

02:16:23.470 --> 02:16:26.670
Uh, good morning, Council. Um, my name is Jacara,

02:16:26.870 --> 02:16:30.329
and over the past 3 years, I have spent countless hours

02:16:30.829 --> 02:16:34.210
in community with residents of 100 Vic.

02:16:34.710 --> 02:16:38.010
Since the encampment's inception, ordinary community members have

02:16:38.510 --> 02:16:42.690
done everything we can to make life a little more survivable for people

02:16:43.190 --> 02:16:46.150
living there. We've purchased water, food, tents,

02:16:46.599 --> 02:16:50.405
sleeping bags, survival gear, harm reduction, and sometimes

02:16:50.421 --> 02:16:53.937
given the clothes off our backs to help keep people alive.

02:16:55.013 --> 02:16:59.139
We have driven residents to medical appointments, visited people in hospital,

02:16:59.572 --> 02:17:03.008
and brought food to those relocated to motels who are struggling

02:17:03.041 --> 02:17:06.155
with food insecurity. We have been there as a

02:17:06.655 --> 02:17:10.571
shoulder to cry on and listen to residents share their experiences and trauma.

02:17:11.935 --> 02:17:14.777
Many of us have been there late into the night calling First Connect,

02:17:14.909 --> 02:17:18.309
Mi'kmaq First Nation, to find shelter beds that were not available and

02:17:18.809 --> 02:17:21.939
have had to break the devastating news that none were available.

02:17:23.108 --> 02:17:26.409
We've helped people set up tents in the dark late at night when they had

02:17:26.909 --> 02:17:30.169
nowhere else to go. We've brought meals to people who have not eaten

02:17:30.669 --> 02:17:34.069
all day. We have shown up during extreme heat waves,

02:17:34.289 --> 02:17:38.209
bitter cold and winter storms, and severe weather because people's

02:17:38.709 --> 02:17:42.150
lives depended on it. In doing so, We've come to

02:17:42.650 --> 02:17:46.430
know the residents of 100 Vic not as a policy issue, but as our neighbors,

02:17:46.890 --> 02:17:50.969
our friends, and as our loved ones. That is why it is

02:17:51.469 --> 02:17:54.830
so painful to watch this conversation continue to

02:17:55.330 --> 02:17:59.790
be framed around enforcement, removal, and punishment.

02:18:00.290 --> 02:18:04.170
In contrast, while the community has been trying to keep people alive,

02:18:04.250 --> 02:18:08.480
this council passed a punitive bylaw and used our tax dollars

02:18:08.980 --> 02:18:12.280
to pursue legal action against some of the most vulnerable people in

02:18:12.780 --> 02:18:17.340
our community. Justice Gibson ultimately found that bylaw to be unconstitutional.

02:18:18.700 --> 02:18:22.319
Appealing that decision will not house a single person or address chronic

02:18:22.819 --> 02:18:26.700
homelessness. Invoking the notwithstanding clause won't either.

02:18:27.200 --> 02:18:30.780
It will inevitably result in locking human beings in cages

02:18:31.280 --> 02:18:34.937
for the crime of being poor. Neither option will

02:18:34.985 --> 02:18:38.110
create affordable housing, increase income supports,

02:18:38.254 --> 02:18:41.924
improve access to healthcare, or address the conditions that led people

02:18:42.020 --> 02:18:43.927
to become unhoused in the first place.

02:18:45.065 --> 02:18:48.670
What these options do accomplish, though, is directing more

02:18:48.686 --> 02:18:52.532
public resources toward fighting people who have already been failed

02:18:53.032 --> 02:18:56.747
by multiple systems. As community members, we are

02:18:57.099 --> 02:18:59.930
absolutely exhausted. For years,

02:19:00.430 --> 02:19:03.430
advocates, frontline workers, researchers,

02:19:03.930 --> 02:19:07.950
and people with lived expertise have consistently identified the interventions that

02:19:08.450 --> 02:19:12.149
reduce homelessness and improve safety. Yet we continue to spend

02:19:12.649 --> 02:19:16.230
enormous amounts of time here debating how to remove people from the

02:19:16.730 --> 02:19:20.450
only legal place of last resort available to them when they have nowhere else to

02:19:20.950 --> 02:19:24.390
go. I'm also deeply troubled by the process that has brought

02:19:24.890 --> 02:19:28.459
us here. This meeting was called on short notice during working

02:19:28.959 --> 02:19:33.098
hours and separate from the regular council meeting. Public participation

02:19:33.598 --> 02:19:36.938
shouldn't be treated as a procedural hurdle to clear

02:19:37.438 --> 02:19:41.369
after major decisions have already been discussed behind closed doors.

02:19:41.659 --> 02:19:45.059
This approach further erodes public trust in a council

02:19:45.559 --> 02:19:48.949
that many residents already feel disconnected from.

02:19:49.189 --> 02:19:53.300
I'd normally stand here and ask council not to appeal Justice Gibson's decision

02:19:53.800 --> 02:19:57.840
and not to support the notwithstanding clause if I thought it would actually make

02:19:58.340 --> 02:20:02.740
a difference. But consistently, we've watched this council discuss important discussions—

02:20:04.039 --> 02:20:07.700
important decisions in-camera and then performatively invite public

02:20:08.200 --> 02:20:12.090
input after positions appear already settled.

02:20:12.220 --> 02:20:15.580
Nevertheless, I'm here because the public record matters. And for the

02:20:16.080 --> 02:20:19.320
record, I'm asking you to focus your efforts on the methods that experts and

02:20:19.820 --> 02:20:23.370
folks with lived expertise have repeatedly told you will reduce homelessness

02:20:23.870 --> 02:20:27.470
and improve safety. To wrap up, I'd like to share the

02:20:27.970 --> 02:20:31.590
words of my friend Dwayne, a resident of 100 Vic, who asked

02:20:32.090 --> 02:20:35.830
me to read this heartfelt statement to you on his behalf. And I hope

02:20:36.330 --> 02:20:39.770
I'm allowed to have time for that. It's not easy.

02:20:40.270 --> 02:20:43.790
It's really hard out here. Everyone here goes through something

02:20:44.290 --> 02:20:47.810
every day. We have to have somewhere we can actually live

02:20:47.910 --> 02:20:51.490
and be a family and a community. It is

02:20:51.990 --> 02:20:55.749
not easy here and we get robbed, but you're making it harder.

02:20:56.210 --> 02:20:59.890
This impacts more than the encampment. It impacts everyone in the

02:21:00.390 --> 02:21:03.590
community who is homeless. If you take it away, you're going to

02:21:04.090 --> 02:21:07.610
have more people staying in isolated areas of the community, at City

02:21:08.110 --> 02:21:12.090
Hall, on doorsteps. It's going to get worse. The encampment

02:21:12.590 --> 02:21:15.820
is a comforting place and a place where people belong. We save each

02:21:16.320 --> 02:21:19.250
other, we look out for each other. You can try to take this away,

02:21:19.519 --> 02:21:22.759
but you can't take away our community and our sense of comfort and

02:21:23.259 --> 02:21:26.960
belonging. We don't want people scared in the streets. We are

02:21:27.460 --> 02:21:31.100
not bothering people, and we need somewhere to belong. If you take it away,

02:21:31.460 --> 02:21:35.080
you will make us feel hopeless. This is about safety for us and for

02:21:35.580 --> 02:21:39.520
other people. Thank you. Thank you, J'kara. We do have a question.

02:21:40.020 --> 02:21:43.730
Councillor Schantz. Excuse me.

02:21:44.230 --> 02:21:46.759
Thank you for coming in today.

02:21:47.450 --> 02:21:51.170
Looking at the safe tenting protocols in some other

02:21:51.670 --> 02:21:54.910
jurisdictions, they're often limited to

02:21:55.410 --> 02:21:59.310
a small number of tents together. Can you speak to how that

02:21:59.810 --> 02:22:03.290
would work with the community here?

02:22:04.250 --> 02:22:07.882
Yeah, I don't feel like I'm personally qualified to answer

02:22:07.898 --> 02:22:11.382
that. I think folks like Dr. Laura Pin, who spoke earlier,

02:22:11.671 --> 02:22:15.525
have provided you research previously that could speak

02:22:15.541 --> 02:22:19.282
to that better than I could. Yeah. Any other questions?

02:22:22.205 --> 02:22:25.946
Thank you very much, and thank you for coming

02:22:25.994 --> 02:22:29.671
as a delegation. The next 3 delegations registered

02:22:29.735 --> 02:22:34.584
after the timeline, so they will get 3 minutes. And the first

02:22:35.084 --> 02:22:38.589
First one is Sid McDonald from Kitchener. Sid, are you here? Welcome to the podium.

02:22:39.089 --> 02:22:39.400
You have 3 minutes.

02:22:44.930 --> 02:22:50.370
I'm short,

02:22:50.870 --> 02:22:54.170
sorry. Okay.

02:22:54.670 --> 02:22:58.290
Hi. Thank you so much for the time to speak with you today. I'm delegating

02:22:58.320 --> 02:23:01.980
as an individual, but I do want to say that I was one of

02:23:02.480 --> 02:23:05.999
the people who did work with Laura Pinn on her project.

02:23:06.119 --> 02:23:10.560
I went out with my colleague in the community and conducted—

02:23:11.060 --> 02:23:14.380
together we did dozens of interviews, surveys with folks,

02:23:14.500 --> 02:23:17.960
and we heard a lot about how displacement

02:23:18.460 --> 02:23:22.140
affected them. Laura talked about people losing their phone,

02:23:23.100 --> 02:23:26.240
people couldn't find their outreach worker, their outreach worker couldn't

02:23:26.740 --> 02:23:30.140
find them and this whole

02:23:30.640 --> 02:23:34.439
strategy of displacing people for

02:23:34.939 --> 02:23:38.620
whatever reason is counterintuitive to what a lot of

02:23:39.120 --> 02:23:43.020
people want, which is to end homelessness. We can see that if somebody can't

02:23:43.520 --> 02:23:46.880
find their outreach worker, how are they going to be connected

02:23:47.380 --> 02:23:50.780
to services? And really,

02:23:51.280 --> 02:23:54.590
you don't have to displace people. The judge told you that

02:23:55.090 --> 02:23:58.749
there is a solution. Just as Joanna said, it is possible

02:23:59.249 --> 02:24:02.809
to build the transit hub still. You can do it while complying with the

02:24:03.309 --> 02:24:07.450
Charter. And while we are a two-tier municipality, there are

02:24:07.950 --> 02:24:12.230
all of the mayors around this circle as well. So I hope you can work

02:24:12.730 --> 02:24:16.050
together to approach this safe tenting protocol.

02:24:17.650 --> 02:24:21.000
Um, there was a question regarding, um, that parking lot is not

02:24:21.500 --> 02:24:24.900
appropriate and that housing is better able to meet human rights,

02:24:25.400 --> 02:24:28.500
I believe. And that's not verbatim, but, um, I think I can speak for a

02:24:29.000 --> 02:24:31.589
lot of people here that we agree we don't want people to live in encampments.

02:24:32.140 --> 02:24:35.200
But when we have people staying in shelter for

02:24:35.700 --> 02:24:39.130
years now because there's no housing to transition into,

02:24:39.320 --> 02:24:42.760
we've created a bottleneck where now the shelters are full.

02:24:43.260 --> 02:24:46.920
So people who are newly homeless or who have been restricted from shelter

02:24:47.100 --> 02:24:50.580
now have nowhere to go. That's why we have the

02:24:51.080 --> 02:24:54.369
encampment, and that's why it's important to have a place of last resort.

02:24:56.400 --> 02:25:00.020
So I'm urging you today, please don't

02:25:00.520 --> 02:25:03.700
appeal this decision by Justice Gibson.

02:25:04.180 --> 02:25:08.000
Don't waste the resources that could be used to

02:25:08.500 --> 02:25:12.300
go into asking staff to explore this protocol or discussing with each

02:25:12.800 --> 02:25:16.210
other how you could do something so

02:25:16.710 --> 02:25:20.850
that we're not displacing people and so that people are connected to services that

02:25:21.350 --> 02:25:24.440
ultimately find them housing.

02:25:25.730 --> 02:25:28.850
I also urge you to be the leaders that

02:25:29.350 --> 02:25:33.510
I know you are and push back against Doug Ford. He has

02:25:34.010 --> 02:25:36.650
done a lot of things. We know that our chair is now going to be

02:25:37.150 --> 02:25:40.800
appointed. Um, I think we can take

02:25:41.300 --> 02:25:43.370
more of a stand, and I think we can do it together.

02:25:44.750 --> 02:25:47.839
So I'm going to thank you for my time. I'm at the end of my

02:25:48.339 --> 02:25:51.490
time. Um, that's what I'm urging you to do.

02:25:52.339 --> 02:25:56.020
Sid, thank you very much for delegating. I have no questions, but thank you for

02:25:56.520 --> 02:25:59.800
being here. Thanks. The next delegation also has 3 minutes.

02:25:59.840 --> 02:26:01.040
Uh, Ratika Shirmali.

02:26:12.259 --> 02:26:15.490
Welcome, and you have 3 minutes. Thank you. Um, hi,

02:26:15.990 --> 02:26:19.110
I'm Ritika. I work at the Social Development Center as a program

02:26:19.610 --> 02:26:23.610
coordinator of Social program, and I'm reading this press release

02:26:24.110 --> 02:26:27.530
on behalf of the organization that I work for. In light of

02:26:28.030 --> 02:26:31.900
Council's impending response to the Gibson decision, The Social Development Center is

02:26:32.400 --> 02:26:36.200
back at council to call for a safe tenting framework.

02:26:36.339 --> 02:26:40.720
In February, we submitted 1,820 signatures calling

02:26:41.220 --> 02:26:45.080
for a safe tenting framework. The key role of a safe tenting framework

02:26:45.580 --> 02:26:49.560
is to act as the option of last resort for all the people unable

02:26:50.060 --> 02:26:54.220
to access the housing stability system. We call these people supportless

02:26:54.720 --> 02:26:57.865
because their life the right to life, liberty, and security of

02:26:58.365 --> 02:27:01.695
the person is threatened. When my colleagues talk to you and

02:27:02.195 --> 02:27:05.525
your colleagues at the lower, lower tier municipalities about safe tenting,

02:27:06.038 --> 02:27:09.163
we heard that the biggest hesitation on implementing a safe

02:27:09.227 --> 02:27:13.009
tenting framework is the struggle to find suitable locations

02:27:13.313 --> 02:27:16.262
at a scale to meet the number of people becoming supportless.

02:27:17.255 --> 02:27:21.262
We believe this hesitation is rooted in the misconception of safe tenting

02:27:21.470 --> 02:27:24.830
as be-all solution when we all know that it is

02:27:25.330 --> 02:27:29.129
not. As the option of last resort, safe tenting is one puzzle

02:27:29.629 --> 02:27:33.290
piece in the solution. It catches the people falling through the gaps into

02:27:33.790 --> 02:27:37.170
supportlessness. A safe tenting framework is not a relief

02:27:37.670 --> 02:27:41.170
from homelessness. Living in a tent is still substandard shelter.

02:27:41.510 --> 02:27:44.710
However, it's a relief from homelessness. It allows

02:27:45.210 --> 02:27:50.000
people to access a base level of service to manage their survival and dignity.

02:27:50.500 --> 02:27:53.939
It gives them a place to legally exist. It's the option of last

02:27:54.439 --> 02:27:58.120
resort. When paired with other interventions, the number and size

02:27:58.620 --> 02:28:02.800
of safe tenting zones needed can be reduced to a far more manageable experience.

02:28:03.380 --> 02:28:06.760
That is why we are encouraging you to see safe tenting as just

02:28:07.260 --> 02:28:10.920
one pillar of a supportlessness relief strategy. A supportless

02:28:11.420 --> 02:28:15.420
relief strategy pairs safe tenting with multiple 24/7 low barrier

02:28:15.920 --> 02:28:19.850
hubs and some key reforms to the shelter and transitional housing systems.

02:28:20.280 --> 02:28:24.099
The current shelter and transitional housing systems are ill-equipped to

02:28:24.599 --> 02:28:27.859
handle any increasingly traumatized populations.

02:28:28.359 --> 02:28:31.650
We need reforms that take into account the risk of supportlessness.

02:28:32.540 --> 02:28:36.320
We need low barrier spaces to allow the spaces with barriers

02:28:36.820 --> 02:28:40.740
to also thrive. This is where our proposed 24/7 low

02:28:41.240 --> 02:28:44.760
barrier hubs framework comes in, an idea being developed by

02:28:45.260 --> 02:28:49.090
the Continuum of Care constellation within the Plan to End Chronic Homelessness.

02:28:49.500 --> 02:28:53.660
It builds on the success of programs like A Better 10 Cities visitor policy

02:28:54.160 --> 02:28:58.150
and the low barrier warming centers run by the Porchlight and Thresholds.

02:28:58.520 --> 02:29:02.380
Supporting the sustainability of these programs into 24/7

02:29:02.880 --> 02:29:06.780
low barrier spaces is a key puzzle piece to allow safe safe-denting zones

02:29:07.280 --> 02:29:10.300
to remain an option of last resort. Both are required.

02:29:11.819 --> 02:29:15.120
A safe-denting framework is needed. We have to have an option

02:29:15.620 --> 02:29:19.340
of that last resort to act as a foundation for a fundamental human

02:29:19.840 --> 02:29:23.280
right. But it is not only one piece of a bigger process to stop the

02:29:23.780 --> 02:29:27.920
rise of supportlessness. Taken as a whole, the scale and impact

02:29:28.420 --> 02:29:31.980
of safe-denting zones is put into context. There is no need

02:29:32.480 --> 02:29:36.310
to fear massive tent cities. Alongside pumped-up and reformed

02:29:36.810 --> 02:29:40.490
programs that already exist, we can keep the option of last resort as an

02:29:40.990 --> 02:29:44.630
option of last resort. Thank you. Thank you very much for

02:29:45.130 --> 02:29:48.870
delegating. I see no questions, so the next delegation is

02:29:49.370 --> 02:29:51.210
Jacob Stubbs.

02:29:56.940 --> 02:29:58.240
Welcome, you have 3 minutes.

02:30:05.410 --> 02:30:08.620
Good morning, Chair and councillors. My name is Jacob.

02:30:09.540 --> 02:30:13.120
I'm speaking in good faith today, not only as a resident of the region

02:30:13.620 --> 02:30:16.960
but as an individual with some personal experience living

02:30:17.460 --> 02:30:21.060
at the 100 Victoria Street encampment, as well as

02:30:21.560 --> 02:30:25.440
directly supporting and advocating for our neighbors living there in similar circumstances

02:30:25.940 --> 02:30:29.402
in the region. I have some firsthand experience of the measures

02:30:29.434 --> 02:30:32.533
taken and not taken by the region regarding such encampment.

02:30:34.412 --> 02:30:38.185
Over the past several years, residents have watched two important

02:30:38.685 --> 02:30:43.452
public priorities become unnecessarily pitted against one another: providing

02:30:43.564 --> 02:30:47.482
dignified options for people experiencing homelessness and

02:30:47.578 --> 02:30:50.452
building the transit infrastructure our region needs for its future.

02:30:51.700 --> 02:30:54.320
The transit hub, as you may agree,

02:30:55.100 --> 02:30:58.420
is an important public project. It should make mobility

02:30:58.920 --> 02:31:02.740
more accessible, reduce car dependency, be an important step towards a more resilient

02:31:03.240 --> 02:31:07.350
and less carbon-intensive society, and help build a more connected region.

02:31:08.400 --> 02:31:11.620
But the people at the encampment should never have been pushed into a

02:31:12.120 --> 02:31:15.480
position where their basic survival appears to be in such conflict with

02:31:15.980 --> 02:31:18.131
that goal. Last month,

02:31:18.628 --> 02:31:22.682
Justice Gibson made clear that the region cannot simply remove people

02:31:22.890 --> 02:31:26.351
without a lawful alternative. More importantly,

02:31:26.816 --> 02:31:30.725
the court also identified possible solutions: a safe tenting

02:31:30.821 --> 02:31:34.811
protocol, a reasonably proximate alternative site, or both.

02:31:36.093 --> 02:31:38.640
In other words, this council has options.

02:31:40.178 --> 02:31:43.751
What concerns me the most is that public discussion now

02:31:43.815 --> 02:31:47.854
seems increasingly focused on whether the province may intervene rather

02:31:47.870 --> 02:31:51.654
than on whether the region will act. Waiting for Doug Ford to

02:31:52.154 --> 02:31:55.790
step in using emergency powers does not remove responsibility

02:31:56.367 --> 02:31:59.589
from local decision makers. The court

02:31:59.733 --> 02:32:03.661
has already outlined a path forward. Choosing not to pursue it

02:32:03.901 --> 02:32:05.152
would itself be a choice.

02:32:07.524 --> 02:32:10.025
If that choice leads to further conflict,

02:32:10.329 --> 02:32:13.938
further litigation, and further harm to vulnerable residents,

02:32:14.900 --> 02:32:18.750
the public will rightfully ask why other options provided

02:32:19.250 --> 02:32:20.804
to you were not seriously pursued.

02:32:22.905 --> 02:32:26.370
This is not a question of choosing between housing justice and public

02:32:26.870 --> 02:32:30.990
transit. It is a question of whether we are willing to do the work necessary

02:32:31.086 --> 02:32:34.759
to advance both of those with grace. I am

02:32:34.936 --> 02:32:38.128
asking council to move forward with a safe tenting

02:32:38.224 --> 02:32:41.810
protocol or identify a lawful alternative site

02:32:42.310 --> 02:32:45.530
that respects people's autonomy and engages directly with

02:32:46.030 --> 02:32:50.040
residents and service providers to develop a serious solution.

02:32:51.370 --> 02:32:54.850
The court has provided a roadmap. The many of

02:32:55.350 --> 02:32:59.010
us affected by this issue have waited long enough. Please use

02:32:59.510 --> 02:33:02.830
the tools that are already available to you, and thank

02:33:03.330 --> 02:33:06.500
you for listening. Thank you, Jacob.

02:33:06.564 --> 02:33:09.952
We have a question. Councilor James. Thank you. Through you, Chair, uh,

02:33:10.096 --> 02:33:13.773
Jacob, thank you for coming in and speaking. Not often easy

02:33:14.110 --> 02:33:17.145
coming and delegating to any council, um,

02:33:17.563 --> 02:33:21.240
and for sharing that you, um, had resided

02:33:21.336 --> 02:33:24.644
at 100 Vic, if I heard correctly. Is that, that correct? Uh,

02:33:24.788 --> 02:33:28.064
yep. Okay, so, um, and going

02:33:28.080 --> 02:33:32.090
to ask these questions and Choose to answer

02:33:32.590 --> 02:33:34.510
them if you want to. If you don't want to, you don't have to.

02:33:35.010 --> 02:33:38.040
I'm going to preface it with that. In your opinion,

02:33:38.330 --> 02:33:42.500
and with you having that lived experience,

02:33:43.910 --> 02:33:46.280
is it safe? Is it humane?

02:33:49.270 --> 02:33:53.220
And I just want to preface this because we've had delegations speak about—

02:33:55.380 --> 02:33:59.380
attacks happening there, safety. And I mean, and this is,

02:33:59.880 --> 02:34:02.340
that's hearsay, and, and this is why I want to ask you directly,

02:34:03.040 --> 02:34:06.220
um, and that's why I said you do not have to answer this,

02:34:06.640 --> 02:34:10.800
but we've heard people come in and speak to situations

02:34:11.300 --> 02:34:14.980
and, you know, and trying to find a

02:34:15.480 --> 02:34:19.160
place of last resort that is safe for people, that is humane for people,

02:34:19.660 --> 02:34:23.330
that is dignified And I'm conscious

02:34:23.830 --> 02:34:27.469
of that when people do not have a place to sleep,

02:34:27.950 --> 02:34:31.930
a place to feel safe. So I would love to hear

02:34:32.430 --> 02:34:35.550
kind of your perspective if you want to.

02:34:36.050 --> 02:34:39.310
Yeah, thank you. I actually respect that question.

02:34:39.710 --> 02:34:43.310
I do keep in mind like the holistic factors

02:34:43.810 --> 02:34:47.090
of like how you have to make decisions and keeping all these factors in

02:34:47.590 --> 02:34:50.760
mind. Factors in mind. I think a key aspect

02:34:51.260 --> 02:34:55.020
that gets lost through the lens of government and the state is the

02:34:55.520 --> 02:34:59.600
factor of autonomy and choice for people when

02:35:00.100 --> 02:35:01.389
there is limited options.

02:35:05.040 --> 02:35:08.920
Obviously, there's flaws with any decision. I think a key

02:35:09.420 --> 02:35:13.260
thing here is that when people are choosing one

02:35:13.760 --> 02:35:17.378
option over another, that choice in and of itself is one that

02:35:18.871 --> 02:35:22.994
allows for the decision of safety to

02:35:25.225 --> 02:35:27.311
be in the hands of those residents, right?

02:35:31.467 --> 02:35:36.056
Yeah, I think choice is a key theme there. I think

02:35:37.484 --> 02:35:40.950
a lot of the methods of throwing funding and policing and all these kinds of

02:35:41.450 --> 02:35:44.900
things, through the lens

02:35:45.400 --> 02:35:48.707
of government and the state and the powers that you have,

02:35:48.819 --> 02:35:52.288
it makes sense that those decisions

02:35:52.788 --> 02:35:57.379
are made through that lens. But providing

02:35:57.411 --> 02:36:01.057
an alternative to give people the choice for themselves is a key thing.

02:36:01.571 --> 02:36:05.281
Having housing that is proper shelter

02:36:05.781 --> 02:36:08.445
and all these things is an important factor. And I do respect that. That that

02:36:08.573 --> 02:36:11.830
is something that provides safety for people. But ultimately,

02:36:12.263 --> 02:36:15.841
giving people the choice of what is most safe for them is

02:36:16.258 --> 02:36:20.301
ultimately one that puts less pressure

02:36:20.429 --> 02:36:24.119
on, in my opinion, all factors involved, all parties

02:36:24.619 --> 02:36:28.002
involved, and basically threads the needle in

02:36:28.227 --> 02:36:31.115
giving people the most safe option. And yeah,

02:36:31.676 --> 02:36:38.049
I hope that— Okay. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you,

02:36:38.549 --> 02:36:42.270
Chair. Thank you for providing your insight and your lived experience today. Did you know

02:36:42.770 --> 02:36:46.570
that Ontario is the only province in Canada that does not

02:36:47.070 --> 02:36:50.610
fully fund the cost of supportive housing and shelter

02:36:51.110 --> 02:36:55.190
costs? Um, I didn't.

02:36:55.690 --> 02:36:59.530
I, I don't necessarily see it as like an argument to avoid

02:36:59.670 --> 02:37:03.270
like providing alternatives and options, but— I agree

02:37:03.770 --> 02:37:07.389
with you. I just feel that putting all of the

02:37:07.889 --> 02:37:11.010
housing resources on the municipality is not the answer

02:37:11.510 --> 02:37:15.350
because also property taxes are not geared to income.

02:37:15.850 --> 02:37:19.910
So I hear you, I'm with you, and I really hope that Ontario will

02:37:20.410 --> 02:37:23.910
follow the lead of other provinces across Canada and

02:37:24.410 --> 02:37:29.300
provide more funding funding for more supportive housing options,

02:37:29.800 --> 02:37:32.540
um, because again, um,

02:37:33.040 --> 02:37:36.440
everybody deserves more than a parking lot to live

02:37:36.940 --> 02:37:40.180
in. Thank you. So Jacob, I think that's— that literally was a

02:37:40.680 --> 02:37:44.020
did you know question. I don't know if you want to respond. Um,

02:37:44.520 --> 02:37:47.660
I, I'll keep it brief. Um, yeah,

02:37:48.160 --> 02:37:51.590
I just think that the emphasis on

02:37:52.393 --> 02:37:56.290
continuing to provide a solution through

02:37:57.990 --> 02:38:01.839
the lens of people not directly actually dealing with it. The key

02:38:02.339 --> 02:38:05.543
thing is that the choice of the people dealing with the issue in and

02:38:06.043 --> 02:38:12.102
of itself gets erased when we constantly override

02:38:12.602 --> 02:38:16.688
it in the sense of choosing what

02:38:17.188 --> 02:38:20.660
is dignified for other people. Through the lens of the state is kind

02:38:21.160 --> 02:38:24.800
of a problem that ironically defeats kind of the point of

02:38:25.300 --> 02:38:28.620
those goals that are legitimately stated of providing people

02:38:29.120 --> 02:38:32.840
dignity and safety and stuff like that. That's just my perspective. And yeah,

02:38:33.340 --> 02:38:36.080
thank you. If I could just make one more comment, Chair Redmond.

02:38:36.580 --> 02:38:40.230
Quick. I do not want to evict anyone,

02:38:40.280 --> 02:38:43.760
but I feel that approving the safe tensing bylaw

02:38:44.260 --> 02:38:47.660
sends the message to the community and to the province that

02:38:48.160 --> 02:38:51.399
this is good enough, and it's not. Okay,

02:38:51.899 --> 02:38:55.009
that was a comment. Thank you, Jacob. Those are all the questions we have.

02:38:55.820 --> 02:38:58.600
So thank you,

02:38:59.100 --> 02:39:02.560
Jacob. And we have one more delegation. I'm going to

02:39:03.060 --> 02:39:06.500
call, uh, Jason Fitzgerald from Kitchener.

02:39:07.000 --> 02:39:10.780
We called you earlier and you weren't here, so You're

02:39:11.280 --> 02:39:14.979
in the 5-minute category, so the floor

02:39:15.479 --> 02:39:16.399
is yours and you have 5 minutes.

02:39:18.959 --> 02:39:23.580
Excuse me. Good morning. I apologize.

02:39:24.080 --> 02:39:27.900
I ran out of power on my phone and I didn't wake up. I live

02:39:28.400 --> 02:39:31.170
at 100 Victoria Street North.

02:39:36.270 --> 02:39:39.750
I've been a resident of Waterloo Region

02:39:40.250 --> 02:39:43.399
for 51 years.

02:39:44.250 --> 02:39:47.020
I've lived at 100 Vic for the last 2 years now.

02:39:48.969 --> 02:39:51.549
I apologize, I'm in a little bit of pain, sorry.

02:39:53.610 --> 02:39:56.950
I've worked for, since I

02:39:57.450 --> 02:39:58.520
was 14 years old.

02:40:03.641 --> 02:40:08.070
Up until 7

02:40:08.570 --> 02:40:12.323
years ago where I was injured at

02:40:12.823 --> 02:40:16.271
work and I lost my job

02:40:17.378 --> 02:40:18.084
due to,

02:40:21.133 --> 02:40:24.985
I worked for a company in this town that

02:40:25.546 --> 02:40:28.403
when I broke my back,

02:40:30.600 --> 02:40:36.469
I was targeted and basically

02:40:36.534 --> 02:40:39.452
fired because I was put on restrictions for life.

02:40:43.189 --> 02:40:46.268
I went through all of what I could.

02:40:47.438 --> 02:40:49.908
I broke my back. I got put on pills.

02:40:51.287 --> 02:40:55.521
I took those pills. I tried to go through rehab. The rehab

02:40:56.021 --> 02:40:59.880
was too expensive, and I was continued to

02:41:00.380 --> 02:41:04.360
stay on medication. That medication became way

02:41:04.860 --> 02:41:08.640
too much, and I found alternative ways

02:41:09.140 --> 02:41:12.800
to deal with my pain. The problem that

02:41:13.300 --> 02:41:16.500
I've noticed down at the

02:41:17.000 --> 02:41:20.440
tents is mental health. People dealing

02:41:20.940 --> 02:41:24.990
with pain, People losing their

02:41:25.490 --> 02:41:29.949
jobs. Um, this council doesn't understand

02:41:30.449 --> 02:41:34.510
what it is to live

02:41:35.010 --> 02:41:38.670
on the streets. I dare you,

02:41:39.170 --> 02:41:42.770
any one of you, to take 2 months and live off

02:41:43.270 --> 02:41:46.590
ODSP, ODSP, and live on the streets.

02:41:46.620 --> 02:41:50.360
I dare any one of you to feel that

02:41:50.860 --> 02:41:54.139
shame of how it

02:41:54.639 --> 02:41:58.819
is to depend on someone else to live your life.

02:42:00.540 --> 02:42:05.080
I had everything.

02:42:05.580 --> 02:42:08.700
I had a car, trailer, motorcycles, boats.

02:42:09.040 --> 02:42:12.640
I've lost all my stuff

02:42:13.140 --> 02:42:17.160
3 times now and I have nothing. But a backpack and a

02:42:17.660 --> 02:42:21.880
tent. That every night I go to bed and hope that I don't lose the

02:42:22.380 --> 02:42:23.249
rest of what I have right now.

02:42:26.880 --> 02:42:31.040
And to say that you

02:42:31.540 --> 02:42:35.400
guys wanted to criminalize people for having

02:42:35.900 --> 02:42:39.300
to be on the streets, but not

02:42:39.800 --> 02:42:43.660
their own fault is ridiculous.

02:42:44.160 --> 02:42:46.680
Wake up, wake up, okay?

02:42:49.080 --> 02:42:52.360
It's ridiculous. Anyone in this city is one paycheck

02:42:52.860 --> 02:42:56.339
away of being where I am. I'm going to ask you

02:42:56.839 --> 02:43:00.420
to please restrain from clapping. Jason, go ahead.

02:43:00.920 --> 02:43:04.200
Sorry, I'm just— I'm one paycheck.

02:43:04.700 --> 02:43:08.340
11,421 people are

02:43:08.840 --> 02:43:12.019
without homes right now. Is that what the paper said the other day? Does anybody

02:43:12.519 --> 02:43:16.539
know? Almost 11,500 people.

02:43:17.039 --> 02:43:20.800
You know what their alternatives is? The Victoria Inn, the Mayflower,

02:43:21.300 --> 02:43:25.320
the other two, Weber. Have you guys been

02:43:25.820 --> 02:43:29.750
in any of these places? Do you know what they're living in?

02:43:29.840 --> 02:43:32.590
I've sat in a washroom with a toilet above me,

02:43:32.960 --> 02:43:36.270
leaks on me. Cockroaches all over the

02:43:36.770 --> 02:43:37.849
place, bed bugs.

02:43:38.349 --> 02:43:42.510
You guys don't understand.

02:43:43.010 --> 02:43:46.070
This is what you guys are paying for to help housing people.

02:43:46.609 --> 02:43:49.109
We have a place brand new for St.

02:43:49.609 --> 02:43:52.780
John's with how many buildings over there with no funding?

02:43:53.090 --> 02:43:56.590
None. Not one single funding. You guys

02:43:57.090 --> 02:44:00.050
make this meeting this morning at a special time in the morning.

02:44:00.750 --> 02:44:03.590
You know how many people would have been here if this was a normal meeting

02:44:04.090 --> 02:44:07.780
at nighttime? This place would be roaring. I'm telling you that right now.

02:44:08.190 --> 02:44:12.319
And it's a shame too. That's one thing I'm very disrespectful about.

02:44:15.310 --> 02:44:19.610
The one common factor down there, I'll say, is pain.

02:44:20.110 --> 02:44:22.930
I've seen a lot of people go through it. They get caught up. It's not

02:44:23.430 --> 02:44:27.293
the drugs. It's this government. The provincial

02:44:27.793 --> 02:44:31.507
government, the federal government has failed this country. You bring

02:44:31.603 --> 02:44:35.145
people from another country, you give them everything. The people that

02:44:35.645 --> 02:44:38.814
live here work their whole lives and

02:44:39.314 --> 02:44:42.484
they get nothing. My brother had a surgery.

02:44:42.740 --> 02:44:46.410
You had a bitch come from another country, he was pushed off his surgery

02:44:46.426 --> 02:44:49.582
date because a man got off the plane yesterday and needed surgery.

02:44:50.608 --> 02:44:53.880
This is how you treat your own citizens here in this country. That have

02:44:54.380 --> 02:44:57.160
been here for forever. This is ridiculous.

02:44:58.900 --> 02:45:02.220
Jason, you're out of time. Do you want to sum up just your last comment?

02:45:02.720 --> 02:45:06.420
Yeah, okay. I'm sorry. I just want to say that if you guys go

02:45:06.920 --> 02:45:10.340
down there and talk to the people that are in this encampment and all

02:45:10.840 --> 02:45:15.340
over shelters, that the government has

02:45:15.840 --> 02:45:20.810
failed us. People need

02:45:21.310 --> 02:45:24.770
to go through pain. The problem with

02:45:25.270 --> 02:45:28.830
pain is they'll give you the drugs. Will they

02:45:29.330 --> 02:45:32.910
give you the money to do the rehabilitation to

02:45:33.410 --> 02:45:37.410
get yourself better? No, they just leave you on the drugs. Those drugs become

02:45:37.910 --> 02:45:42.050
street drugs. That's where you go. Mental health

02:45:42.550 --> 02:45:46.220
is a huge problem, and I've seen it down there. I've talked to

02:45:46.720 --> 02:45:50.259
a girl that has mental health that lives in Encampment. She came down there

02:45:50.759 --> 02:45:54.180
and started doing $200 fetti every single day because she thought

02:45:54.680 --> 02:45:57.760
she needed to fit in. It's ridiculous.

02:45:58.260 --> 02:46:01.750
Ridiculous. And if you want to know,

02:46:01.780 --> 02:46:05.400
counselor, if it's safe down there, it's safe because

02:46:05.900 --> 02:46:09.340
the people like me and other people, we make it safe. We protect

02:46:09.840 --> 02:46:13.140
those people. I make sure They're doing drugs, but I

02:46:13.640 --> 02:46:15.829
make sure they have water. I make sure they're protected.

02:46:16.920 --> 02:46:20.339
I make sure they have clean needles. I'm the

02:46:20.839 --> 02:46:24.360
one that makes sure that stuff happens. We have people that come in there and

02:46:24.860 --> 02:46:28.250
cause trouble. We call the police. They come and do nothing.

02:46:28.780 --> 02:46:32.600
Nothing. Nothing to protect us. So guess what

02:46:33.100 --> 02:46:36.280
happens? Violence happens to that person that comes in

02:46:36.780 --> 02:46:40.480
and tries to destroy our place. And then that person goes to jail.

02:46:40.980 --> 02:46:43.889
The person that lives there trying to def— protect his home,

02:46:44.339 --> 02:46:47.640
right? Jason, thank you very much,

02:46:48.140 --> 02:46:51.560
and thank you for coming in. Colleagues, we've heard

02:46:52.060 --> 02:46:55.440
a lot this morning. It's been a long morning. Uh, do we want a 5-minute

02:46:55.940 --> 02:46:59.040
bio break, or do we want to— okay, so we're going to recess for 5

02:46:59.540 --> 02:47:00.320
minutes and then we'll reconvene.

02:47:03.150 --> 02:47:04.513
Get ready.

02:55:53.030 --> 02:55:53.930
Zoom,

02:55:56.190 --> 02:55:59.630
and we're going to consider a motion, um, from closed

02:56:00.130 --> 02:56:03.587
committee summary from June 2nd, 2026, and Clerk will now display it

02:56:03.603 --> 02:56:04.693
to the— on the screen.

02:56:17.532 --> 02:56:20.963
I will read it. This is the, uh, from closed

02:56:20.995 --> 02:56:25.082
committee recommends that the Regional Municipality of Waterloo appeal the

02:56:25.582 --> 02:56:29.170
decision of Ontario Superior Court, Justice— for Justice

02:56:29.234 --> 02:56:31.780
J. Gibson, dated dated May 21st,

02:56:32.280 --> 02:56:35.739
2026, in the matter of the Regional Municipality of Waterloo and

02:56:36.239 --> 02:56:39.920
named respondents, court file— and it's a long number— uh, to the

02:56:40.420 --> 02:56:44.260
Ontario Court of Appeal, and that the Justice Chief Administrative

02:56:44.760 --> 02:56:48.080
Officer, in consultation with the regional solicitor, be authorized

02:56:48.580 --> 02:56:51.780
and directed to take all necessary steps and execute

02:56:52.280 --> 02:56:55.680
any required documentation to give effect to this resolution.

02:56:56.180 --> 02:57:00.200
If I have a mover and a seconder. Shame on you!

02:57:00.700 --> 02:57:03.300
Shame on you! Move by Councilor Erb,

02:57:03.800 --> 02:57:06.840
seconded?

02:57:07.340 --> 02:57:10.680
Who's going to— Second? I'll second it, Chair. Thank you.

02:57:11.180 --> 02:57:14.440
So it's been moved and seconded. Colleagues,

02:57:14.940 --> 02:57:18.920
are there any questions? Shame on you! Shame on

02:57:19.420 --> 02:57:25.750
you! Shame on you! Shame on you! So I'm gonna I'm

02:57:26.250 --> 02:57:29.150
going to ask the gallery to, if you want to have a conversation, please take

02:57:29.650 --> 02:57:32.909
it outside. Yeah, will you be there? You want to talk

02:57:33.409 --> 02:57:37.350
to the people? Yeah, sure. Can you—

02:57:37.850 --> 02:57:40.420
shame on all of you. We're just making comments. It's okay. What was allowed?

02:57:41.030 --> 02:57:44.810
Not a question, comment. So I'm not seeing any speakers. We can move.

02:57:46.070 --> 02:57:47.040
Are you on eScribe?

02:57:49.040 --> 02:57:53.360
Okay, so Councilor Wolff, go ahead.

02:58:01.519 --> 02:58:04.700
Okay, uh, I understand council's frustration around

02:58:05.200 --> 02:58:08.640
the encampments of Victoria and Weber, um, but let us choose a path

02:58:09.140 --> 02:58:12.440
that reflects who we are at our best. Uh, I'm speaking

02:58:12.940 --> 02:58:16.850
against council pursuing appeal of Justice Gibson's

02:58:17.350 --> 02:58:20.890
ruling. We have spent far too much money on legal

02:58:21.390 --> 02:58:24.930
fees, staff, and council time. Our chances

02:58:25.430 --> 02:58:29.129
of winning the appeal are far from guaranteed. We are ignoring

02:58:29.629 --> 02:58:33.970
the solution offered by Justice Gibson and ignoring two rulings.

02:58:34.470 --> 02:58:37.970
Council should not count on Premier Ford's

02:58:38.470 --> 02:58:41.459
promise to use the notwithstanding clause if we lose the appeal.

02:58:42.213 --> 02:58:43.932
I find this unacceptable.

02:58:46.645 --> 02:58:51.318
Uh, declaring the encampment

02:58:51.818 --> 02:58:55.444
illegal and evicting its residents is not a solution.

02:58:56.038 --> 02:58:59.523
Without protocols, we do not eliminate encampments.

02:58:59.731 --> 02:59:03.713
We simply make them more dangerous, more hidden,

02:59:04.227 --> 02:59:08.050
and more chaotic. This put lives at risk.

02:59:08.260 --> 02:59:11.500
Alongside the practical considerations, there is

02:59:12.000 --> 02:59:15.619
something even more fundamental: our commitment to the Canadian

02:59:16.119 --> 02:59:20.340
Charter of Rights and Freedoms. The Charter exists to protect everyone,

02:59:20.840 --> 02:59:24.160
especially those who have the least power. It ensures that government

02:59:24.660 --> 02:59:28.970
actions remain fair, measured, and respectful of human rights.

02:59:29.140 --> 02:59:32.840
The Premier's suggestion of invoking notwithstanding clause

02:59:33.340 --> 02:59:37.109
in this context is deeply concerning.

02:59:37.599 --> 02:59:41.619
Using the notwithstanding clause would mean deliberately overriding

02:59:42.119 --> 02:59:46.160
constitutional rights. It would send a message that the rights of people

02:59:46.660 --> 02:59:50.100
experiencing homelessness are negotiable. That is

02:59:50.600 --> 02:59:55.020
not a precedent we should be willing to set. Courts have—

02:59:55.520 --> 02:59:59.027
please don't clap. Courts have

02:59:59.075 --> 03:00:02.936
increasingly recognized that forcibly removing people from

03:00:03.436 --> 03:00:07.247
encampments without offering adequate alternatives can violate

03:00:07.747 --> 03:00:10.563
Charter protections relating to life,

03:00:10.756 --> 03:00:12.823
liberty, and security of the person.

03:00:14.922 --> 03:00:18.030
Invoking the notwithstanding clause

03:00:18.142 --> 03:00:22.068
of this section would not solve homelessness. It would not create

03:00:22.568 --> 03:00:26.680
housing. It would not address mental health. Health and addiction challenges,

03:00:27.180 --> 03:00:31.710
it would simply strip away legal protection from people who are already vulnerable.

03:00:32.419 --> 03:00:36.480
This crisis requires compassion, evidence-based policy,

03:00:36.980 --> 03:00:40.980
and respect for human dignity, not constitutional shortcuts.

03:00:41.480 --> 03:00:45.240
To those who, who came today to speak, thank you for reminding us

03:00:45.740 --> 03:00:49.040
of our shared responsibility to care for one another and

03:00:49.540 --> 03:00:52.931
to uphold the values that defend us. Again, let us choose a

03:00:52.979 --> 03:00:56.862
path that reflects who we are at our best. And I

03:00:56.910 --> 03:01:00.327
have no doubt that everyone on council cares and

03:01:00.343 --> 03:01:03.729
is trying to do their best, but a community that protects

03:01:04.229 --> 03:01:07.788
rights, that leads with empathy, and that refuses

03:01:07.900 --> 03:01:11.028
to leave anyone behind is what we aspire

03:01:11.528 --> 03:01:14.783
to. Councillor James. Thank you. Through you,

03:01:14.799 --> 03:01:18.430
Chair, um, I want to thank all the delegates

03:01:18.930 --> 03:01:21.839
who took the time to speak today. I also want to thank our staff,

03:01:22.009 --> 03:01:25.529
the regional staff who've been working on this. I want

03:01:26.029 --> 03:01:29.700
to also acknowledge the significant commitment and care that is being expressed,

03:01:29.950 --> 03:01:34.270
that has been expressed, for individuals, um, who are experiencing

03:01:34.770 --> 03:01:38.410
homelessness, living rough within our community. I've listened closely

03:01:38.910 --> 03:01:42.350
to the perspectives shared throughout the discussion. There is no question

03:01:42.850 --> 03:01:46.810
that there is a deep commitment, concern, and shared desire to

03:01:47.310 --> 03:01:50.790
find humane and effective solutions. In my

03:01:51.290 --> 03:01:54.910
view, attempting protocol may have made sense as a proactive measure prior

03:01:55.410 --> 03:01:59.710
to the decision to proceed with court action regarding 100 Victoria.

03:01:59.990 --> 03:02:03.670
However, we're not operating in that earlier context anymore. We are now

03:02:04.170 --> 03:02:08.243
within an active legal and operational process, and that reality constrains range the options

03:02:08.356 --> 03:02:11.953
before us. I also want to be clear that

03:02:12.453 --> 03:02:15.968
I do not believe the courts or attempting protocol alone

03:02:16.468 --> 03:02:20.015
will resolve the underlying challenges we are facing. We are dealing with

03:02:20.064 --> 03:02:23.227
a much broader systemic housing shelter

03:02:23.838 --> 03:02:27.435
operations operating beyond our capacity.

03:02:27.933 --> 03:02:31.483
The closure of CTS sites, reduced funding with a

03:02:31.983 --> 03:02:35.624
significant shortfall of supportive housing units, strained agencies

03:02:35.640 --> 03:02:38.398
who do not have the resources to meet the growing needs.

03:02:39.248 --> 03:02:43.001
At the same time, we are seeing growing pressures on seniors

03:02:43.097 --> 03:02:46.176
who are at risk, youth who are at risk,

03:02:47.106 --> 03:02:50.474
those who, as we have heard, face injury at

03:02:50.554 --> 03:02:53.136
work and are one paycheck away,

03:02:55.862 --> 03:02:59.615
where other levels of government and coordination have really not kept

03:03:00.115 --> 03:03:03.920
pace with the need. I also want to recognize that this is

03:03:04.420 --> 03:03:08.860
not simply a municipal issue. We are operating within a two-tiered

03:03:09.360 --> 03:03:12.780
system that requires alignment across area municipalities

03:03:13.280 --> 03:03:17.240
and provincial partners. Without that alignment, even well-intended

03:03:17.740 --> 03:03:21.640
local solutions are difficult to implement effectively.

03:03:22.380 --> 03:03:26.050
I want to acknowledge that I do not support the court injunction It

03:03:26.550 --> 03:03:30.450
didn't. But this is what's been dealt. Some of us walked into

03:03:30.950 --> 03:03:35.030
this situation. That said,

03:03:35.530 --> 03:03:39.069
we are in a process underway. So I do

03:03:39.569 --> 03:03:43.910
remain committed and open to some more community-based solutions,

03:03:44.410 --> 03:03:47.510
as we've heard today. In fact, I've been encouraged by outreach from private

03:03:48.010 --> 03:03:53.000
residents offering space and potential partnerships to support those Currently,

03:03:53.500 --> 03:03:57.299
while I supported the idea of attempting protocol while presented by Councillor Pam

03:03:57.799 --> 03:04:01.189
Wolf, and she knows that I support that, and it was defeated.

03:04:03.540 --> 03:04:06.740
And so now we're working with an op— direct decisions that have already

03:04:07.240 --> 03:04:10.520
been made. And I believe the solution must go

03:04:11.020 --> 03:04:14.900
beyond a single policy tool. It will require coordinated actions

03:04:15.400 --> 03:04:19.170
across government, sustained investment in housing and supports and a commitment

03:04:19.670 --> 03:04:23.310
to addressing the root causes of homelessness rather than its

03:04:23.810 --> 03:04:27.380
most visible symptoms. If we have better employment standards,

03:04:27.910 --> 03:04:30.120
perhaps Jason would not be where he is today.

03:04:31.310 --> 03:04:34.990
So this is deeply systemic, it's deeply national, and it's

03:04:35.490 --> 03:04:38.830
affecting people all across this country. And I do not want to be doing performative

03:04:39.330 --> 03:04:41.790
things knowing that the outcome is not going to be what we intend.

03:04:44.770 --> 03:04:47.899
Councillor Gowing. Are you— Councillor James, you're done?

03:04:48.399 --> 03:04:51.520
Thank you, Chair. I am done. Okay, I'm going to ask that please,

03:04:52.600 --> 03:04:54.650
if you're here to listen, that's great. If you want to have a conversation,

03:04:55.300 --> 03:04:58.739
please take it elsewhere. There are people watching online and it becomes very difficult

03:04:59.239 --> 03:05:02.660
to follow the, um, conversation. Councillor Gowing.

03:05:03.160 --> 03:05:06.720
Uh, thank you, Chair. I want to start by saying, uh, that,

03:05:07.220 --> 03:05:10.750
uh, to all the delegates, thanks for coming in today. I do hear you.

03:05:11.370 --> 03:05:14.730
Um, my, my view on this has changed since I joined

03:05:15.230 --> 03:05:19.350
Regional Council in August. I spent time listening, learning, and looking at how

03:05:19.850 --> 03:05:24.250
other communities are trying to respond to homelessness and encampments.

03:05:24.410 --> 03:05:27.970
I do not believe encampments are a housing solution. They're not.

03:05:28.470 --> 03:05:32.530
But I also cannot ignore the reality that people are living outside in our community.

03:05:32.930 --> 03:05:36.010
We need a better, clearer, and more humane way to respond.

03:05:37.254 --> 03:05:41.020
We need to protect public spaces, but we also need to protect

03:05:41.181 --> 03:05:44.755
human dignity. We need to support residents and businesses,

03:05:45.252 --> 03:05:48.939
but we also need to recognize that people living outside are still members of

03:05:49.439 --> 03:05:52.706
this community. I also want to be clear that this cannot

03:05:52.786 --> 03:05:56.248
only be about how we respond when someone is already living

03:05:56.312 --> 03:05:59.630
outside. We need to keep working on the housing side of

03:06:00.130 --> 03:06:03.830
this issue as well. That is why I have brought forward and supported

03:06:04.503 --> 03:06:08.402
work around looking at Municipal Housing Corporation and other ways the region

03:06:08.979 --> 03:06:11.611
can create more sustainable housing solutions.

03:06:12.686 --> 03:06:15.974
If we only focus on what happens after people are already

03:06:16.474 --> 03:06:19.311
in encampments, we are dealing with the symptom, not the cause.

03:06:20.691 --> 03:06:24.205
This is a difficult issue, but I think we need to move beyond only

03:06:24.301 --> 03:06:27.702
reacting. We need a framework that is fair,

03:06:27.991 --> 03:06:31.660
practical, and compassionate. And we need long-term

03:06:32.160 --> 03:06:35.889
housing solutions that actually reduce the need for encampments in the first place.

03:06:36.229 --> 03:06:40.510
I also want to explain why I am supporting the appeal.

03:06:40.799 --> 03:06:44.100
For me, this is not about turning our backs on the vulnerable people.

03:06:44.600 --> 03:06:47.820
It's about a legal clarity. I believe this

03:06:48.320 --> 03:06:51.740
ruling could have very broad impacts, not only

03:06:52.240 --> 03:06:55.760
on the region of Waterloo, but the municipalities across Ontario and Canada.

03:06:56.356 --> 03:07:00.000
Municipalities need to understand that what

03:07:00.016 --> 03:07:03.115
authority they have, what responsibilities they have,

03:07:03.469 --> 03:07:06.808
and how to balance public land, public safety,

03:07:07.161 --> 03:07:11.497
infrastructure, shelter access, and the rights of people living unsheltered.

03:07:12.524 --> 03:07:15.912
At the same time, I do not believe an appeal is enough

03:07:16.412 --> 03:07:19.733
on its own. We need to do— what we need

03:07:20.071 --> 03:07:24.309
is to do public policy. We needed— we need

03:07:24.341 --> 03:07:28.339
clear rules, better supports, and a stronger framework for

03:07:28.839 --> 03:07:32.337
how we respond when people are living outside and do not have a safe,

03:07:32.691 --> 03:07:34.987
accessible, and appropriate indoor options.

03:07:36.849 --> 03:07:40.815
So I can support the appeal for legal clarity while also

03:07:40.879 --> 03:07:43.625
saying clarity at the region needs to do more.

03:07:45.198 --> 03:07:46.627
Thank you. Councillor Rodriguez.

03:07:47.710 --> 03:07:50.910
Yeah, thanks, Chair. And I want to thank the 30-plus people

03:07:51.410 --> 03:07:54.530
who came to speak with us today. I woke up this morning feeling like we're

03:07:55.030 --> 03:07:59.370
stubbing our toe on this issue time and time again, and we have a 90-page

03:07:59.870 --> 03:08:04.090
roadmap to do what we wanted to, to do last year,

03:08:04.590 --> 03:08:08.190
which is advance an important public project but also care for the

03:08:08.690 --> 03:08:11.310
people at 100 Victoria. And so we have, we have a roadmap, we have a

03:08:11.810 --> 03:08:15.570
path forward, um, it plays right into the hand of— there's

03:08:16.070 --> 03:08:18.770
so many other legal matters in front of the courts across the province right now

03:08:19.270 --> 03:08:22.830
that so many of us would stand behind, whether it's the size of councils or

03:08:23.330 --> 03:08:26.810
the ability for municipal decisions on bike lanes, all of these types of

03:08:27.310 --> 03:08:29.630
decisions. We need to let the court process play out, and we have a decision.

03:08:30.050 --> 03:08:32.630
So let's let that decision be our roadmap.

03:08:33.830 --> 03:08:37.370
And if I think to one of the delegates who said if the province wants

03:08:37.870 --> 03:08:41.400
to do something, let them. Thanks, Chair. So colleagues,

03:08:41.900 --> 03:08:45.119
um, I— it's been moved and seconded. I have no further, um,

03:08:45.619 --> 03:08:48.310
uh, councillors who want to speak, so please cast your ballot.

03:08:53.980 --> 03:08:57.640
Chair, I don't have access to eScribe, but I'm in favor.

03:08:57.800 --> 03:08:59.540
Thank you, Councillor Salonen.

03:09:09.710 --> 03:09:25.810
Colleagues, that is

03:09:26.310 --> 03:09:30.310
carried. I would turn your attention to correspondence under 6.1 to 6.3.

03:09:30.610 --> 03:09:37.610
Okay,

03:09:38.110 --> 03:09:42.689
so

03:09:43.189 --> 03:09:46.950
we're gonna take 5-minute

03:09:47.450 --> 03:09:52.550
break.

03:11:10.860 --> 03:11:14.060
I'm going to draw your attention to correspondence

03:11:14.560 --> 03:11:16.900
which we have received. Item 6.1 to 6.3.

03:11:17.700 --> 03:11:21.040
I now will consider a motion to enact the bylaw's first, second,

03:11:21.540 --> 03:11:25.180
and third readings, and this is to confirm all the actions of today's special council

03:11:25.680 --> 03:11:29.739
meeting. If I could have a mover. Moved by Councilor Foxton, seconded by

03:11:30.239 --> 03:11:33.760
Councilor Nowak. Um, please use your eScribe to

03:11:34.260 --> 03:11:37.960
vote, and we are ratifying the decisions that have been, uh,

03:11:38.460 --> 03:11:42.000
made. In favor,

03:11:42.500 --> 03:11:44.020
Chair. Thank you, Councilor Salonen.

03:12:22.593 --> 03:12:26.271
Colleagues, that is carried. I now need a mover and a seconder for adjournment.

03:12:27.251 --> 03:12:31.058
Moved by Councilor Gowing, seconded by Councilor Wolf. Um, do I have

03:12:31.122 --> 03:12:33.451
unanimous consent for this meeting to be adjourned?

03:12:34.640 --> 03:12:38.269
Yes. Thank you all very much.
